1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sigar''' is a character in ''Hversum Noreg byggdist''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Ættartolur", the genealogies appended to the ''Hversu Noregr byggdist'' in the Flatey Book, introduce Halfdan the Old as the ruler of Ringiríki. He has nine sons, including Sigar:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"''Their son was Halfdan the Old. And when he took the kingdom . . . .He married Alfny, daughter of King Eymund from Holmgard. They had nine sons. One was named Thengill, who was called Mannathengill, Raesi, Gram, Gylfi, Hilmir, 
2 CONC Jofurr, Tiggi, Skyli, and Harri. These nine, it is said, were all of equal age, and were so exceptional, that in all accounts their names are held as honorable names, and king's names. It is said that none of them had children, an
2 CONC d later they all fell in battle. But they had another nine sons. They were named Hildr, Naefill, Audi, Skelfi, Dag, Bragi, Budli, Lofdi, and '''Sigarr'''. Hildr, '''Sigarr''' and Lofdi were all warrior kings. Audi, Budli, and Naef
2 CONC ill were sea-kings, and Dagr, Skelfi, and Bragi stayed on land.''"
2 CONT
2 CONT >"'''''Sigarr''' was the father of Siggeir, who married Signy, the daughter of the king of the Volsungs. '''Sigarr''' was also the father of Sigmund, who married Hilda, daughter of Grjotgard, King of Maeri. His son was named Sigar
2 CONC r, father of Signy. He had Hagbard hanged. That is the dynasty of the Siklings.''"
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Skáldskaparmál'' (Snorri Sturluson) gives the same list of nine sons: "the ninth, Sigar [Sigarr], whence come the Siklings: that is the house of Siggeir [Siggeirr], who was son-in-law of Völsung [Vǫlsungr],—and the house of Siga
2 CONC r, who hanged Hagbard [Hagbarðr]."
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
