1 NOTE {geni:about_me} “ The "historical" arms of Thérouanne, as they were carved on the war memorial at the beginning of the 1920s, or as they appear on a stained glass window in the choir of the church (1901) draw, as is the case is fr
2 CONC equent, their origin of a legend. They are "of silver with the head of Moor tied Gules, with the chief Azure with three (or sown with ) fleurs-de-lys of gold" . They thus appear on an engraving presenting a perspective of the city
2 CONC  in 1553, the year of its destruction by Charles V. Several historians, including Malbrancq in the 17th century, recall that this coat of arms evokes the legend, quite improbable moreover, of an African prince named Morineus, who 
2 CONC founded the city before the Roman conquest. This legend is based on the similarity between the words Moor and Morins, Celtic people established until around the 4th century around Thérouanne, capital of Morinie. On the war memoria
2 CONC l and on the stained glass window, the Moor's head is shown from the front. Wanting to abandon its old weapons, we do not know for what reasons, the city of Thérouanne chose a new coat of arms in 1988: it is the one we see today o
2 CONC n the pediment of the village hall and on the documents. municipal. This "new" coat of arms "with three bolsters" is inspired by an old seal of the "Community of the inhabitants of Thérouanne", dating from 1483 and kept in the Arc
2 CONC hives du Nord. The three bolsters are themselves borrowed from the coat of arms of the old abbey of the place.The fleur de lys, which appears in the counter-seal of the seal of 1483, like those which are on the head of the old coa
2 CONC t of arms, are undoubtedly an evocation of the Lys, flowing river in Thérouanne. No other explanation should be sought from them. (Jacques Dulphy)
