2 CONT ==[http://v1.steinkjerkunnskapsportal.no/egge/index.php?art_id=320 Olve på Egge is the intruders' spokesman in the meeting with the later saint-king:]
2 CONT
2 CONT The next king who came into conflict with the intruders because they wanted to stick to the old ceremonies and the old faith was Olav Trygvason, and after him Olav Haraldsson again came up in the same situation. The two Christian 
2 CONC Olavane had a direct and resolute attitude to trust issues. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Olve på Egge is the intruders' spokesman in the meeting with the later saint-king:
2 CONT
2 CONT 107. This autumn, King Olav heard news from within Trondheim that the farmers had crowded guest stalls during winter nights, and there was a great deal of drinking. The king was told that all the commemorative bowls there were sig
2 CONC ned to the aces in the old way. It was also said that they had killed oxen and horses and smeared the blood on the stables and held bloodbaths, and it was said that there would be a year's fine. They also said that all the people 
2 CONC thought it was good to see that the gods had been angry because the hollow eyes had turned to Christianity. When the king heard of this, he sent messengers into Trondheim and voted for the farmers he mentioned. There was a man cal
2 CONC led Olve på Egg, which they called after the farm he lived on; he was a powerful and noble man, and he was the foreman of those who went to the king for the peasants. When they came to the king, he brought this complaint against t
2 CONC hem. But Olve answered for the farmers and said that they hadn't had any guest houses this autumn, as close as their feasts and drinking parties and some friends. "But that," he said, "as they have told Dykk about how the words fe
2 CONC ll on us Trønders when we were drinking, all wise people can beware of such talk, but I cannot answer for stupid and drunk people, what they say ." Olve was a eloquent and eloquent man, and he defended the farmers against these co
2 CONC mplaints. But finally the king said that the intruders should testify themselves about what faith they had. After this, the farmers were given permission to go home, and as soon as they were finished. "as they have told Dykk about
2 CONC  how the words fell on us Trønders when we were drinking, all wise people can beware of such talk, but I cannot answer for drunken and drunken people, what they say." Olve was a eloquent and eloquent man, and he defended the farme
2 CONC rs against these complaints. But finally the king said that the intruders should testify themselves about what faith they had. After this, the farmers were given permission to go home, and as soon as they were finished. "as they h
2 CONC ave told Dykk about how the words fell on us Trønders when we were drinking, all wise people can beware of such talk, but I cannot answer for drunken and drunken people, what they say." Olve was a eloquent and eloquent man, and he
2 CONC  defended the farmers against these complaints. But finally the king said that the intruders should testify themselves about what faith they had. After this, the farmers were given permission to go home, and as soon as they were f
2 CONC inished.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 108. Later on in the winter, the king was told that the intruders had a large gathering at Mæren, and that there were large spots at the midwinter hunt, and there they were looking for peace and a good winter. When the king though
2 CONC t he had been assured of this, he sent men and messengers into Trondheim and voted the peasants out to the city, and this time and again named the men whom he thought were the wisest. The farmers had conversations and consulted ab
2 CONC out this message, and all those who had traveled before in the winter were most uneasy about this journey. Finally, Olve set out on his journey, when all the farmers begged him. When he came out into the city, he immediately went 
2 CONC to the king, and they entered into conversation. Then the king complained to the farmers, because they had had a midwinter drought. Olve replied and said that the farmers were innocent in this: "We had Christmas parties," he said,
2 CONC  "and drinking parties around the villages, and the farmers don't cook so little for their Christmas dishes that there isn't much left over, and this, sir, they drank long after. On Mæren there is a large manor house and large bui
2 CONC ldings and a large village around it, and there people think it's a lot of fun to drink a lot of people together." The king answered little and was somewhat morose, and he probably knew better about this than that. they had now to
2 CONC ld. The king asked the peasants to go back: "But I will probably learn the truth," he said, "even if they want to hide it, and then they must not object. But as it has been with these, they must not do such a thing again." After t
2 CONC his the peasants went home and told of their journey, and that the king was rather angry. On Mæren there is a large manor house and large buildings and a large village around it, and there people think it's a lot of fun to drink a
2 CONC  lot of people together." The king answered little and was somewhat morose, and he probably knew better about this than that. they had now told. The king asked the peasants to go back: "But I will probably learn the truth," he sai
2 CONC d, "even if they want to hide it, and then they must not object. But as it has been with these, they must not do such a thing again." After this the peasants went home and told of their journey, and that the king was rather angry.
2 CONC  On Mæren there is a large manor house and large buildings and a large village around it, and there people think it's a lot of fun to drink a lot of people together." The king answered little and was somewhat morose, and he probab
2 CONC ly knew better about this than that. they had now told. The king asked the peasants to go back: "But I will probably learn the truth," he said, "even if they want to hide it, and then they must not object. But as it has been with 
2 CONC these, they must not do such a thing again." After this the peasants went home and told of their journey, and that the king was rather angry. "even if they want to hide it, and then they must not object. But as it has been with th
2 CONC em, they must not do such things in the past." After this the peasants went home and told of their journey, and that the king was rather angry. "even if they want to hide it, and then they must not object. But as it has been with 
2 CONC them, they must not do such things in the past." After this the peasants went home and told of their journey, and that the king was rather angry.
2 CONT
2 CONT 109. King Olav had a large guest house at Easter time, and had many townspeople and farmers in his team. After Easter, the king had the ship shoved on the water and the oars and the travel on board and put in the sails and tent an
2 CONC d rigging, and let them float the sails at the wharves. Immediately after Easter, King Olav sent a store to Verdalen.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a man called Toralde; he was the king's yearling and ran his farm at Haug. The king sent a message to him that he should come to him as quickly as he could. Toralde did not shy away from this journey and immediately set 
2 CONC out for the city with the messengers. He dealt with the king privately and asked how much truth there was in what he had been told about the customs of the intruders, whether it was the case that they engaged in exposure. "I want,
2 CONC " said the king, "that you should tell me as it is, and the truest thing you know; you are guilty of it, since you are my husband. Toralde answers: "Lord, I will tell Dykk first, that I have brought to the city both my sons and my
2 CONC  wife, and all the idleness I could bring with me. But if you want to know the truth from me, then it shall be as you wish. But if I say what is true, you will have to see for yourself how I am doing. " The king said: "Tell the tr
2 CONC uth about what I ask you. I'll see how you're doing, so you won't worry about anything." Then Toralde said: "If I'm going to say it as it is, then I have to say it's true, king, that in Trondheim most of the people are pagans in t
2 CONC heir faith , yet some of them are baptized there. They have the custom of having a feast in the autumn and a greeting in the winter, and another at the midwinter hunt, and the third in the summer, and then they greet the summer. T
2 CONC hose who are involved in this are the eyes and the spar buildings and the verdøls and the skøyen. There are 12 men who stand before the guild; now this spring it is Olve who will hold the feast, and now he is having a very busy ti
2 CONC me at Mæren, and there they have brought everything that is needed for the feast." When the king learned the truth, he let his people gather together, and let told his men to go on board. The king named men to steer the ship, and 
2 CONC chiefs for each group, and said which ship each group should go on board. They were soon reiuge, and the king entered the fjord with 5 ships and 300 men. It was a good turn, and the ship made good speed for the wind, and no one ex
2 CONC pected the king to come in so soon. The king came to Mæren at night and immediately surrounded the house. There Olve was captured, and the king quickly killed him and many other men; but the king took all the inn's food and had on
2 CONC  board his ships, and likewise all the goods, both household linen and clothing and belongings that they had brought there, and exchanged it as spoils among his men. The king haunted those men and whom he thought had been most gui
2 CONC lty in this council; some of them were caught and put in irons, and some got away, and many he took away their property. Thus the king called the peasants into action; but when he had captured many of the nobles and had them in hi
2 CONC s power, their relatives and friends agreed to promise the king that they would listen, and there was no rebellion against the king in Trondheim this time. He converted the whole people to the right faith, and appointed priests th
2 CONC ere and had churches built and via them. The king judged Olve unworthy and submitted to him all that he eight. But of the other men, whom he thought were the most guilty, he had some killed and some disgraced, some he chased out o
2 CONC f the country, and fined some. Then the king went out again to Nidaros. He converted the whole people to the right faith, and appointed priests there and had churches built and via them. The king judged Olve unworthy and submitted
2 CONC  to him all that he eight. But of the other men, whom he thought were the most guilty, he had some killed and some disgraced, some he chased out of the country, and fined some. Then the king went out again to Nidaros. He converted
2 CONC  the whole people to the right faith, and appointed priests there and had churches built and via them. The king judged Olve unworthy and submitted to him all that he eight. But of the other men, whom he thought were the most guilt
2 CONC y, he had some killed and some disgraced, some he chased out of the country, and fined some. Then the king went out again to Nidaros.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://nn.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egge_i_Steinkjer Egg in Steinkjer, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Egge in Steinkjer is an old manor house in Steinkjer municipality in Trøndelag , 2 km north of the city centre. It lies on a ridge, and that is what gave it its name.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Snorre'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Egge gard is mentioned for the first time in Soga om Håkon den gode , around the year 960 according to the usual reckoning, as the hometown of Trond Haka , the chief who was responsible for the exposure in Sparbyggjafylket , when 
2 CONC the people of Trønder made plans to have the king exposed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next time Egge gard is mentioned is in the Soga about Olav the holy winter 1020-21. At the time, Olve på Egge was the spokesman for the farmers in Inntrøndelag , whom the king blamed for continuing with the exposure in secret.
2 CONC  The king then learned that there was to be a big feast for all the intruders on Mære and that Olve was to be in charge of the feast. The king came to Mære at night and killed Olve.
2 CONT
2 CONT Egge (and the other properties, and widow Sigrid Toresdotter ) were taken over by Kalv Arnesson . Kalv became a powerful and headstrong countryman who alternated between being an ally with the king and with the lord earls, who bec
2 CONC ame the leader of the peasant army in the battle of Stiklestad and who brought Magnus Olavsson home from Gardarike in 1034. He owned Egge and lived there until he fled the country after to have fallen out of favor with Magnus for 
2 CONC a few years in the 1030s, and the king took all his possessions. Kalv got the farm and property back in 1050 after his brother Finn Arnesson had asked Harald Hardråde about that, but he all died the following summer when the king 
2 CONC sent him and a small flock ashore against a large army on Funen . Later we no longer belong to Egge and the chiefs there.
2 CONT
