2 CONT
2 CONT 46. OF OLAF THE TREE-FELLER.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Olaf, King Ingjald's son, heard of his father's end, he went
2 CONT
2 CONT with the men who chose to follow him to Nerike; for all the
2 CONT
2 CONT Swedish community rose with one accord to drive out Ingjald's
2 CONT
2 CONT family and all his friends. Now, when the Swedes got
2 CONT
2 CONT intelligence of him he could not remain there, but went on
2 CONT
2 CONT westwards, through the forest, to a river which comes from the
2 CONT
2 CONT north and falls into the Venner lake, and is called Klar river.
2 CONT
2 CONT There they sat themselves down, turned to and cleared the woods,
2 CONT
2 CONT burnt, and then settled there. Soon there were great districts;
2 CONT
2 CONT which altogether were called Vermeland; and a good living was to
2 CONT
2 CONT be made there. Now when it was told of Olaf, in Sweden, that he
2 CONT
2 CONT was clearing the forests, they laughed at his proceedings, and
2 CONT
2 CONT called him the Tree-feller. Olaf got a wife called Solva, or
2 CONT
2 CONT Solveig, a daughter of Halfdan Guldtand, westward in Soleyar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan was a son of Solve Solvarson, who was a son of Solve the
2 CONT
2 CONT Old, who first settled on these islands. Olaf Tree-feller's
2 CONT
2 CONT mother was called Gauthild, and her mother was Alov, daughter of
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf the Sharp-sighted, king in Nerike. Olaf and Solva had two
2 CONT
2 CONT sons: Ingjald and Halfdan. Halfdan was brought up in Soleyar, in
2 CONT
2 CONT the house of his mother's brother Solve, and was called Halfdan
2 CONT
2 CONT Hvitbein.
2 CONT
2 CONT 47. OLAF THE TREE-FELLER'S DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were a great many people who fled the country from Sweden,
2 CONT
2 CONT on account of King Ivar; and when they heard that King Olaf had
2 CONT
2 CONT got good lands in Vermeland, so great a number came there to him
2 CONT
2 CONT that the land could not support them. Then there came dear times
2 CONT
2 CONT and famine, which they ascribed to their king; as the Swedes used
2 CONT
2 CONT always to reckon good or bad crops for or against their kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Swedes took it amiss that Olaf was sparing in his sacrifices,
2 CONT
2 CONT and believed the dear times must proceed from this cause. The
2 CONT
2 CONT Swedes therefore gathered together troops, made an expedition
2 CONT
2 CONT against King Olaf, surrounded his house and burnt him in it,
2 CONT
2 CONT giving him to Odin as a sacrifice for good crops. This happened
2 CONT
2 CONT at the Venner lake. Thus tells Thjodolf of it: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "The temple wolf, by the lake shores,
2 CONT
2 CONT The corpse of Olaf now devours.
2 CONT
2 CONT The clearer of the forests died
2 CONT
2 CONT At Odin's shrine by the lake side.
2 CONT
2 CONT The glowing flames stripped to the skin
2 CONT
2 CONT The royal robes from the Swedes' king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus Olaf, famed in days of yore,
2 CONT
2 CONT Vanished from earth at Venner's shore."
2 CONT
2 CONT **********************
2 CONT
2 CONT Events in the life of _Oláfr Trételgju Ingjaldsson
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT ·went with the men who chose to follow him, when heard of his father's end, to Nerike; for all the Swedish community rose with one accord to drive out Ingjald's family and all his friends
2 CONT
