2 CONT General references:
2 CONT From "The Ynglinga Saga":
2 CONT
2 CONT "53. BY Gudröd jägaren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudröd, Halfdan's son, succeeded. He was called Gudröd Magnificent and also Gudröd the hunter. He was married to Alfhild, daughter of King Alfarin of Alfheim, and had with her half the district of Vingulmark. Their son Olav was af
2 CONC terwards called Geirstad - Alf. Alfheim, at that time, was the name of the country between the Glommen and Gotha rivers. But when Alfhild died, King Gudröd sent his men west to Agder to the king who ruled there, and whose name was
2 CONC  Harald Redbeard. They were to make proposals to their daughter Aasa on the king's behalf, but Harald reduced the match, and the ambassadors returned to the king and told him about the outcome of their case. Shortly after King Gud
2 CONC röd hove his ships into the water and proceeded with great force in them to Agder. He landed immediately and arrived completely unexpectedly at night to King Harald's house. When Harald was aware that an army was at hand, he went 
2 CONC out with the men he had about him, and it was a big blow, but he wanted the men so much. King Harald and his son Gyrd fell, and King Gudröd took a large booty. He carried away with him Aasa, the daughter of King Harald, and had a 
2 CONC wedding with her. They had a son in their marriage called Halfdan, and last fall that Halfdan was a year old Gudröd went to a round party. He lay with his ship in Stiflesund, where they had drunk hard, so that the king was very dr
2 CONC unk. In the evening, when dark, the king left on the ship, and when he came to the end of the aisle from the ship to the shore, a man ran towards him, stuck a spear through him and killed him. The man was immediately killed, and i
2 CONC n the morning when it was light the man discovered that Aasa's side -boy: nor did she hide that it was done by her order. ... "
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT The Oseberg ship's funeral is the most richly furnished Scandinavian tomb ever found. The clinker-built ships in oak with twelve on board planks and fifteen end up oars on each side, which indicates that she must have had a crew o
2 CONC f 35-40 men. The trunk and stern are decorated with ornate wood carvings. The ship was probably to be used as a royal yacht, for voyages in the fjords and along the coasts of Norway, by people of high rank. One of the women may ha
2 CONC ve been the legendary queen Åsa, founder of the Norwegian royal family, hence the name Oseberg: "Åsa's hill". 3
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa married Gudröd "Veidekonge" Halvdansson, son of Halvdan Milde og Mat- Ille and Liv Dagsdotter, about 810. (Gudröd "Veidekonge" Halvdansson was born around 775 in Vestfold, Norway and died around 826 in Vestfold, Norway.) The c
2 CONC ause of his death was the murder by his second wife Åsa.
2 CONT
