2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa Haraldsdotter (født ca. 800 død ca. 834) var dronning over Vestfold, Romerike og Vingulmark. Hun var datter av Harald Granraude, en småkonge fra Agder. Mor til Halvdan Svarte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det har vært fremført teorier om at det skulle være dronning Åsa som var gravlagt i Osebergskipet. Dette regnes nå som lite sannsynlig.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hun var gift med Gudrød Halvdansson Veidekonge, som hadde røvet henne. Gudrød hadde fått nei av Åsas far da han fridde, og på grunn av det drepte han både faren og broren før han røvet Åsa med seg. Det er mulig dronning Åsa sto ba
2 CONC k mordet på sin ektemann ca. år 821. Etter Gudrøds død dro hun til sin fars rike og regjerte der til hennes sønn, Halvdan, ble voksen nok til å ta over.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa Haraldsdottir of Agder (Floruit 834) was a semi-legendary Norwegian Viking age Queen regnant of the petty kingdom Agder and mother of Halfdan the Black and grandmother of Harald Fairhair, according to the sagas of the Ynglinge
2 CONC  clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa was the daughter of King Harald Granraude of Agder and a reputed beauty. King Gudrød the Hunter of Borre in Vestfold proposed marriage to her after the death of his first wife, but her father refused the marriage. Gudrød Veide
2 CONC konge then killed her father and her brother, abducted her and married her. One year later, she became the mother of Halfdan the Black. One year after this, Åsa took her revenge and had her servant kill her husband. She left the k
2 CONC ingdom of Borre to her stepson Olaf Geirstad-Alf and took her own son with her to the kingdom of Agder, her birth country, were she took power. Åsa ruled Agder for twenty years, and after this she left the throne to her son. He al
2 CONC so demanded half of his father's kingdom from his halfbrother.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are theories that queen Åsa is the woman buried with the famous Oseberg ship from 834, but this is not confirmed.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_%C3%85sa
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød the Hunter (Old Norse: Guðrǫðr veiðikonungr, Norwegian: Gudrød Veidekonge) was a semi-legendary king in south-east Norway, during the early Viking Age. He is mentioned in the skaldic poem Ynglingatal. Snorri Sturluson elabo
2 CONC rates on Gudrød's story in Heimskringla, written c. 1230; however, this is not considered to be a historical account by modern historians. The following account is taken from Heimskringla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød was the son of Halfdan the Mild of the House of Yngling and Liv Dagsdotter of Vestmar. He married Alfhild, a daughter of Alfarin the king of Alfheim (Bohuslän), which was the name of the area between Glomma and Göta älv, an
2 CONC d inherited half the province of Vingulmark. They had a son, Olaf Gudrødsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Alfhild died, Gudrød sent his warriors to Agder and its king, Harald, to propose a marriage with his daughter Åsa. However, Harald Granraude declined, so Gudrød decided to take his daughter by force.
2 CONT
2 CONT They arrived at night. When Harald realised that he was being attacked, he assembled his men and fought well, but died together with his son Gyrd. Gudrød carried away Åsa and married her. He raped her and she gave him a son named 
2 CONC Halfdan who would be called Halfdan the Black.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall, when Halfdan was a year old, Gudrød was having at a feast in Stiflesund. He was very drunk and in the evening, as he was walking on the gangway to leave the ship, an assassin thrust a spear through Gudrød, killing him
2 CONC . Gudrød's men instantly killed the assassin, who turned out to be Åsa's page-boy. Åsa admitted that the page-boy had acted on her behalf.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
