2 CONT Alt Death Year: 323, 367, 369
2 CONT
2 CONT http://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanlande Vanlande eller Vanland (fornvästnordiska Vanlandi) var kung av Svitjod av Ynglingaätten och satt i Uppsala. Han är omnämnd i Ynglingatal och Ynglingasagan i Heimskringla. Han ska ha varit son 
2 CONC till Svegder som han efterträdde som kung.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vanlande beskrivs av Snorre som en stor krigare som ofta reste ut i världen. En vinter bodde han hos Snjå den gamle (Snær gamli) i Lappland (Finnland) och fick hans dotter Driva till maka. Våren efter for han hem, men lovade att k
2 CONC omma tillbaka om tre år. När Vanlande glömde bort detta och inte kom tillbaka på tio år skickade hon deras son Visbur till honom med sejdkvinnan Huld. Hon skulle skicka kungen tillbaka till Driva eller om detta misslyckades skulle
2 CONC  hon döda honom. När hon sejdade fick Vanlande lust att fara till Lappland men hindrades av sina män. Huld skickade då en mara som red honom till döds. Han begravdes sedan på Skutåns strand och Visbur efterträdde honom som kung.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vanlande är troligen bara en mytologisk gestalt och kan då ha tillhört gudastammen vanerna i den nordiska mytologin.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanlandi Vanlandi or Vanlande was a Swedish king at Uppsala of the House of Yngling in Norse mythology. He was the son of Sveigðir whom he succeeded as king. He married a girl from Finnland (territorie
2 CONC s inhabited by Finno-Ugric peoples), but forgot about her. In revenge, the girl arranged so that Vanlandi was hag ridden to death. He was succeeded by his son Visbur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson wrote of Vanlandi in his Ynglinga saga (1225) (note that the translator has rendered Finnland as Finland):
2 CONT
2 CONT Vanlandi hét son Svegðis, er ríki tók eptir hann ok réð fyrir Uppsala auð; hann var hermaðr mikill, ok hann fór víða um lönd. Hann þá vetrvist á Finnlandi með Snjá hinum gamla, ok fékk þar dóttr hans Drífu. En at vári fór hann á b
2 CONC rott, en Drífa var eptir, ok hét hann at koma aptr á þriggja vetra fresti; en hann kom eigi á 10 vetrum. Þá sendi Drífa eptir Huld seiðkonu, en sendi Vísbur, son þeirra Vanlanda, til Svíþjóðar. Drífa keypti at Huld seiðkonu, at ho
2 CONC n skyldi síða Vanlanda til Finnlands, eða deyða hann at öðrum kosti. En er seiðr var framiðr, þá var Vanlandi at Uppsölum; þá gerði hann fúsan at fara til Finnlands, en vinir hans ok ráðamenn bönnuðu honum, ok sögðu at vera mundi 
2 CONC fjölkyngi Finna í farfýsi hans. Þá gerðist honum svefnhöfugt, ok lagðist hann till svefns. En er hann hafði lítt sofnat, kallaði hann ok sagði, at mara trað hann. Menn hans fóru til ok vildu hjálpa honum; en er þeir tóku uppi til 
2 CONC höfuðsins, þá trað hon fótleggina, svá at nær brotnuðu; þá tóku þeir til fótanna, þá kafði hon höfuðit, svá at þar dó hann. Svíar tóku lík hans, ok var hann brendr við á þá er Skúta heitir. Þar váru settir bautasteinar hans.[1] 		
2 CONC  Vanlande, Swegde's son, succeeded his father, and ruled over the Upsal domain. He was a great warrior, and went far around in different lands. Once he took up his winter abode in Finland with Snae the Old, and got his daughter Dr
2 CONC iva in marriage; but in spring he set out leaving Driva behind, and although he had promised to return within three years he did not come back for ten. Then Driva sent a message to the witch Huld; and sent Visbur, her son by Vanla
2 CONC nde, to Sweden. Driva bribed the witch- wife Huld, either that she should bewitch Vanlande to return to Finland, or kill him. When this witch-work was going on Vanlande was at Upsal, and a great desire came over him to go to Finla
2 CONC nd; but his friends and counsellors advised him against it, and said the witchcraft of the Finn people showed itself in this desire of his to go there. He then became very drowsy, and laid himself down to sleep; but when he had sl
2 CONC ept but a little while he cried out, saying that the Mara was treading upon him. His men hastened to him to help him; but when they took hold of his head she trod on his legs, and when they laid hold of his legs she pressed upon h
2 CONC is head; and it was his death. The Swedes took his body and burnt it at a river called Skytaa, where a standing stone was raised over him.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri also quoted some lines from Ynglingatal composed in the 9th century:
2 CONT
2 CONT     En á vit
2 CONT     Vilja bróður
2 CONT     vitta véttr
2 CONT     Vanlanda kom,
2 CONT     þá er trollkund
2 CONT     of troða skyldi
2 CONT     liðs grímhildr
2 CONT     ljóna bága;
2 CONT     ok sá brann á beði Skútu
2 CONT     menglötuðr,
2 CONT     er mara kvalði.[4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT     And Vanlande, in a fatal hour,
2 CONT     Was dragg'd by Grimhild's daughter's power,
2 CONT     The witch-wife's, to the dwelling-place
2 CONT     Where men meet Odin face to face.
2 CONT     Trampled to death, to Skytaa's shore
2 CONT     The corpse his faithful followers bore;
2 CONT     And there they burnt, with heavy hearts,
2 CONT     The good chief killed by witchcraft's arts.[6][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT Iste [Swegthir] genuit Wanlanda, qui in somno a dæmone suffocatus interiit, quod genus dæmoniorum norwegico sermone mara vocatur. Hic genuit Wisbur [...][8] 	
2 CONT He [Sveigde] sired Vanlande, who died in his sleep, suffocated by a goblin, one of the demonic species known in Norwegian as 'mare'. He was the father of Visbur, [...][9] 	
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and also gives Vanlandi as the successor of Svegðir and the predecessor of Visbur: v Svegðir. vi Vanlandi. vii Visburr. viii Dómaldr[10].
2 CONT
2 CONT Geography
2 CONT
2 CONT Geographical note: According to the article Skuttunge in Nationalencyklopedin, the creek skutá passed its name onto the village of Skuttunge and the parish of Skuttunge. The area does not only contain raised stones, but also 45 gr
2 CONC ave fields (most from the Iron Age), including a dolmen. The creek is today named after the village.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Konge i Uppsala (Sverige)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vanlandi or Vanlande was a Swedish king at Uppsala of the House of Yngling in Norse mythology. He was the son of Sveigðir whom he succeeded as king. He married a girl from Finnland (territories inhabited by Finno-Ugric peoples), b
2 CONC ut forgot about her. In revenge, the girl arranged so that Vanlandi was hag ridden to death. He was succeeded by his son Visbur.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson wrote of Vanlandi in his Ynglinga saga (1225) (note that the translator has rendered Finnland as Finland): Vanlande, Swegde's son, succeeded his father, and ruled over the Upsal domain. He was a great warrior, and
2 CONC  went far around in different lands. Once he took up his winter abode in Finland with Snae the Old, and got his daughter Driva in marriage; but in spring he set out leaving Driva behind, and although he had promised to return with
2 CONC in three years he did not come back for ten. Then Driva sent a message to the witch Huld; and sent Visbur, her son by Vanlande, to Sweden. Driva bribed the witch- wife Huld, either that she should bewitch Vanlande to return to Fin
2 CONC land, or kill him. When this witch-work was going on Vanlande was at Upsal, and a great desire came over him to go to Finland; but his friends and counsellors advised him against it, and said the witchcraft of the Finn people show
2 CONC ed itself in this desire of his to go there. He then became very drowsy, and laid himself down to sleep; but when he had slept but a little while he cried out, saying that the Mara was treading upon him. His men hastened to him to
2 CONC  help him; but when they took hold of his head she trod on his legs, and when they laid hold of his legs she pressed upon his head; and it was his death. The Swedes took his body and burnt it at a river called Skytaa, where a stan
2 CONC ding stone was raised over him.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanlandi
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vanlande var son till Sveigder och tog över makten efter honom och styrde över Uppsala öd. Han var en krigare och han for vida omkring i länderna. Han tog vinterviste i Finland hos Snjå den gamle och fick hans dotter Driva till hu
2 CONC stru. När det blev vår for han bort och lämnade Driva kvar men lovade henne att komma åter inom tre vintrar. När han inte återkommit inom 10 vintrar skickade Driva efter sejdkvinnan Huld och kommer överens med henne att hon skulle
2 CONC  sejda så att Vanlade skulle komma till Finland eller som andra utväg skulle han dödas. Sonen Visbur sändes till Svitjod. När sejden gjordes var Vanlande i Uppsala. Då fick han lust att fara till Finland, men hans rådgivare förbjö
2 CONC d honom för de trodde att det var Finnarnas trolldom som gav honom lusten. Under natten grep maran honom och klämde hans huvud så att dog. Sverna tog hans lik och han blev bränd vid den å som heter Skutå och reste hans bautastenar
2 CONC . 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vanland, Svegders Søn, givtede sig paa et af sine Tog med den finske Kong Snø den Gamles Datter Drifva, med Løfte om to Aar efter at hente hende til Upsala. Da Vanland opsatte dette i 10 Aar, og Drifva havde raadspurgt Spaaqvinden
2 CONC  Huld, afsendte hun sin Søn Wisbur for at paakræve Løftets Opfyldelse. Vanland, i Begyndelsen villig dertil, afslog dog Reisen paa sine  Hofmænds Raad, hvorpaa en heftig Sygdom angreb ham saa han deraf omkom.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vanlande, Swegde's son, succeeded his father, and ruled over the Upsal domain. He was a great warrior, and went far around in different lands. Once he took up his winter abode in Finland with Snae the Old, and got his daughter Dri
2 CONC va in marriage; but in spring he set out leaving Driva behind, and although he had promised to return within three years he did not come back for ten. Then Driva sent a message to the witch Huld; and sent Visbur, her son by Vanlan
2 CONC de, to Sweden. Driva bribed the witch- wife Huld, either that she should bewitch Vanlande to return to Finland, or kill him. When this witch-work was going on Vanlande was at Upsal (a parish and a village outside Uppsala in Sweden
2 CONC ), and a great desire came over him to go to Finland; but his friends and counsellors advised him against it, and said the witchcraft of the Finn people showed itself in this desire of his to go there. He then became very drowsy, 
2 CONC and laid himself down to sleep; but when he had slept but a little while he cried out, saying that the Mara (a supernatural creature which is believed to torment people in their sleep by sitting on their chest and "riding" them, t
2 CONC hus causing nightmares) was treading upon him. His men hastened to him to help him; but when they took hold of his head she trod on his legs, and when they laid hold of his legs she pressed upon his head; and it was his death. The
2 CONC  Swedes took his body and burnt it at a river called Skytaa, where a standing stone was raised over him.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vanlandi
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Vanlandi or Vanlande (Old Norse "Man from the Land of the Vanir"[1]) was a Swedish king at Uppsala of the House of Yngling in Norse mythology. He was the son of Sveigðir whom he succeeded as king. He married a girl from Finnland 
2 CONC (territories inhabited by Finno-Ugric peoples), but forgot about her. In revenge, the girl arranged so that Vanlandi was hag ridden to death. He was succeeded by his son Visbur.'
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vanlandi or Vanlande was a Swedish king at Uppsala of the House of Yngling in Norse mythology. He was the son of Sveigðir whom he succeeded as king. He married a girl from Finnland (territories inhabited by Finno-Ugric peoples), b
2 CONC ut forgot about her. In revenge, the girl arranged so that Vanlandi was hag ridden to death. He was succeeded by his son Visbur. Vanlande, Swegde's son, succeeded his father, and ruled over the Upsal domain. He was a great warrior
2 CONC , and went far around in different lands. Once he took up his winter abode in Finland with Snae the Old, and got his daughter Driva in marriage; but in spring he set out leaving Driva behind, and although he had promised to return
2 CONC  within three years he did not come back for ten. Then Driva sent a message to the witch Huld; and sent Visbur, her son by Vanlande, to Sweden. Driva bribed the witch- wife Huld, either that she should bewitch Vanlande to return t
2 CONC o Finland, or kill him. When this witch-work was going on Vanlande was at Upsal, and a great desire came over him to go to Finland; but his friends and counsellors advised him against it, and said the witchcraft of the Finn people
2 CONC  showed itself in this desire of his to go there. He then became very drowsy, and laid himself down to sleep; but when he had slept but a little while he cried out, saying that the Mara was treading upon him. His men hastened to h
2 CONC im to help him; but when they took hold of his head she trod on his legs, and when they laid hold of his legs she pressed upon his head; and it was his death. The Swedes took his body and burnt it at a river called Skytaa, where a
2 CONC  standing stone was raised over him
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Killed by a nightmare brought on by sorcer wife, Driva.
2 CONT
2 CONT A great warrior. He is said once to have taken up his winter abode in Finland, which together with several archaeological finds, point to an early intercourse between Sweden and Finland
2 CONT
2 CONT Said to have been trampled to death by a nightmare. [A History of the Vikings, p. 37]
2 CONT
2 CONT A great warrior, he is said once to have taken up his winter abode in Finland. 
2 CONT
