2 CONT
2 CONT Konge først på 800-tallet
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød Veidekonge
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød Veidekonge (dvs. jegeren, av gn. veidr, jakt) ifølge Snorre konge over Vestfold og Romerike. Gudrød drepte Agderkongen, hærtok og ektet hans datter Åsa, som fødte han sønnen:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Halvdan Svarte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød ble drept av Åsas trell. 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Gyldendals Store Konversasjonsleksikon (1965) D-G, sid. 3271.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød Halvdansson Veidekonge (født ca. 780 død ca. 821) var konge over Vestfold, Romerike og halve Vingulmark (omtrent dagens Østfold). Gudrød var Halvdan Øysteinsson den gavmilde og Liv Dagsdotters sønn, og far til Halvdan Svart
2 CONC e og Olav Geirstadalv Gudrødsson. Frem til ca. 815 var han gift med Alvhild Alfarinsdatter, og deretter med Åsa Haraldsdatter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød fikk halve Vingulmark som medgifte da han giftet seg med Alvhild, datter av kong Alvarin Alvheim fra Alvheim (Bohuslän).
2 CONT
2 CONT Etter Alfhilds tidlige død, la Gudrød ut på frierferd til Agder, etter datteren til kong Harald Granraude, Åsa. Harald avviste ham og Gudrød svarte med å ta livet av ham og hans sønn Gyrd, for så å røve med seg Åsa og ta henne til
2 CONC  ektemake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød ble myrdet i 821, på ordre fra Åsa, for mordet på hennes far og bror, og for å ha voldtatt henne.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Forgjenger:
2 CONT
2 CONT Halvdan Øysteinsson den gavmilde  Konge av Vestfold (ca. 800–821) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Etterfølger:
2 CONT
2 CONT Olav Geirstadalv Gudrødsson 
2 CONT
2 CONT Forgjenger:
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald Granraude  Konge av Agder (ca. 815–ca. 821) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Etterfølger:
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa Haraldsdotter  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Han var Veidekonge. (Den stormodige) 2. gang gift med Åsa Haraldsdtr.
2 CONT
2 CONT 53. OF GUDROD THE HUNTER.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrod, Halfdan's son, succeeded. He was called Gudrod the Magnificent, and also Gudrod the Hunter. He was married to Alfhild, a daughter of King Alfarin of Alfheim, and got with her half the district of Vingulmark. Their son Olaf
2 CONC  was afterwards called Geirstad-Alf. Alfheim, at that time, was the name of the land between the Glommen and Gotha rivers. Now when Alfhild died, King Gudrod sent his men west to Agder to the king who ruled there, and who was call
2 CONC ed Harald Redbeard. They were to make proposals to his daughter Aasa upon the king's account; but Harald declined the match, and the ambassadors returned to the king, and told him the result of their errand. Soon after King
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrod hove down his ships into the water, and proceeded with a great force in them to Agder. He immediately landed, and came altogether unexpectedly at night to King Harald's house. When Harald was aware that an army was at hand,
2 CONC  he went out with the
2 CONT
2 CONT men he had about him, and there was a great battle, although he wanted men so much. King Harald and his son Gyrd fell, and King Gudrod took a great booty. He carried away with him Aasa, King Harald's daughter, and had a wedding wi
2 CONC th her. They had a son by their marriage called Halfdan; and the autumn that Halfdan was a year old Gudrod went upon a round of feasts. He lay with his ship in Stiflesund, where they had been drinking hard, so that the king was ve
2 CONC ry tipsy. In the evening, about dark, the king left the ship; and when he had got to the end of the gangway from the ship to the shore, a man ran against him, thrust a spear through him, and killed him. The man was instantly put t
2 CONC o death, and in the morning when it was light the man was discovered to be Aasa's page-boy: nor did she conceal that it was done by her orders. Thus tells Thjodolf of it: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Gudrod is gone to his long rest,
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite of all his haughty pride --
2 CONT
2 CONT A traitor's spear has pierced his side:
2 CONT
2 CONT For Aasa cherished in her breast
2 CONT
2 CONT Revenge; and as, by wine opprest,
2 CONT
2 CONT The hero staggered from his ship,
2 CONT
2 CONT The cruel queen her thrall let slip
2 CONT
2 CONT To do the deed of which I sing:
2 CONT
2 CONT And now the far-descended king,
2 CONT
2 CONT At Stiflesund, in the old bed
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the old Gudrod race, lies dead."
2 CONT
2 CONT 54. OF KING OLAF'S DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olaf came to the kingdom after his father. He was a great warrior, and an able man; and was besides remarkably handsome, very strong and large of growth. He had Westfold; for King Alfgeir took all Vingulmark to himself, and placed
2 CONC  his son Gandalf over it. Both father and son made war on Raumarike, and subdued the greater part of that land and district. Hogne was the name of a son of the Upland king, Eystein the Great, who subdued for himself the whole of H
2 CONC edemark, Toten, and Hadeland. Then Vermeland fell off from Gudrod's sons, and turned itself, with its payment of scatt, to the Swedish king. Olaf was about twenty years old when Gudrod died; and as his brother Halfdan now had the 
2 CONC kingdom with him, they divided it between them; so that Olaf got the eastern and Halfdan the southern part. King Olaf had his main residence at Geirstad. There he died of a disease in his foot, and was laid under a mound at Geirst
2 CONC ad. So sings Thjodolf: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Long while this branch of Odin's stem
2 CONT
2 CONT Was the stout prop of Norway's realm;
2 CONT
2 CONT Long while King Olaf with just pride
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruled over Westfold far and wide.
2 CONT
2 CONT At length by cruel gout oppressed,
2 CONT
2 CONT The good King Olaf sank to rest:
2 CONT
2 CONT His body now lies under ground,
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried at Geirstad, in the mound."
2 CONT
