3 TITL Family of Arnold of Ghent
3 NOTE https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lambert_of_Ardres
3 NOTE Lambert of Ardres (active 1194–1203) was a chronicler in the twelfth-century Kingdom of France, from on the frontiers of the County of Flanders.[1]
4 CONT
4 CONT By 1194, Lambert was the parish priest of Ardres. He was related to the Counts of Guînes, for whom he wrote a Historia comitum Ghisnensium, begun around 1196 and left unfinished in 1203.[1] It is a mixture of history and folklore.
4 CONC  It also contains a contemporary description of a donjon. A chapter of Georges Duby's The Knight, The Lady, and the Priest is dedicated to Lambert's Historia.[1]
4 CONT
4 CONT Lambert's Historia has been published in a number of editions:
4 CONT
4 CONT Godfrey de Ménilglaise (ed.), Chronique de Guines et d'Ardres par Lambert, curé d'Ardres (Paris, 1855).[2]
4 CONT Johannes Heller (ed.), "Lamberti ardensis historia comitum Ghisnensium", in Monumenta Germaniae Historica, Scriptores, vol. 24 (1879), 550-642.[3]
4 CONT Leah Shopkow (trans.), The History of the Counts of Guines and Lords of Ardres (University of Pennsylvania Press, 2000)
4 CONT References[edit]
4 CONT ^ Jump up to: a b c Cyriel Moeyaert, "Aarde (Ardres), Lambert van (Lambertus Ardensis)", Nationaal Biografisch Woordenboek, vol. 16 (2002), 1-3.
4 CONT ^ "Chronique de Guines et d'Ardre par Lambert, curé d'Ardre". Gallica. Bibliothèque nationale de France.
4 CONT ^ "Lamberti ardensis historia comitum Ghisnensium". dmgh.de. Monumenta Germaniae Historica.
