2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar Halvdansson 'Vidfamne' Ívarr inn víðfaðmi (Icelandic)
2 CONT
2 CONT == Basics ==
2 CONT * Father: Halfdan "Snälle" "The Valiant" Haraldsson (Sogubrot, Heimskringla, Hervarar Saga)
2 CONT * Mother: Moalda "Digri" (source unclear)
2 CONT * Wife: Gyrithe Alfsdatter (source unclear)
2 CONT ** Daughter: Audur the Deep-Minded
2 CONT
2 CONT == Synopsis ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar Vidfamne or Ívarr inn víðfaðmi (Ivar the Wide-Fathoming) was a Danish and Swedish king hailing from Scania. He may have died c. 700.[1]  According to the Heimskringla and the Hervarar saga, Ivar was also the king of Norway, D
2 CONC enmark, Saxony  and parts of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar in the Sagas
2 CONT
2 CONT He began as king of Scania and conquered Sweden by defeating Ingjald Illråde. He is then said to have conquered all of Scandinavia and parts of England. Because of his harsh rule, many Swedes fled west and populated Värmland under
2 CONC  its king Olof Trätälja. His last campaign was in North Eastern Europe where he died, defeated by Odin in disguise. (Another source claims he took his life by drowning himself in the Gulf of Finland.)
2 CONT
2 CONT According to both Ynglinga saga and Sögubrot, his homeland was Scania, but according to the Ynglinga saga, he had to flee Scania when his uncle Guðröðr of Scania had slain his father Halfdan the Valiant. The Ynglinga saga, Histori
2 CONC a Norvegiæ, Hervarar saga and Upplendinga Konungum tell that Ivar conquered Sweden after Ingjald's suicide, and later returned to take Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Hversu Noregr byggdist and Njal's Saga, he was the son of Halfdan the Valiant (also given as his father in the Ynglinga saga and the Hervarar saga), son of Harald the Old, son of Valdar, son of Roar (Hroðgar) of the h
2 CONC ouse of Skjöldung (Scylding). According to Hversu, Njal's saga, the Lay of Hyndla and Sögubrot, Ivar had a daughter named Auðr the Deep-Minded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sögubrot relates that when Ivar was the king of Sweden, he gave his daughter Auðr the Deep-Minded to king Hrœrekr slöngvanbaugi of Zealand, in spite of the fact that she wanted to marry Hrœrek's brother Helgi the Sharp. Hrœrekr an
2 CONC d Auðr had the son Harald Wartooth. Ivar made Hrœrekr kill his brother Helgi, and after this, he attacked and killed Hrœrekr. However, Auðr arrived with the Zealand army and chased her father Ivar back to Sweden. The following yea
2 CONC r, Auðr went to Gardariki with her son Harald and many powerful men and married its king Raðbarðr. This was the opportunity for Ivar to conquer Zealand.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hervarar saga does not mention any daughter named Auðr. Instead it mentions an Alfhild. Ivar gave her to Valdar whom Ivar made subking of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, when Ivar learnt that Auðr had married without his permission, he marshalled a great leidang from Denmark and Sweden and went to Gardariki. He was very old at the time. However, when they had arrived at the borders of Rað
2 CONC barð's kingdom, Karelia (Karjálabotnar), he threw himself overboard. Harald then returned to Scania to become its ruler. In the Lay of Hyndla, Ivar, Auðr, Hrœrekr and Harald appear. Raðbarðr also appears, but there is no informati
2 CONC on about his relationship with them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Lagerquist, Lars O. (1997). Sveriges Regenter, från forntid till nutid. Stockholm: Norstedts. pp. 22. ISBN 91-1-963882-5.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar Vidfamne var en nordisk sagokung  vars existens inte är historiskt belagd, men vilken förekommer i ett flertal isländska sagor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vidfamne skall från början ha varit kung av Skåne. Enligt Snorre Sturlasson blev han sedermera även kung av Svitjod, samt av Norge, Danmark, Finland, Saxland, Kurland, Estland, Gårdarike och norra England, det vill säga Northumbri
2 CONC a. Han skall ha erövrat sveaväldet efter innebränningen av Ingjald Illråde, och sedan ha underlagt sig alla kustländerna kring Östersjön och Nordsjön. Vidfamne omnämns dock inte i Saxo Grammaticii Gesta Danorum, där Sigurd Ring, u
2 CONC tan mellanleden Vidfamne och Harald Hildetand, efterträder Ingjald Illråde som kung.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vidfamne var enligt berättelserna son till Halvdan Snälle av Skjöldungaätten, och enligt Hervararsagan var han, på sin mors sida, ättling till en östgotisk kungasläkt. Han var gift med en dotter till Heidrek av Småland (son till A
2 CONC gantyr, kung i Småland). Han var far till Aud Djupaudga (gift först med Hrörek av Lejre och sedan med Radbart av Gårdarike). I Danmark satte han enligt den sägenhistoriska Sögubrot sin dotters make Hrörek till underkonung men lät 
2 CONC senare dräpa honom. Därefter gjorde han Valdar till kung.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar Vidfamne skall ha omkommit (kastades i vattnet och drunknade) på en härfärd till Gårdarike då han blev ordentligt uppläxad för sin grymhet, svekfullhet och makthunger, av sin egen fosterfar Hord. Han kastade sig i havet efter
2 CONC  sin fosterfar, men kom aldrig mer upp. Äldre forskning, bland annat av Birger Nerman, påstod att Ivar skulle vara höglagd på Valsgärde, i den näst äldsta gravhögen som daterats till slutet av 600-talet, utanför Uppsala.[1][2] Ida
2 CONC g har man gått ifrån dessa antaganden, och bland annat Riksantikvarieämbetet fastslår att vi inte vet vilka som blivit begravda där.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Frans G. Bengtsson gjorde sin romanhjälte Röde Orm till ättling av Ivar Vidfamne.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------
