2 CONT Bjørn Farmann ("Bjørn the Tradesman", also called Bjørn Haraldsson, Farmand and Kaupman, c. ? – c. 927) was one of the sons of Harald Fairhair, the first king of Norway, and was the king of Vestfold. He was the great-grandfather o
2 CONC f Olaf II of Norway, through his son Gudrød Bjørnsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father, Harald Fairhair, died, his kingdom was divided up between his sons. Bjørn became the king of Vestfold, a Norwegian county west of the Oslofjord, and is considered as the founder of Tønsberg, Vestfold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn was killed by his brother Eirik Bloodaxe in a feud, around 927 CE. He is said to be buried in the big Farmann burial mound (Farmannshaugen), outside Tønsberg, in Sem, close to the manor of the Jarlsberg family.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn Farmann
2 CONT
2 CONT Ifølge Snorres kongesagaer skulle Harald Hårfagres sønn Bjørn Farmann ha blitt gravlagt i Farmannshaugen like ved Sem kongsgård (i dag Jarlsberg hovedgård) utenfor Tønsberg. Arkeologiske undersøkelser i 1917 og 1918 viste imidlert
2 CONC id at haugen ikke inneholdt noen grav.Bjørn Farmann (Haraldsson) (død 927) var en småkonge i Tunsberg (Tønsberg) i Vestfold, sønn av Harald Hårfagre (Halvdansson) og bror av Eirik Blodøks (Haraldsson). Eirik tok livet av broren Bj
2 CONC ørn Farmann. Dette står beskrevet i Snorres kongesagaer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn hadde «hovedbølet» (hovedsete) sitt på kongsgården Sem utenfor Tønsberg i Vestfold. I sagaen om Harald Hårfagre i Heimskringla forteller Snorre Sturlason at kong Bjørn hadde kjøpskip i fart til andre land. Bjørn skal ha vært
2 CONC  både en klok og omtykt mann, og var en god høvding. Han hadde sønnen Gudrød, som ble far til Harald Grenske, som siden ble far til Olav den Hellige.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn Farmann skal ha blitt drept av broren Eirik Blodøks på Sem kongsgård og gravlagt i Farmannshaugen der. Ved utgravninger i 1917 og 1918 viste det seg imidlertid at haugen ikke inneholdt noen grav, men antakelig var en rituell
2 CONC  haug fra jernalderen.
2 CONT
2 CONT [rediger] Ordet «farmann»
2 CONT
2 CONT Forbedringspotensial: Denne artikkelen har forbedringspotensial. Ordboksstoff og generelt stoff som ikke angår Bjørn bør skilles ut i egen artikkel
2 CONT
2 CONT Farmann var betegnelsen for «handelsmann» eller «reisende/sjøfarende handelsmann» i norrøn tid. På islandsk er farman et levende ord og i Sverige finnes ennå ordet farmansbönder i bruk om bønder som også er handelsmenn. Stammen av
2 CONC  ordet er "å fare". Den tilsvarende betegnelsen på engelsk er "to fare", å reise eller ferdes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Før den norske språkreformen i 1917 ble «farmann» stavet som «farmand».
2 CONT
2 CONT I det moderne Norge er begrepet «farmand» mest kjent ved at det har gitt navn til forretningsbladet Farmand som ble gitt ut mellom 1891 og 1989. Forretningsbladet ble relansert med den nye stavemåten Farmann i 2003. Bjørn Farmann 
2 CONC har ellers gitt navn til Vestfolds største kjøpesenter, Farmandstredet i Tønsberg, og Farmannstorget samme sted, der Bjørn Farmann-monumentet av Aase Texmon Rygh ble reist 1971.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hentet fra «http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bj%C3%B8rn_Farmann»
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn Farmann ("Bjørn the Tradesman", also called Bjørn Haraldsson, Farmand and Kaupman, c. ? – c. 927) was one of the sons of Harald Fairhair, the first king of Norway, and was the king of Vestfold. He was the great-grandfather o
2 CONC f Olaf II of Norway, through his son Gudrød Bjørnsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father, Harald Fairhair, died, his kingdom was divided up between his sons. Bjørn became the king of Vestfold, a Norwegian county west of the Oslofjord, and is considered as the founder of Tønsberg, Vestfold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn was killed by his brother Eirik Bloodaxe in a feud, around 927 CE. He is said to be buried in the big Farmann burial mound (Farmannshaugen), outside Tønsberg, in Sem, close to the manor of the Jarlsberg family.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn Farmann ("Bjørn the Tradesman", also called Bjørn Haraldsson, Farmand and Kaupman, c. ? – c. 927) was the king of Vestfold. Bjørn was one of the sons of Harald Fairhair, the first king of Norway. Bjørn Farmann was also the g
2 CONC reat-grandfather of Olaf II of Norway, through his son Gudrød Bjørnsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn Farmann was one of the sons born of Harald Fairhair with Svanhild, daughter of Eystein Earl. When Harald Fairhair died, his kingdom was divided up between his sons. Bjørn Farmann became the king of Vestfold, the county west 
2 CONC of the Oslofjord, and is considered as the founder of Tønsberg. Erik Bloodaxe (Old Norse: Eiríkr blóðøx, Norwegian: Eirik Blodøks) was the eldest son of Harald Fairhair and became the second king of Norway (930-934). Once the powe
2 CONC r was in his hands, Erik Bloodaxe began to quarrel with his other brothers and had four of them killed, including Bjørn Farmann. Bjørn was killed by Eirik Bloodaxe in a feud around 927 CE. He is said to be buried in Farmannshaugen
2 CONC  (from the Old Norse word haugr meaning burial mound), outside Tønsberg in Sem, close to the manor of the Jarlsberg family. Later in battle at Tønsberg, Erik Bloodaxe killed Olaf Haraldsson Geirstadalf, king of Vingulmark and late
2 CONC r also of Vestfold together with Sigrød Haraldsson, king of Trondheim .
2 CONT
2 CONT Egil Skallagrimsson
2 CONT
2 CONT Egil Skallagrimsson tells this of Bjørn: Bjørn var farmaður mikill, var stundum í víking, en stundum í kaupferðum; translated into English: Björn was a great traveller; sometimes as viking, sometimes as tradesman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson tells this of Bjørn, in an extract from Heimskringla, Harald Harfager's Saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT “ King Harald and Svanhild's son Bjorn, who went by the name Bjorn Farmann, ruled over Vestfold at that time, and generally lived at Tunsberg, and went but little on war expeditions. Tunsberg at that time was much frequented by me
2 CONC rchant vessels, both from Viken and the north country, and also from the south, from Denmark, and Saxland. King Bjorn had also merchant ships on voyages to other lands, by which he procured for himself costly articles, and such th
2 CONC ings as he thought needful; and therefore his brothers called him Farman (the Seaman), and Kaupman (the Chapman). Bjorn was a man of sense and understanding, and promised to become a good ruler. He made a good and suitable marriag
2 CONC e, and had a son by his wife, who was named Gudrød Bjørnsson. Eirik Blood-axe came from his Baltic cruise with ships of war, and a great force, and required his brother Bjorn to deliver to him King Harald's share of the scat and i
2 CONC ncomes of Vestfold. But it had always been the custom before, that Bjorn himself either delivered the money into the king's hands, or sent men of his own with it; and therefore he would continue with the old custom, and would not 
2 CONC deliver the money. Eirik again wanted provisions, tents, and liquor. The brothers quarrelled about this; but Eirik got nothing and left the town. Bjorn went also out of the town towards evening up to Saeheim. In the night Eirik ca
2 CONC me back after Bjorn, and came to Saeheim just as Bjorn and his men were seated at table drinking. Eirik surrounded the house in which they were; but Bjorn with his men went out and fought. Bjorn, and many men with him, fell. Eirik
2 CONC , on the other hand, got a great booty, and proceeded northwards. But this work was taken very ill by the people of Viken, and Eirik was much disliked for it; and the report went that King Olaf would avenge his brother Bjorn, when
2 CONC ever opportunity offered. King Bjorn lies in the mound of Farmanshaug at Saeheim. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn Farmann ("Bjørn the Tradesman", also called Bjørn Haraldsson, Farmand and Kaupman, c. ? – c. 927) was one of the sons of Harald Fairhair, the first king of Norway, and was the king of Vestfold. He was the great-grandfather o
2 CONC f Olaf II of Norway, through his son Gudrød Bjørnsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father, Harald Fairhair, died, his kingdom was divided up between his sons. Bjørn became the king of Vestfold, a Norwegian county west of the Oslofjord, and is considered as the founder of Tønsberg, Vestfold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bjørn was killed by his brother Eirik Bloodaxe in a feud, around 927 CE. He is said to be buried in the big Farmann burial mound (Farmannshaugen), outside Tønsberg, in Sem, close to the manor of the Jarlsberg family.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bjørn Farmann, född cirka 895, död 927, kung i Vestfold, var son till Harald Hårfager och Svanhild Øysteinsdotter. Far till Gudrød Bjørnsson. Dödad av brodern Erik Blodyx. Tillnamnet farman var en medeltida term som betyder "köpma
2 CONC n". I Egil Skallagrimssons saga står om Bjørn: "Bjørn var farmaður mikill, var stundum í víking, en stundum í kaupferðum": "Björn var en stor resenär; ibland som viking, ibland som handelsman". Björn Farman är ett bra exempel på e
2 CONC n nordman som kunde växla mellan att vara fredlig farman, och rövande viking.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Leo: Europäische Stammtafeln, Band II, Frank Baron Freytag von Loringhoven, 1975, Isenburg, W. K. Prinz von, Reference: Page 75.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bjørn Farmann (Haraldsson) (død om lag 927) var konge i Tønsberg og bror av kong Eirik Blodøks (Haraldsson). Han var sønn av kong Harald Hårfagre (Halvdansson) og Svanhild Øysteinsdotter. Bjørn hadde hovudbølet sitt på kongsgarden
2 CONC  Sem i Vestfold. I soga om Harald Hårfagre i Heimskringla fortel Snorre Sturlason at kong Bjørn hadde kjøpskip i fart på andre land. Bjørn var ein klok og omtenkt mann, og såg ut til å bli ein god hovding. Eirik Blodøks tok livet 
2 CONC av Bjørn Farmann. Grunnen skal ha vore at Bjørn ikkje ville gje han dei skattane kong Harald hadde rett på i Vestfold. Kong Bjørn skal vera gravlagd i Farmannshaugen på Sem. Gjennom sonen Gudrød vart Bjørn Farmann stamfar til kong
2 CONC  Olav II den heilage. Farmann var ordet for «handelsmann» eller «reisande/sjøfarande handelsmann» i norrøn tid. På Island er ordet «farman» framleis levande og i Sverige finst ordet «farmandsbønder» i bruk om bønder som også er ha
2 CONC ndelsmenn. Ordet «Farmann» i namnet Bjørn Farmann er såleis ikkje eit eigenamn, men tydde Kong Bjørn «handelsmannen» (farmann) frå Tunsberg.
2 CONT
