1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Bio Summary: Jean III de Croix'''
2 CONT Role: Knight (Miles), Lord of Mandre, Croix, and Flers.
2 CONT Active Period: Late 13th Century (fl. 1260 – 1285).
2 CONT In Warlop’s Chapter 220 and his supporting data on the nobility of the Castellany of Lille, Jean III de Croix represents the entry of the Halluin family into the "Lille High Nobility." The House of Croix was one of the four most a
2 CONC ncient families of the Lille region (Les quatre grandes familles de Lille).
2 CONT '''1. Documented Evidence (The Warlop Rigor)'''
2 CONT Warlop verifies Jean III’s status and his marriage to Margaretha van Halewijn through several key administrative records:
2 CONT • The 1272 Comtal Act: Warlop cites a document where Jean III de Croix acts as a witness for Count Guy de Dampierre. In this record, Jean is identified as holding the lordship of Mandre, which was a significant fief in the borderl
2 CONC ands.
2 CONT • The Dower Settlement: Warlop tracks the property of Margaretha van Halewijn. He notes that after the death of her first husband (Simon van Vaernewyck), her subsequent legal actions involve the de Croix family. This transition of
2 CONC  "guardianship" over her dower lands is the primary proof of their union.
2 CONT • The Seigneurie of Flers: Jean III is the first to be definitively linked in the charters to the lordship of Flers, which would remain a major seat for his descendants for centuries.
2 CONT '''2. Strategic Value to the Halluin Line'''
2 CONT For your database, Jean III represents a Southward Geopolitical Shift:
2 CONT • The Lille Border: While the Halluins were expanding east (Oudenaarde) and north (Ghent), the marriage to Jean de Croix anchored them in the South. The Croix family controlled the "gateway" between the County of Flanders and the 
2 CONC Kingdom of France.
2 CONT • Military Prestige: The de Croix were famous for their crusading history and their role as "Peer-vassals" of the Lille Castle. By marrying a daughter into this house, the Halluins gained a powerful ally in the defense of the sout
2 CONC hern border.
2 CONT '''3. Family and Succession'''
2 CONT • Spouse: Margaretha van Halewijn (her second marriage).
2 CONT • Confirmed Children (per Warlop):
2 CONT 1. Jean IV de Croix: The successor to the lordship of Croix and Flers. He carried a quartering of the Halluin arms in later heraldic rolls.
2 CONT 2. Gilles de Croix: A younger son who held the fief of Mandre and appears in the military rolls of 1302.
2 CONT 3. Agnès de Croix: Mentioned in a 1285 charter regarding a religious endowment in Lille.
2 CONT Database Entry Note
2 CONT Insert table
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Rigor" Check
2 CONT Warlop points out that because the name "Jean" was so common in the de Croix family, earlier genealogists often combined Jean III and his son Jean IV. Warlop separates them by identifying the 1272 signature (Jean III) versus the 1
2 CONC 302 signature (Jean IV), noting that the seniority of the 1272 witness list requires an older man of established rank.
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes:
2 CONT -The House of de Croix is NOT the same as The House of Croy (or de Croÿ). They are two distinct families with different origins, different heraldry, and different geopolitical trajectories.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <span style="color:red;font-weight:bold"> This Biographical summary is drafted with AI assistance, cross-referenced with House of Halluin lineage records, Ernest Warlop, Hist. E. De Vlaamse Adel Voor 1300, pertinent documents of e
2 CONC vidence, etc. (The Summary is intended for leads only, please check your sources.)</span>
2 CONT …..
2 CONT From Juric, Daniel. “L’Armorial Des Villes et Villages de France.” Armorialdefrance.fr, 2023, armorialdefrance.fr/accueil.php. Accessed 28 Nov. 2025.
2 CONT CROIX.— Ferrain; canton de Roubaix.—'''D'argent à la croix d'azur.''' Armes de la maison de Croix qui posséda la seigneurie de ce village jusqu'à la fin du XV^ siècle. Celte terre passa ensuite aux de Noyelles, qui en obtinrent l'
2 CONC érection en comté, le i3 avril 1617, puis revint à une branche de la famille de Croix, et échut, par diverses alliances, aux de Beauffort 5, aux de Bauffremez ^, et enfin aux de Frémont de Rosay.
2 CONT >CROIX.— Ferrain; canton of Roubaix.—'''Argent, a cross azure.''' Arms of the House of Croix, which possessed the lordship of this village until the end of the 15th century. This land then passed to the de Noyelles family, who obt
2 CONC ained its elevation to a county on April 13, 1617, then returned to a branch of the de Croix family, and passed, through various alliances, to the de Beauffort, the de Bauffremez, and finally to the de Frémont de Rosay family....
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000222865165869&size=medium}}
