2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In Norse mythology, Dyggvi or Dyggve (Old Norse "Useful, Effective"[1]) was a Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. Dyggvi died and became the husband of Hel, Loki's daughter. Dyggvi was succeeded by his son Dag the Wise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attestations
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson wrote of Dygvvi's father Domar in his Ynglinga saga (1225):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dyggvi hét son hans, er þar næst réð löndum, ok er frá honum ekki sagt annat, en hann varð sóttdauðr.[2] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Dygve was the name of his son, who succeeded him in ruling the land; and about him nothing is said but that he died in his bed.[3][4] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT About Dyggvi's mother Snorri had more to say:
2 CONT
2 CONT Móðir Dyggva var Drótt, dóttir Danps konungs, sonar Rígs, er fyrstr var konungr kallaðr á danska tungu; hans ættmenn höfðu ávalt síðan konungsnafn fyrir hit œzta tignarnafn. Dyggvi var fyrstr konungr kallaðr sinna ættmanna; en áðr
2 CONC  váru þeir dróttnar kallaðir, en konur þeirra dróttningar, en drótt hirðsveitin. En Yngvi eða Ynguni var kallaðr hverr þeirra ættmanna alla ævi, en Ynglingar allir saman. Drótt dróttning var systir Dans konungs hins mikilláta, er 
2 CONC Danmörk er við kend.[2] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Dygve's mother was Drott, a daughter of King Danp, the son of Rig, who was first called "king" in the Danish tongue. His descendants always afterwards considered the title of king the title of highest dignity. Dygve was the first 
2 CONC of his family to be called king, for his predecessors had been called "Drottnar", and their wives "Drottningar", and their court "Drott". Each of their race was called Yngve, or Yngune, and the whole race together Ynglinger. The Q
2 CONC ueen Drott was a sister of King Dan Mikillati, from whom Denmark took its name.[3][4] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT In his Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson included a piece from Ynglingatal composed in the 9th century:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Kveðkat dul,
2 CONT     nema Dyggva hrör
2 CONT     Glitnis gná
2 CONT     at gamni hefr,
2 CONT     því at jódis
2 CONT     Ulfs ok Narfa
2 CONT     Konungmann
2 CONT     kjósa skyldi;
2 CONT     ok allvald
2 CONT     Yngva þjóðar
2 CONT     Loka mær
2 CONT     of leikinn hefr.[2][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dygve the Brave, the mighty king,
2 CONT     It is no hidden secret thing,
2 CONT     Has gone to meet a royal mate,
2 CONT     Riding upon the horse of Fate.
2 CONT     For Loke's daughter in her house
2 CONT     Of Yngve's race would have a spouse;
2 CONT     Therefore the fell-one snatched away
2 CONT     Brave Dygve from the light of day.[3][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hujus [Domar] filius Dyggui item in eadem regione vitæ metam invenit. Cui successit in regnum filius ejus Dagr [...][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Likewise Dyggve, his [Domar's] son, reached the limit of his life in that same region [Sweden]. His son Dag [...][8] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók also cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and it also gives Dyggvi as the successor of Dómarr and the predecessor of Dagr: ix Dómarr. x Dyggvi. xi Dagr.[9]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 8. DYGVE - King of Sweden from 162 until he died in his bed in 190. His son was:
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Fra SHM nr. 4/1996. Det er usikkert hva Dyggves virkelige navn var. Han har nok båret et trakisk eller persisk navn som var uforståelig for våre forfedre. Snorre beretter i Heimskringla at Dyggves mor het Drott og var datter av ko
2 CONC ng Danp, hvis far, Rig, var den første som kaltes konge. Dyggve skulle også være den første i sin ætt som kaltes konge, mens de tidligere ble kalt drotner. Dette kan være en indikasjon på at det fra omkring år 164 foregikk en fora
2 CONC ndring i navnebruken fra de uforståelige norrøne navn som vi kjenner idag. Det heter at Dyggves mar, Drott, var søster av kong Dan, og dermed skulle hun i tilfelle være datter av kong Rig, alias Hodd, Balders banemann, og kongen v
2 CONC ed Dnjepr i Russland var hennes morfar og ikke far.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Fra SHM nr. 4/1996. Det er usikkert hva Dyggves virkelige navn var. Han har nok båret et trakisk eller persisk navn som var uforståelig for våre forfedre. Snorre beretter i Heimskringla at Dyggves mor het Drott og var datter av ko
