2 CONT i) [RAGNVALD "the Wise" (-[894]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga names “Eystein the Clatterer, father of the wise counsellor Earl Rognwald the Powerful…”[129].  Snorre names Ragnvald Earl of More, a son of Eystein "Glumra" when recording that he had become a supporter of King Ha
2 CONC rald who had invested him with the districts of North More and Raumsdal[130].  Jarl of North and South Möre and of Raumsdal in Norway.]    
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)  [SIGURD "Riki/the Mighty" (-[892], bur Sydero, Dornoch Firth).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that Ragnvald gave “all the islands” (indicating Shetland and Orkney from the context) to “his brother Sigurd, the forecastleman on King Harald´s ship” and that Harald I "Hårfagre" King of Norway gave “the 
2 CONC title of earl” to Sigurd who remained in the islands after the king returned to Norway[131].  Orkneyinga Saga records that Sigurd “joined forces with Thorstein the Red, the son of Olaf the White and Aud the Deep-Minded” and togeth
2 CONC er conquered “the whole of Caithness and a large part of Argyll, Moray and Ross”, Sigurd building “a stronghold…in the south of Moray”[132].  Orkneyinga Saga records that Sigurd defeated and beheaded “Mælbrigte Earl of the Scots” 
2 CONC but that he died from an infected wound because a tooth in Mælbrigte´s head, strapped to Sigurd´s saddle, scratched his leg, and was buried “in a mound on the bank of the River Oykel”[133].  The Complete Peerage identifies the pla
2 CONC ce as “Sydero on the Dornoch Firth at the estuary of the Ekkialsbakki[134].  The Complete Peerage dates Sigurd´s appointment as Jarl to [875] and his death to [892][135], but there seems no basis for either date apart from interna
2 CONC l consistency with its reconstruction of events in the lives of this family.  Both dates should therefore be considered as extremely approximate, and as noted below the earlier date leads to difficult chonological difficulties wit
2 CONC h the reported career of Sigurd´s nephew Turf-Einar.]  [One child:] 
2 CONT
2 CONT (a) [GUTHORM (-[893]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga records that Sigurd was succeeded by his son “Guthorm who ruled the earldom for a year but died childless”[136].  According to Snorre´s Harald Harfager´s Saga, Sigurd died “without children” but his Saga of Olaf Ha
2 CONC raldson names Guthorm as son of Sigurd, recording that he succeeded his father as earl for one year[137].] 
2 CONT
2 CONT iii)  [MALAHULC .  
2 CONT
2 CONT Orderic Vitalis (who wrote in [1113]) names Malahulc as an uncle of Rollo and ancestor of the Tosny family[138].  He is not mentioned in the Sagas.]  See TOSNY.  
2 CONT =---------------------------=
2 CONT I henhold til Orknøyingenes saga går Ragnvalds Mørejarls ætt langt tilbake: «Heite Gors sønn var far til Sveide sjøkonge, far til Halvdan den gamle, far til Ivar Opplendingejarl, far til Øystein Glumra, far til Ragnvald jarl den m
2 CONC ektige og den rådsnare». Den samme sagaen strekker hans ætt tilbake til en mytologisk opprinnelse til skikkelse ved navn Fornjot fra Finland eller Kvenland.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1). Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagres saga, avsnitt 10. 
2 CONT Snorre Sturlasson: Olav den helliges saga, avsnitt 96.  Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 279.  Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 76
2 CONT
2 CONT Eystein is mentioned in several sources, but the so-called "Ascrida" (no Norwegian/Norse name, would have to be Astridr or Ása) is not mentioned in the sagas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=&emne=nor&person=%D8ystein%20Glumra From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Eystein (Forrás): http://www.thepeerage.com/p10478.htm#i104779
2 CONT
2 CONT Eystein 'the Noisy' Glumra, Jarl of the Uplanders (1)  M, #104779, b. 788 Last Edited=17 Apr 2005
2 CONT
