2 CONT The reason the notion of a family crest was brought into th
2 CONT
2 CONT e languagewas that those who were armigerous (entitled to bear arms) used to put their crest or achieveme 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT After Halfdan Whiteleg's death, according to the sagas, his son Eystein ruled Vestfold until a rival king named Skjold used his magic powers to have Eystein knocked overboard during a sailing expedition. Eystein's body was recover
2 CONC ed from the sea and buried with great ceremony.
2 CONT
2 CONT # Note: [Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flander & Kiev]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Note: Title: Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev, by Rupert Alen & Anna Dahlquist, 1997, King's River Publ.
2 CONT
2 CONT # Note: Page: 7 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps05/ps05_090.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT 51. OF KING EYSTEIN'S DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eystein, Halfdan Hvitbein's son, became king after in Raumarike
2 CONT
2 CONT and Westfold. He was married to Hild, a daughter of Eric
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnarsson, who was king in Westfold. Agnar, Eric's father, was a
2 CONT
2 CONT son of Sigtryg, king in the Vend district. King Eric had no son,
2 CONT
2 CONT and died while King Halfdan Hvitbein was still in life. The
2 CONT
2 CONT father and son, Halfdan and Eystein, then took possession of the
2 CONT
2 CONT whole of Westfold, which Eystein ruled over as long as he lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time there lived at Varna a king called Skjold, who was a
2 CONT
2 CONT great warlock. King Eystein went with some ships of war to
2 CONT
2 CONT Varna, plundered there, and carried away all he could find of
2 CONT
2 CONT clothes or other valuables, and of peasants' stock, and killed
2 CONT
2 CONT cattle on the strand for provision, and then went off. King
2 CONT
2 CONT Skjold came to the strand with his army, just as Eystein was at
2 CONT
2 CONT such a distance over the fjord that King Skjold could only see
2 CONT
2 CONT his sails. Then he took his cloak, waved it, and blew into it.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Eystein was sitting at the helm as they sailed in past
2 CONT
2 CONT Jarls, and another ship was sailing at the side of his, when
2 CONT
2 CONT there came a stroke of a wave, by which the boom of the other
2 CONT
2 CONT ship struck the king and threw him overboard, which proved his
2 CONT
2 CONT death. His men fished up his body, and it was carried into
2 CONT
2 CONT Borre, where a mound was thrown up over it, out towards the sea
2 CONT
2 CONT at Raden, near Vodle. So says Thjodolf: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "King Eystein sat upon the poop
2 CONT
2 CONT Of his good ship: with sudden swoop
2 CONT
2 CONT The swinging boom dashed him to hell,
2 CONT
2 CONT And fathoms deep the hero fell
2 CONT
2 CONT Beneath the brine. The fury whirl
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Loke, Tempest's brother's girl,
2 CONT
2 CONT Grim Hel, clutched his soul away;
2 CONT
2 CONT And now where Vodle's ocean bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Receives the ice-cold stream, the grave
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Eystein stands -- the good, the brave!" 
2 CONT
2 CONT *****************************
2 CONT
2 CONT Events in the life of Eysteinn "the Fart" Hálfdansson 
2 CONT
2 CONT ·King Skjold of Varna came to the strand with his army, just as Eystein was at such a distance over the fjord that King Skjold could only see his sails. Then he took his cloak, waved it, and blew into it. King Eystein was sitting 
2 CONC at the helm as they sailed in past Jarls, and another ship was sailing at the side of his, when there came a stroke of a wave, by which the boom of the other ship struck the king and threw him overboard, which proved his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT ·given Vestfold to rule over after his father took possession of Eystein's deceased father-in-law's territory 
2 CONT
2 CONT burial 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT in Borre, Raden, near Vodle, Vestfold.
2 CONT
2 CONT ·His men fished up his body, and it was carried into Borre, where a mound was thrown up over it, out towards the sea at Raden, near Vodle. So says Thjodolf: -- "King Eystein sat upon the poop Of his good ship: with sudden swoop Th
2 CONC e swinging boom dashed him to hell, And fathoms deep the hero fell Beneath the brine. The fury whirl Of Loke, Tempest's brother's girl, Grim Hel, clutched his soul away; And now where Vodle's ocean bay Receives the ice-cold stream
2 CONC , the grave Of Eystein stands -- the good, the brave!" 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT ·went with some ships of war to Varna, the land of King Skjold, and plundered there, and carried away all he could find of clothes or other valuables, and of peasants' stock, and killed cattle on the strand for provision, and then
2 CONC  began his return home
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Eystein "Fret/Fjert" Halfdansson, son of Halvdan Olavsson Kvitbein
2 CONT
2 CONT http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%98ystein_Halvdansson
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eystein_Halfdansson
2 CONT
2 CONT Øystein Halvdansson / Eystein Halfdansson / Eysteinn Hálfdansson
2 CONT
2 CONT (In English: Eystein Halfdansson)
2 CONT
2 CONT King in part of Norway: Romerike / Vestfold
2 CONT
2 CONT http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=gb&emne=asatru&person=%D8ystein%20Halvdansson
2 CONT
2 CONT Øystein Halvdansson (levde i tiden rundt 730 e. Kr.) var, i henhold til Snorre Sturlassons Ynglingesagaen, småkonge over Vestfold og Romerike. Han var sønn av Halvdan Kvitbein og Åsa Øysteinsdotter og ble gift med Hild Eiriksdotte
2 CONC r, datter til Eirik Agnarsson, småkonge i Vestfold. Deres sønn var Halvdan Øysteinsson, også kalt for den den gavmilde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Øystein arvet av sin far Solør, store deler av Hedmark, Toten, Hadeland og en del av Vestfold. Etter at svigerfaren Eirik Agnarsson døde sønnesløs arvet Øystein også resten av Vestfold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre forteller at Øystein dro på plyndringsferd til Varna og tok med seg alt av husdyr og verdisaker. Kongen av Varna var på denne tiden var Skjøld som etter sigende var «en stor trollmann». Da Skjøld kom til stranda og så Øyste
2 CONC ins seil vinket han med kappen sin og blåste inn i den. Trolldommen fikk bommen på Øysteins skip til å svinge og slo ham over bord. I sjøen druknet Øystein og det ble hans bane. Liket ble derimot berget og ført til Borre, hvor det
2 CONC  ble hauglagt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Druknet, begravet på Borrestranda (Hauglagt)
2 CONT
2 CONT Den eldste Noregshistoria frå 1170 fortel at Øystein hadde tilnamnet fjert
2 CONT
