2 CONT
2 CONT After Halfdan Whiteleg's death, according to the sagas, his son Eystein ruled Vestfold until a rival king named Skjold used his magic powers to have Eystein knocked overboard during a sailing expedition. Eystein's body was recover
2 CONC ed from the sea and buried with great ceremony.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Note: [Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flander & Kiev]
2 CONT     Note: Title: Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev, by Rupert Alen & Anna Dahlquist, 1997, King's River Publ.
2 CONT     Note: Page: 7
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Raumerike and Vestfold Eysteinn "the Fart" Hálfdanarson was given Vestfold to rule over after his father took possession of Eystein's deceased father-in-law's territory. He was King between 750 and 780.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Hild Eiríksdóttir, daughter of King of Vestfold Eiríkr Agnarsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went with some ships of war to Varna, the land of King Skjold, and plundered there, and carried away all he could find of clothes or other valuables, and of peasants' stock, and killed cattle on the strand for provision, and th
2 CONC en began his return home.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Skjold of Varna came to the strand with his army, just as Eystein was at such a distance over the fjord that King Skjold could only see his sails. Then he took his cloak, waved it, and blew into it. King Eystein was sitting a
2 CONC t the helm as they sailed in past Jarls, and another ship was sailing at the side of his, when there came a stroke of a wave, by which the boom of the other ship struck the king and threw him overboard, which proved his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in Borre, Raden, near Vodle, Vestfold, Norway. His men fished up his body, and it was carried into Borre, where a mound was thrown up over it, out towards the sea at Raden, near Vodle.
2 CONT
2 CONT So says Thjodolf: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "King Eystein sat upon the poop
2 CONT
2 CONT Of his good ship: with sudden swoop
2 CONT
2 CONT The swinging boom dashed him to hell,
2 CONT
2 CONT And fathoms deep the hero fell
2 CONT
2 CONT Beneath the brine. The fury whirl
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Loke, Tempest's brother's girl,
2 CONT
2 CONT Grim Hel, clutched his soul away;
2 CONT
2 CONT And now where Vodle's ocean bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Receives the ice-cold stream, the grave
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Eystein stands -- the good, the brave!"
2 CONT
