1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT The legendary Ragnar Sigurdson Lothbrok is a mythical viking primarily depicted by the two sagas ''Ragnar’s Tale'' and ''The Tale of Ragnar’s Sons''. Heimskringla, Hervarar saga ok Heiðreks, Sögubrot af nokkrum fornkonungum.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are people that suggest that all these 'sagas' are mythical fairytales should remember that not many people were litterate ( could read and write ) at this time. The oral tradition was in total practise.The people that put w
2 CONC ords on these events were often monks in monestries and they had their own opinion of what was going on. 'Saga' means 'Outsaying' from the nordic word 'utsaga' meaning an outspoken telling about things. Those that made this had th
2 CONC eir own experiences and had seen things and heard tellings by elders.
2 CONT Ragnar is depicted as a viking king of Denmark and Sweden who marries at a young age, is widowed and then remarries, having at least two sons with his first wife and at least five with his second. Book IX of ''Gesta Danorum'' desc
2 CONC ribes an earlier marriage than the sagas (giving Ragnar three wives in total with another son and two unnamed daughters) as well as Ragnar having children with women other than his wives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The first half of the sagas are situated in Scandinavia and deal firstly with Ragnar’s marriages and the deaths of his older sons in battle, including the associated revenge of those deaths. As Scandinavia in this time period was 
2 CONC not literate there are no historical records to either support or negate these stories. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The later parts of the sagas and Saxo’s work detail Ragnar’s exploits raiding England and mainland Europe, then Ragnar’s death in England and his sons' revenge attack against [http://www.geni.com/people/%C3%86lla-II-King-of-Northu
2 CONC mbria/6000000003243197392 King Ælla of Northumbria] (presented as the initial objective of the invasion of England in 865 by the Great Heathen Army). Ragnar’s sons by his second marriage all go on to be famous vikings themselves a
2 CONC nd in some cases kings, dominating Scandinavia and impacting on European affairs for the remainder of the century, as well as spawning several royal dynasties. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With the second portion of the stories taking place in Christian Europe it is possible to correlate some of the facts of the stories with written accounts of the time, notably the ''Anglo-Saxon Chronicle''. Evidence from the ''Chr
2 CONC onicle'' pertains more to Ragnar’s sons than Ragnar himself and are circumstantial in nature so that they cannot be taken as a verification that the sagas are factual records of historical events, rather only that portions of the 
2 CONC sagas’ stories seem to reflect historical events.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Data Justifications and Merging Guide== 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Name=== 
2 CONT
2 CONT The early sources do not use the names Ragnar and Lothbrok in combination to refer to a single person. The first recorded instance of the names being so used is Ari Þorgilsson’s reference to Ívarr Ragnarssonr loðbrókar in his ''Ís
2 CONC lendingabók'', written between 1120 and 1133. As a common name used in popular culture Ragnar Lodbrok Sigurdson is used for this profile but historically there is a strong argument that Ragnar and Lodbrok were husband and wife. In
2 CONC deed, if the origins of the legendary Ragnar lie with the Danish viking Reginheri there is no particular reason to accept as accurate the patronym Sigurdson.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Lothbrok=== 
2 CONT
2 CONT The sagas and Saxo relate Ragnar’s famous nickname “Hairy-Breeches” to his exploits in slaying a giant serpent(s) to rescue his first (or second) wife. However, in their earliest forms the sagas do not use the names Ragnar and Lot
2 CONC hbrok in combination, rather his sons are referred to in different portions of the saga as the “sons of Ragnar” and “the sons of Lothbrok”. Taken in combination, the form of spelling used for Lothbrok indicates that it is a femini
2 CONC ne name. This has been taken by academics to suggest that Loðbróka was actually Ragnar’s wife and that the two names were conflated in later versions of the story.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death Date=== 
2 CONT
2 CONT While later texts describe the Great Heathen Army as a revenge attack, which would imply it taking place soon after Ragnar’s death, the contemporary chronicles do not make this connection. As a latest date this would set Ragnar’s 
2 CONC death to before 865. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnar is often linked historically to Reginherus / Reginheri, a ''jarl'' at the court of Danish king Horik I who raided Paris in 845 and reportedly died not long after. As the strongest candidate for a historical Ragnar, this has
2 CONC  been used as the lower limit for an estimated date range.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death Location=== 
2 CONT
2 CONT The sagas and early English sources place Ragnar’s death in Northumbria, specifically in a snakepit. Later English sources relocate the murder to East Anglia with the murderer variously being King Edmund or a man named Berne. 
2 CONT Reginheri death location is not specifically detailed but contemporary Frankish reports indicate that he died shortly after returning to the court of King Horik, suggesting a death location in Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Birth Date=== 
2 CONT
2 CONT With a death between 845 and 865 and at least two marriages and seven children to account for (three and nine respectively, according to Saxo) a birth date before 795 seems to be the strongest statement that can be made with a low
2 CONC er limit of 765, making him 80 years old as an absolute maximum if he died in 845.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Birth Location=== 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweden or Denmark are the two logical locations for his birth, although technically neither nation as today existed in the 700s.. Most likely where his mother was born. Alfjheimr ( Alvhem ) todays Gothenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT The legendary king Ragnar of the sagas and other writings seems most closely associated with Denmark. 
2 CONT Reginheri as a Danish viking could logically be assumed to have been born in Denmark (though by no means is this proof).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Wives=== 
2 CONT
2 CONT The first woman he had a relation with was Ladgerda, Gerda from Lade in Gaulavalley Norway ( todays Trondheim ), 
2 CONT barn: Ragnars eldest children,
2 CONT one son Fridleif Ragnarsson and two unnamed daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second woman was Thora 'Borgarhjort' Herrödsdaughter 
2 CONT barn: 
2 CONT Eric and Agnar. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The third woman was Gisla Asbjørnsdatter 790, daughter of a Friesian leader
2 CONT barn : Ubbe Ragnarsson, earl of Friesland 820-878
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth woman was Aslaug ' Kraka'Sigurdsdatter'  'Randalin'  daughter of Sigurd Fafnisbane Volsung
2 CONT barn ;
2 CONT Ivar ' Benløs ' Ragnarsson, King of Dublin ca 820-873
2 CONT Rathbart Ragnarsson 818-
2 CONT Bjørn 'Jernside ' Ragnarsson, konge av Sverige appr.815-862
2 CONT Halvdan ' Hvitserk '  Ragnarsson, konge av Russland 820-877/853
2 CONT Sigurd '  Ormöga 'Ragnarsson, konge av Danmark 820-891
2 CONT Ragnhild Ragnarsdatter 820
2 CONT Regnald Ragnarsson 820-
2 CONT Dag Dunvarth Ragnarsson ?820-
2 CONT Agnar Ragnarsson, prins av Danmark ?820-
2 CONT
