2 CONT
2 CONT One of the things that makes this a difficult question to discuss is
2 CONT that the question "Was Ragnar Lothbrok historical?" is itself
2 CONT somewhat ambiguous. Thus, before the question can be discussed, the
2 CONT question has to first be more clearly defined. To mention two
2 CONT opposite extremes, a skeptic could ask whether or not everything which
2 CONT is said about the character of Ragnar Lothbrok is historically
2 CONT accurate, observe that the answer is certainly "no", and then claim
2 CONT victory. At the other extreme, a proponent of a historical Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok could ask if a Viking by the name of Ragnar ever existed,
2 CONT point out that a Viking having the correct name ("Reginheri") appears
2 CONT in the Frankish annals, and claim that Ragnar Lothbrok was therefore
2 CONT historical. Neither of these two extremes is acceptable in a serious
2 CONT argument on the subject, so I will discuss the subject from the
2 CONT following middle ground. The criteria which I will use are that in
2 CONT order for Ragnar Lothbrok to be considered as historical, there should
2 CONT be a historically documented person of that name who actually
2 CONT performed a significant number of the deeds attributed to the
2 CONT legendary Ragnar Lothbrok. I think these are reasonable criteria, and
2 CONT the remainder of this discussion is based on these principles. Now,
2 CONT to answer the question: No, Ragnar Lothbrok does not appear to be a
2 CONT historical figure, based on the above criteria. I will give some
2 CONT comments as to why I have this opinion, and then mention some reading
2 CONT material for those who want more.
2 CONT
2 CONT RAGNAR
2 CONT
2 CONT The contemporary historical records of the ninth century (when Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok supposedly lived) show only one Viking of the correct name, a
2 CONT Viking named "Reginheri" (a Latin form equivalent to the name Ragnar)
2 CONT in France WHO DIED IN THE YEAR 845, according to the contemporary
2 CONT Frankish annals. The emphasized words in the previous sentence are
2 CONT often conveninetly overlooked by those who wish to use Reginheri as a
2 CONT historical prototype for Ragnar Lothbrok. Since Reginheri died in
2 CONT France in the year 845, he cannot have participated in the later
2 CONT events which form the principal part of the legendary Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok's exploits. In addition, there is no good evidence that
2 CONT Reginheri was the father of any of the individuals who later came to
2 CONT be regarded as sons of Ragnar Lothbrok. Thus, Reginheri fails to
2 CONT satisfy the criterion mentioned above. No other historical Norseman
2 CONT named Ragnar is known for the appropriate time period.
2 CONT
2 CONT LOTHBROK
2 CONT
2 CONT No contemporary record gives this name, and it is significant that
2 CONT when the name finally does make it appearance in the records 200 years
2 CONT later, it stands alone. (Ari, writing in the twelfth century, was the
2 CONT first known writer to make Ragnar and Lothbrok the same person.) The
2 CONT name first appears (as "Lothbroc") in "Gesta Normannorum Ducum", by
2 CONT William of Jumieges, writing about 1070, in which Lothbroc is called
2 CONT he father of Bjorn Ironside. (A Viking named Bjorn is verified by the
2 CONT contemporary chronicles, but without the nickname.) Adam of Bremen,
2 CONT writing soon afterward, called Ivar the son of "Lodparchus". Besides
2 CONT the fact that this Lothbrok is not attested in any of the contemporary
2 CONT sources, there seems to be another problem, and that is that the name
2 CONT ("Lothbroka") appears to be a women's name. See the article on
2 CONT Ragnars saga" by Rory McTurk in "Medieval Scandinavia: an
2 CONT encyclopedia" (New York and London, 1993). If this argument based on
2 CONT philology is correct, then this Lothbrok(a), if historical at all,
2 CONT would be a women, and clearly not identical with the legendary Ragnarr
2 CONT Lothbrok. (I do not have the background in linguistics to comment
2 CONT further on this gender argument.)
2 CONT
2 CONT RAGNALL
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Fragmentary Annals of Ireland" (edited and translated by Joan N.
2 CONT Radner, Dublin, 1978, formerly called "Three Fragments") has an item
2 CONT of interest which has frequently been pointed out as possibly relating
2 CONT to the legend of Ragnar Lothbrok. In it, a certain Ragnall (Rognvald)
2 CONT son of Alpdan (Halfdan), king of Norway, is mentioned, and his
2 CONT exploits prior to the fall of York to the Danes are given, in a
2 CONT context in which it is at least arguable that Ragnall and Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok were the same person. There are two problem with this
2 CONT interpretation. First, Ragnar and Ragnall are not the same name, even
2 CONT though they look similar. Second, and more important, the Fragmentary
2 CONT Annals are themselves not a contemporary source, and there is good
2 CONT reason to be suspicious about them. However, even if we were to allow
2 CONT that the events given there are historical (a concession which many
2 CONT historians would be unwilling to make), and then concede further that
2 CONT these events form the basis of the Ragnar legend, then we would still
2 CONT have that the person on whom the legend was based did not have the
2 CONT right name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Could RAGNALL and LOTHBROK have been the same person?
2 CONT
2 CONT We have already seen that the only historically attested Ragnar
2 CONT (Reginheri) cannot reasonably be regarded as a historical prototype
2 CONT for Ragnar Lothbrok. Thus, it appears that the best attempt to argue
2 CONT for a historical Ragnar Lothbrok is to propose (as has been done on
2 CONT numerous occasions) that Ragnall and Lothbrok were both the same
2 CONT person, and then assume that the similar (but different) names Ragnall
2 CONT and Ragnar were accidently confused. Thus, let us see what
2 CONT assumptions are needed in order to assume that Ragnall and Lothbrok
2 CONT were the same person, assuming that they existed at all. In order for
2 CONT this to be the case, we must make the following assumptions:
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) We must assume that Adam of Bremen (late eleventh century) was
2 CONT correct in giving "Lodparchus" (i.e., Lothbrok) as the name of the
2 CONT father of Ivar (late ninth century).
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) We must assume that the "Coghad Gaedhel re Gallaibh" ("The War of
2 CONT the Gaedhil with the Gaill", ed. by Todd, London, 1867), a twelfth
2 CONT century Irish source, is correct in stating that Halfdan of Dublin
2 CONT (killed in Ireland in 877, according to the Annals of Ulster) was the
2 CONT son of a certain Ragnall, and that this Ragnall was the same as the
2 CONT Ragnall who appears in the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT (3) We must assume that the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle is correct in
2 CONT stating that a brother (unnamed, but called Ubbe in later sources) of
2 CONT Halfdan and Ivar was killed in England in 878, despite the
2 CONT contradictory testimony of Aethelweard which gives a very different
2 CONT reading for the same event (see 4).
2 CONT
2 CONT (4) We must assume that the chronicle of Aethelweard is wrong in
2 CONT stating that Halfdan brother of Ivar was killed in England in 878, for
2 CONT otherwise that would prove that Halfdan of Dublin (d. 877 in Ireland)
2 CONT was a different person from Halfdan brother of Ivar.
2 CONT
2 CONT (5) In addition to assuming that Halfdan of Dublin was the same
2 CONT person as Halfdan brother of Ivar, we must also assume that this Ivar
2 CONT was the same person as Adam of Bremen's Ivar, keeping in mind that
2 CONT Aethelweard's chronicle, if correct, would imply the existence of two
2 CONT Ivars in the British isles at this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT (6) We must assume that the philological argument making Lothbrok(a)
2 CONT a feminine name is incorrect.
2 CONT
2 CONT (7) If Ari, the earliest author to mention Ragnar Lothbrok, is to be
2 CONT considered a reliable source on this matter, then we must also assume
2 CONT that Halfdan of Dublin was the same person as the Halfdan brother of
2 CONT Sigifrid who appears in the Annals of Fulda for the year 873, despite
2 CONT the severe chronological problems which that would cause with Ari's
2 CONT genealogies.
2 CONT
2 CONT of the above assumptions, numbers (1) through (6) are crucial if one
2 CONT wishes to argue that Ragnall and Lothbrok were the same, and (7) is
2 CONT needed also if it is to be assumed that the information given by Ari
2 CONT is accurate. Given the noncontemporary nature of the first two items,
2 CONT along with the contradictions present some of the others, there is a
2 CONT very small chance that all six of the crucial assumptions are correct.
2 CONT However, if any one of the first six items is false, then the case for
2 CONT Ragnall being the same as Lothbrok collapses, and we must conclude
2 CONT that the "Ragnall Lothbrok" attempt for a historical Ragnar Lothbrok
2 CONT is unsatisfactory. [Note: See R. W. McTurk's article "Ragnarr
2 CONT Lothbrok in the Irish Annals?" (Proceedings of the Seventh Viking
2 CONT Congress, 1976, pp. 93-123), where a different, but much more rigid,
2 CONT list of the same type is given.]
2 CONT
2 CONT CONCLUSIONS
2 CONT
2 CONT Since all of the above attempts to find a historical Ragnar Lothbrok
2 CONT fail to satisfy the mentioned criteria, Lothbrok and Ragnall come from
2 CONT noncontemporary sources which are themselves open to suspicion, and
2 CONT the historical records show nobody else (as far as I know) who could
2 CONT be plausibly identified with Ragnar Lothbrok, it must be concluded
2 CONT that Ragnar Lothbrok is not historical according to the terms
2 CONT described above. In fact, if there is any historical basis to Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok legend, it is quite likely that Ragnar Lothbrok is the result
2 CONT of combining two or more distinct individuals into a single character
2 CONT having the attributes of both, in much the same way as Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok's legendary "father" Sigurd Ring is in fact a composite of
2 CONT two different men who fought against each other for the Danish throne
2 CONT in the year 814, Sigifridus ("Sigurd") and Anulo (of which "Ring" is a
2 CONT translation of Latin "Annulus"). However, such composite characters
2 CONT cannot be considered as historical, and there is no evidence which
2 CONT comes close to being contemporary which shows that either Lothbrok or
2 CONT Ragnall existed.
2 CONT
2 CONT FURTHER READING
2 CONT
2 CONT The most ambitious attempt to portray Ragnar Lothbrok as a historical
2 CONT figure is "Scandinavian Kings in the British Isles 850-880" by Alfred
2 CONT P. Smyth (Oxford University Press, 1977). For a very critical
2 CONT examination of Smyth's views, see "High-kings, Vikings and other
2 CONT kings", by Donnchadh O' Corrain, in Irish Historical Review, vol 21
2 CONT (1979), pp. 283-323 (very highly recommended). Both of these sources
2 CONT cite numerous other relevant sources for those who are interested in
2 CONT further details.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Note: The usual apologies if my transliterations from the Old Norse
2 CONT alphabet into the alphabet available to me is a bit sloppy.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart Baldwin
1 NOTE Historical personage - see www.wikipedia.org
1 NOTE Roi Viking mort dans une fosse à Serpents ou il a été jetté par le Roi Saxon Aella Ier Roi de Deira qui mourra avec son fils Osfrith en 633 sous les coups de trois fils de Sigurd
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-2782
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT Ragnar Lodbrok ('Hairy-Breeks') was a Norse legendary hero from the Viking Age who was thoroughly reshaped in Old Norse poetry and legendary sagas. Ragnar was said to be the son of Siguard Randversson and Alfhild Gandolfsdottir. H
2 CONC e married Aslang Sigurdsdottir of Denmark, daughter of Sigurd 'Fovnesbane', and they had numerous children. The _Ragnarssona báttr_ mentions his sons Bjorn 'Ironside', Hvitserk, Ivar 'the Boneless', and Sigurd 'Snake-in-the-Eye', 
2 CONC and the half-brothers Eric and Agnar. Other names of children linked to him include: Halfdan 'White Shirt', Ragnaldir, Alof/Olaf and Ubbe/Ubbi. Of these, Bjorn 'Ironside' is recorded as having progeny, though others probably did a
2 CONC lso.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Ragnar is something of a hero in his native Scandinavia, reliable accounts of his life are very sketchy and heavily based on ancient Viking sagas. Even the dating of his reign is not certain; there are sources that date i
2 CONC t from 750 to 794, and others from 860 to 865. Neither really matches with what is known of him, though he may perhaps have held power as a warlord from approximately 835 to his death in 865, perhaps only being recognised as king 
2 CONC (of Lethra, in what is now Denmark) in the last five years of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnar apparently spent most of his life as a pirate and raider, invading one country after another. One of his favourite tactics was to attack Christian cities on church feast days, knowing that many soldiers would be in church. 
2 CONC He would generally accept a huge payment to leave his victims alone, only to come back later and demand more riches in exchange for leaving.
2 CONT
2 CONT However he was also a gifted military leader. By 845 he was a powerful man and most likely as contemporary of the first ruler of Russia, the Viking Rurik. It is said he was always seeking new adventures because he was worried that
2 CONC  his freebooting sons would do things that would outshine his own achievements.
2 CONT
2 CONT A historic Ragnar Lodbrok is held to have been a jarl at the court of the Danish king Horik I (814-854), and this Ragnar participated in the Viking plunder of Paris in 845. In that year he is said to have sailed southward, looking
2 CONC  for new worlds to conquer. With 120 ships and 5,000 Viking warriors, he landed in what is now France, probably at the Seine estuary, and ravaged West Francia, as the westernmost part of the Frankish Empire was then known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris was captured in that year and held to ransom by a Viking raider who the sagas say was Ragnar Lodbrok. The king of West Francia, Charlemagne's grandson Charles 'the Bald', paid him a sum not to destroy the city. Ragnar Lodbro
2 CONC k, according to Viking sources, was satisfied with no less than 7,000 pounds of silver in exchange for sparing the city. However, that did not stop Ragnar from attacking other parts of France, and it took a long time for the Frank
2 CONC s to drive him out.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, Ragnar's sons were to return for more booty. Among their feats was destroying the city of Rouen several times. Ultimately some of them settled there permanently, in a land that became known as Normandy (for 'Northmen', as t
2 CONC he Franks called the Scandinavians or the Norman as the Norwegians called themselves).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnar turned his attention to England. In 865 he landed in Northumbria on its north-east coast. It is claimed that there he was defeated in battle for the only time, by King Aelle II of Northumbria. There are several versions of 
2 CONC the story of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the most colourful, Aelle's men were said to have captured Ragnar, and the king ordered him thrown into a pit filled with poisonous snakes. As he was slowly being bitten to death, he is alleged to have exclaimed 'How the little
2 CONC  pigs would grunt if they knew the situation of the old boar!', referring to the vengeance he hoped his sons would wreak when they heard of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT An alternative version says that he landed by accident in East Anglia and there befriended King Edmund before being killed by a jealous courtier. The murderer was said to have escaped to Denmark and to have blamed Edmund for Ragna
2 CONC r's demise.
1 NOTE See Snorre's Saga and the Icelandic Landnamobok (Book of Settlment).
2 CONT "Greatest of all Vikings" (Makers of England, Arthur Bryant, p. 95,Doubleday & Comp., 1962)
2 CONT "Royalty for Commoners", Roderick W. Stuart, 1992, 2nd edition.
2 CONT "Pedigrees of the County Families of Yorkshire", Foster, 1874, 2 vols.vol1: West Riding, vol2: North and East Ridings
2 CONT "Ancestral roots of certain American colonists who came to Americabefore 1700", Weis, 1992, seventh edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Was RAGNAR LOTHBROK historical?
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the things that makes this a difficult question to discuss is
2 CONT that the question "Was Ragnar Lothbrok historical?"  is itself
2 CONT somewhat ambiguous.  Thus, before the question can be discussed, the
2 CONT question has to first be more clearly defined.  To mention two
2 CONT opposite extremes, a skeptic could ask whether or not everything which
2 CONT is said about the character of Ragnar Lothbrok is historically
2 CONT accurate, observe that the answer is certainly "no", and then claim
2 CONT victory.  At the other extreme, a proponent of a historical Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok could ask if a Viking by the name of Ragnar ever existed,
2 CONT point out that a Viking having the correct name ("Reginheri") appears
2 CONT in the Frankish annals, and  claim that Ragnar Lothbrok was therefore
2 CONT historical.  Neither of these two extremes is acceptable in a serious
2 CONT argument on the subject, so I will discuss the subject from the
2 CONT following middle ground.  The criteria which I will use are that in
2 CONT order for Ragnar Lothbrok to be considered as historical, there should
2 CONT be a historically documented person of that name who actually
2 CONT performed a significant number of the deeds attributed to the
2 CONT legendary Ragnar Lothbrok.  I think these are reasonable criteria, and
2 CONT the  remainder of this discussion is based on these principles.  Now,
2 CONT to answer the question:  No, Ragnar Lothbrok does not appear to be a
2 CONT historical figure, based on the above criteria.  I will give some
2 CONT comments as to why I have this opinion, and then mention some reading
2 CONT material for those who want more.
2 CONT
2 CONT RAGNAR
2 CONT
2 CONT The contemporary historical records of the ninth century (when Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok supposedly lived) show only one Viking of the correct name, a
2 CONT Viking named "Reginheri" (a Latin form equivalent to the name Ragnar)
2 CONT in France WHO DIED IN THE YEAR 845, according to the contemporary
2 CONT Frankish annals.  The emphasized words in the previous sentence are
2 CONT often conveninetly overlooked by those who wish to use Reginheri as a
2 CONT historical prototype for Ragnar Lothbrok.  Since Reginheri died in
2 CONT France in the year  845, he cannot have participated in the later
2 CONT events which form the principal part of the legendary Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok's exploits.  In addition, there is no good evidence that
2 CONT Reginheri was the father of any of the individuals who later came to
2 CONT be regarded as sons of Ragnar Lothbrok.  Thus, Reginheri fails to
2 CONT satisfy the criterion mentioned above.  No other historical Norseman
2 CONT named Ragnar is known for the appropriate time period.
2 CONT
2 CONT LOTHBROK
2 CONT
2 CONT No contemporary record gives this name, and it is significant that
2 CONT when the name finally does make it appearance in the records 200 years
2 CONT later, it stands alone.  (Ari, writing in the twelfth century, was the
2 CONT first known writer to make Ragnar and Lothbrok the same person.)  The
2 CONT name first appears (as "Lothbroc") in "Gesta Normannorum Ducum", by
2 CONT William of Jumieges, writing about 1070, in which Lothbroc is called
2 CONT he father of Bjorn Ironside.  (A Viking named Bjorn is verified by the
2 CONT contemporary chronicles, but without the nickname.)  Adam of Bremen,
2 CONT writing soon afterward, called Ivar the son of "Lodparchus".  Besides
2 CONT the fact that this Lothbrok is not attested in any of the contemporary
2 CONT sources, there seems to be another problem, and that is that the name
2 CONT ("Lothbroka") appears to be a women's name.  See the article on
2 CONT Ragnars saga" by Rory McTurk in "Medieval Scandinavia:  an
2 CONT encyclopedia" (New York and London, 1993).  If this argument based on
2 CONT philology is correct, then this Lothbrok(a), if historical at all,
2 CONT would be a women, and clearly not identical with the legendary Ragnarr
2 CONT Lothbrok.  (I do not have the background in linguistics to comment
2 CONT further on this gender argument.)
2 CONT
2 CONT RAGNALL
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Fragmentary Annals of Ireland" (edited and translated by Joan N.
2 CONT Radner, Dublin, 1978, formerly called "Three Fragments") has an item
2 CONT of interest which has frequently been pointed out as possibly relating
2 CONT to the legend of Ragnar Lothbrok.  In it, a certain Ragnall (Rognvald)
2 CONT son of Alpdan (Halfdan), king of Norway, is mentioned, and his
2 CONT exploits prior to the fall of York to the Danes are given, in a
2 CONT context in which it is at least arguable that Ragnall and Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok were the same person.  There are two problem with this
2 CONT interpretation.  First, Ragnar and Ragnall are not the same name, even
2 CONT though they look similar.  Second, and more important, the Fragmentary
2 CONT Annals are themselves not a contemporary source, and there is good
2 CONT reason to be suspicious about them.  However, even if we were to allow
2 CONT that the events given there are historical (a concession which many
2 CONT historians would be unwilling to make), and then concede further that
2 CONT these events form the basis of the Ragnar legend, then we would still
2 CONT have that the person on whom the legend was based did not have the
2 CONT right name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Could RAGNALL and LOTHBROK have been the same person?
2 CONT
2 CONT We have already seen that the only historically attested Ragnar
2 CONT (Reginheri) cannot reasonably be regarded as a historical prototype
2 CONT for Ragnar Lothbrok.  Thus, it appears that the best attempt to argue
2 CONT for a historical Ragnar Lothbrok is to propose (as has been done on
2 CONT numerous occasions) that Ragnall and Lothbrok were both the same
