2 CONT
2 CONT Flourished in the 9th century 
2 CONT Viking whose life passed into legend in medieval European literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Ragnar was said to be the father of three sons, Halfdan, Inwaer (Ivar the Boneless), and Hubba (Ubbe), who led a Viking invasion of East Anglia in 865 seeking to avenge Ragnar's murder. In the Europea
2 CONC n literature of the several centuries following Ragnar's death, his name is surrounded with considerable legend. In the Gesta Danorum (c. 1185) of the Danish historian Saxo Grammaticus, he was a 9th-century Danish king whose campa
2 CONC igns included a battle with the Holy Roman emperor Charlemagne. According to Saxo's legendary history, Ragnar was eventually captured by the Anglo-Saxon king Aella of Northumbria and thrown into a snake pit to die. This story is a
2 CONC lso recounted in the later Icelandic works Ragnars saga lodbrókar and Tháttr af Ragnarssonum. The 12th-century Icelandic poem Krákumál provides a romanticized description of Ragnar's death and links him in marriage with a daughter
2 CONC  of Sigurd (Siegfried) and Brynhild (Brunhild), figures from the heroic literature of the ancient Teutons. The actions of Ragnar and his sons are also recounted in the Orkney Islands' poem Háttalykill.
2 CONT ____________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnarr Loðbrók or Ragnar Lodbrok was a semi-legendary King of Denmark and Sweden who reigned sometime in the eighth or ninth centuries. Although he is something of a hero in his native Scandinavia, reliable accounts of his life a
2 CONC re very sketchy and heavily based on ancient Viking sagas. Even the dating of his reign is not certain; there are sources that date it from 750–794, and others from 860–865. Neither matches with what we know of him, and he probabl
2 CONC y held power as a warlord from approximately 835 to his death in 865, perhaps only being recognized as king in the last five years of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Ragnar was a pagan who claimed to be a direct descendant of the god Odin. One of his favorite strategies was to attack Christian cities on holy feast days, knowing that many soldiers would be in church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raids
2 CONT He spent most of his life as a pirate and raider, invading one country after another. He would generally accept a huge payment to leave his victims alone, only to come back later and demand more riches in exchange for leaving. But
2 CONC  as the extent of his realm shows, he was also a gifted military leader.
2 CONT
2 CONT France
2 CONT By 845, he was a powerful ruler, and most likely a contemporary of the first ruler of Russia, the Viking Rurik. It is said he was always seeking new adventures because he was worried that his freebooting sons would do things that 
2 CONC outshined his own achievements.
2 CONT
2 CONT In that year, he sailed southward, looking for new worlds to conquer. With 120 ships and 5,000 Viking warriors, he landed in modern France, probably at the Seine estuary, and ravaged West Francia, as the westernmost part of the Fr
2 CONC ankish empire was then known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also in 845, Paris was captured and held ransom by a Viking raider, whom the sagas say was Ragnar Lodbrok. The traditional date for this is March 28, which is today referred to as Ragnar Lodbrok Day by many Scandinavians. The King
2 CONC  of West Francia, Charlemagne’s son Charles II "The Bald", paid him a fantastic amount of money not to destroy the city. Ragnar Lodbrok, according to Viking sources, was satisfied with no less than 7,000 pounds of silver in exchan
2 CONC ge for sparing the city. However, that did not stop Ragnar from attacking other parts of France, and it took a long time for the Franks to drive him out.
2 CONT
2 CONT England
2 CONT After he was done with France, he turned his attention to England. In 865, he landed in Northumbria on the northeast coast of England. It is claimed that here he was defeated in battle for the only time, by King Aelle of Northumbr
2 CONC ia. Ella’s men captured Ragnar, and the King ordered him thrown into a pit filled with poisonous snakes. As he was slowly being bitten to death, he was alleged to have exclaimed "How the little pigs would grunt if they knew the si
2 CONC tuation of the old boar!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT One Viking saga states that when his four sons heard the manner of his death, they all reacted in great sorrow. Hvitserk, who was playing chess, gripped the piece so hard that he bled from his fingernails. Björn Ironside grabbed a
2 CONC  spear so tightly that he left an impression in it, and Sigurd Snake-Eye, who was trimming his nails, cut straight through to the bone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnar’s fourth son, Ivar the Boneless soon learned the details of his father’s death and swore that he would avenge his father’s killing, in time-honored Viking tradition. In 866, Ivar crossed the North Sea with a large army, met
2 CONC  King Ella in battle, and captured him. He sentenced him to die according to the custom of Rista Blodörn, an exceedingly painful death. Although this story may not be accurate, like virtually all tales concerning Ragnar Lodbrok, h
2 CONC is death had serious consequences. Ivor was the mastermind behind the attacks on the English mainland in the final quarter of the ninth century. He invaded East Anglia, and the following year attacked York. He was aided by the int
2 CONC ernal struggle for power in Northumbria—which he was of course responsible for by killing Ella. These wars were a prelude to the long struggle of the Saxons of Alfred the Great against the "Danes" a generation later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, in France, the Vikings kept coming back for more booty. Among their feats was destroying the city of Rouen several times. Ultimately, many of them settled there permanently, in a land that became known as Normandy (for 
2 CONC "Northmen", as the Franks called the Vikings).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnar married Aslaug Sigurdsdottir, daughter of Sigurd Wolsung and Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT THE old sagas, or hero tales of the north, are full of stories of enchantment and strange marvels. We have told one of these tales in the record of King Rolf and Princess Torborg. We have now to tell that of Ragnar Lodbrok, a hero
2 CONC  king of the early days, whose story is full of magical incidents. That this king reigned and was a famous man in his days there is no reason to doubt, but around his career gathered many fables, as was apt to be the case with the
2 CONC  legends of great men in those days. To show what these tales were like we take from the sagas the marvellous record of Ragnar and his wives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In East Gothland in the ancient days there lived a mighty jarl, or earl, named Herröd, who was descended from the gods. He had a daughter named Tora, who was famed for her beauty and virtue, but proved as hard to win for a wife as
2 CONC  Princess Torborg had been. She dwelt in a high room which had a wall built around it like a castle, and was called Castle Deer, because she surpassed all other women in beauty as much as the deer surpasses all other animals. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father, who was very fond of her, gave her as a toy a small and wonderfully beautiful snake which he had received in a charmed egg in Bjar- [20] maland. It proved to be an unwelcome gift. The snake was at first coiled in a lit
2 CONC tle box, but soon grew until the box would not hold it, and in time was so big that the room would not hold it. So huge did it become in the end that it lay coiled in a ring around the outer walls, being so long that its head and 
2 CONC tail touched. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It got to be so vicious that no one dared come near it except the maiden and the man who fed it, and his task was no light one, for it devoured an ox at a single meal. The jarl was sorry enough now that he had given his daughter s
2 CONC uch a present. It was one not easy to get rid of, dread of the snake having spread far and wide, and though he offered his daughter with a great dower to the man who should kill it, no one for a long time ventured to strive for th
2 CONC e reward. The venom which it spat out was enough to destroy any warrior. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At length a suitor for the hand of the lovely princess was found in Ragnar, the young son of Sigurd Ring, then one of the greatest monarchs of the age, with all Sweden and Norway under his sway, as the sagas tell. Ragnar, though s
2 CONC till a boy, had gained fame as a dauntless warrior, and was a fit man to dare the venture with the great snake, though for a long time he seemed to pay no heed to the princess. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But meanwhile he had made for himself a strange coat. It was wrought out of a hairy hide, which he boiled in pitch, drew through sand, and then dried and hardened in the sun. The next summer [21] he sailed to East Gothland, hid hi
2 CONC s ships in a small bay, and at dawn of the next day proceeded toward the maiden's bower, spear in hand and wearing his strange coat. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There lay the dreaded serpent, coiled in a ring round the wall. Ragnar, nothing daunted, struck it boldly with his spear, and before it could move in defence struck it a second blow, pressing the spear until it pierced through the
2 CONC  monster's body. So fiercely did the snake struggle that the spear broke in two, and it would have destroyed Ragnar with the venom it poured out if he had not worn his invulnerable coat. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The noise of the struggle and the fierceness of the snake's convulsions, which shook the whole tower, roused Tora and her maids, and she looked from her window to see what it meant. She saw there a tall man, but could not distingu
2 CONC ish his features in the grey dawn. The serpent was now in its death throes, though this she did not know, and she called out: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Who are you, and what do you want?" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnar answered in this verse: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "For the maid fair and wise 
2 CONT
2 CONT I would venture my life. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The scale-fish got its death wound 
2 CONT
2 CONT From a youth of fifteen!"
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Then he went away, taking the broken handle of the spear with him. Tora listened in surprise, for she learned from the verse that a boy of fifteen [22] had slain the great monster, and she marvelled at his great size for his years
2 CONC , wondering if he were man or wizard. When day came she told her father of the strange event, and the jarl drew out the broken spear from the snake, finding it to be so heavy that few men could have lifted it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Who had killed the serpent and earned the reward? The jarl sent a mandate throughout his kingdom, calling all men together, and when they came he told them the story of the snake's death, and bade him who possessed the handle of t
2 CONC he spear to present it, as he would keep his word with any one, high or low. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnar and his men stood on the edge of the throng as the broken head of the spear was passed round, no one being able to present the handle fitting it. At length it came to Ragnar, and he drew forth the handle from his cloak, sho
2 CONC wing that the broken ends fitted exactly. A great feast for the victor was now given by Jarl Herröd, and when Ragnar saw the loveliness of Tora, he was glad to ask her for his queen, while she was equally glad to have such a hero 
2 CONC for her spouse. A splendid bridal followed and the victor took his beautiful bride home. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This exploit gave Ragnar great fame and he received the surname of Lodbrok, on account of the strange coat he had worn. Ragnar and Tora lived happily together but not to old age, for after some years she took sick and died, leavin
2 CONC g two sons, Erik and Agnar, who grew up to be strong and beautiful [23] youths. Ragnar had loved her greatly and after her death said he would marry no other woman. Nor could he comfort himself at home but began to wander abroad o
2 CONC n warlike voyages, that he might drive away his sorrow. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leaving Ragnar Lodbrok to his travels, let us take up the strange story of another fair maiden, who was to have much to do with his future life. She was named Aslög and was the daughter of King Sigurd Fafnisbane, of Germany. Soon 
2 CONC after she was born enemies of her father killed him and her mother and all of his race they could find. Her life was saved by Heimer, foster-father to her mother, who to get her away from the murderers had a large harp made with a
2 CONC  hollow frame, in which he hid the child and all the treasure he could find. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then he wandered far as a travelling harper, letting the child out when they came to solitary woods, and when she wept and moaned silencing her by striking the strings of the harp. After long journeying he came to a cottage in Nor
2 CONC way called Spangerhed, where lived a beggar and his wife. Seeing a gold bracelet under Heimer's rags, and some rich embroidery sticking from the harp, the beggar and his wife killed him during the night and broke open the harp. Th
2 CONC ey found in it the wealth they sought, but the discovery of the pretty little girl troubled them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "What shall we do with this child?" he asked. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "We will bring her up as our own, and name her Kraka, after my mother," said his wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [24] "But no one will believe that ugly old people like us can have so fair a daughter." 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Let me manage it," said the wife. "I will put tar on her head so that her hair will not be too long, and keep her in ragged clothes and at the hardest work." 
2 CONT
2 CONT This they did and little Aslög grew up as a beggar's child. And as she kept strangely silent, never speaking, all people thought her dumb. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One day, when Aslög was well grown, Ragnar Lorbrok came that way, cruising along the Norway coast. The crew was out of bread and men were sent ashore to bake some at a house they saw in the distance. This house was Spangerhed, whe
2 CONC re Kraka dwelt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She had seen the ships come up and the men land, and was ashamed to be seen by strangers as she was, so she washed herself and combed her hair, though she had been bidden never to do so. So long and thick had her hair grown that i
2 CONC t reached to the ground and covered her completely. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the cooks came to bake their bread they were so surprised at the beauty of the maiden that they let the loaves burn while looking at her, and on being blamed for this carelessness on their return to the ship said they could n
2 CONC ot help it, for they had been bewitched by the face of the loveliest maiden they had ever gazed upon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "She cannot be as lovely as Tora was," said Ragnar. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "There was never a lovelier woman," they de- [25] clared, and Ragnar was so struck by their story that he sent messengers ashore to learn if they were telling the truth. If it were so, he said, if Kraka were as beautiful as Tora, 
2 CONC they were bidden to bring her to him neither dressed nor undressed, neither fasting nor satisfied, neither alone nor in company. The messengers found the maiden as fair as the cooks had said and repeated the king's demand. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Your king must be out of his mind, to send such a message," said the beggar's wife; but Kraka told them that she would come as their king wished, but not until the next morning. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The next day she came to the shore where the ship lay. She was completely covered with her splendid hair, worn like a net around her. She had eaten an onion before coming, and had with her the old beggar's sheep dog; so that she h
2 CONC ad fulfilled Ragnar's three demands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her wit highly pleased Ragnar and he asked her to come on board, but she would not do so until she had been promised peace and safety. When she was taken to the cabin Ragnar looked at her in delight. He thought that she surpassed 
2 CONC Tora in beauty, and offered a prayer to Odin, asking for the love of the maiden. Then he took the gold-embroidered dress which Tora had worn and offered it to Kraka, saying in verse, in the fashion of those times: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Will you have Tora's robe? It suits you well. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her white hands have played upon it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lovely and kind was she to me until death."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [26] Kraka answered, also in verse: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "I dare not take the gold-embroidered robe which adorned Tora the fair. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It suits not me. Kraka am I called in coal-black baize. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I have ever herded goats on the stones by the sea-shore."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "And now I will go home," she added. "If the king's mind does not change he can send for me when he will." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then she went back to the beggar's cottage and Ragnar sailed in his ship away. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Of course every one knows without telling what came from such an invitation. It was not long before Ragnar was back with his ship and he found Kraka quite ready to go with him. And when they reached his home a splendid entertainme
2 CONC nt was given, during which the marriage between Ragnar and Kraka took place, everything being rich and brilliant and all the great lords of the kingdom being present. It will be seen that, though the Princess Aslög pretended to be
2 CONC  dumb during her years of youthful life in the beggar's cottage, she found her voice and her wits with full effect when the time came to use them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was now the queen of a great kingdom, and lived for many years happily with her husband Ragnar. And among her children were two sons who were very different from other men. The oldest was called Iwar. He grew up to be tall and
2 CONC  strong, though there were no bones in his body, but only gristle, so that he could not stand, but had to be carried everywhere on a litter. Yet he was very [27] wise and prudent. The second gained the name of Ironside, and was so
2 CONC  tough of skin that he wore no armor in war, but fought with his bare body without being wounded. To the people this seemed the work of magic. There were two others who were like other men. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since the older brothers, the sons of Tora, had long been notable as warriors, the younger brothers, when they grew up, became eager to win fame and fortune also, and they went abroad on warlike expeditions, fighting many battles,
2 CONC  winning many victories, and gaining much riches. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But Iwar, the boneless one, was not satisfied with this common fighting, but wanted to perform some great exploit, that would give them a reputation everywhere for courage. There was the town of Hvitaby (now Whitby, in Yorkshire, 
2 CONC England), which many great warriors had attacked, their father among them, but all had been driven back by the power of magic or necromancy. If they could take this stronghold it would give them infinite honor, said Iwar, and to t
2 CONC his his brothers agreed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To Hvitaby they sailed, and leaving their younger brother Ragnwald in charge of the ships, because they thought him too young to take part in so hard a battle, they marched against the town. The place was ably defended, not only b
2 CONC y men but by two magical heifers, their charm being that no man could stand before them or even listen to their lowing. When these beasts were loosed and ran [28] out towards the troops, the men were so scared by the terrible soun
2 CONC d of their voices that Ironside had all he could do to keep them from a panic flight, and many of them fell prostrate. But Iwar, who could not stand, but was carried into battle upon shields, took his bow and sent his arrows with 
2 CONC such skill and strength that both the magic heifers were slain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then courage came back to the troops and the townsmen were filled with terror. And in the midst of the fighting Ragnwald came up with the men left to guard the ships. He was determined to win some of the glory of the exploit and a
2 CONC ttacked the townsmen with fury, rushing into their ranks until he was cut down. But in the end the townsmen were defeated and the valiant brothers returned with great honor and spoil, after destroying the castle. Thus it was that 
2 CONC the sons of Kraka gained reputation as valiant warriors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But meanwhile Kraka herself was like to lose her queenly station, for Ragnar visited King Osten of Upsala who had a beautiful daughter named Ingeborg. On seeing her, his men began to say that it would be more fitting for their kin
2 CONC g to have this lovely princess for his wife, instead of a beggar's daughter like Kraka. Ragnar heard this evil counsel, and was so affected by it that he became betrothed to Ingeborg. When he went home he bade his men to say nothi
2 CONC ng about this betrothal, yet in some way Kraka came to know of it. That night she asked Ragnar for news and he said he had none to tell. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [29] "If you do not care to tell me news," said Kraka, "I will tell you some. It is not well done for a king to affiance himself to one woman when he already has another for his wife. And, since your men chose to speak of me as a 
2 CONC beggar's daughter, let me tell you that I am no such thing, but a king's daughter and of much higher birth than your new love Ingeborg." 
2 CONT
2 CONT "What fable is this you tell me?" said Ragnar. "Who, then, were your parents?" 
2 CONT
2 CONT "My father was King Sigurd Fafnisbane and my mother was the Amazon Brynhilda, daughter of King Budle." 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Do you ask me to believe that the daughter of these great people was named Kraka and brought up in a peasant's hut?" 
2 CONT
2 CONT The queen now told him that her real name was Aslög and related all the events of her early life. And as a sign that she spoke the truth, she said that her next child, soon to be born, would be a son and would have a snake in his 
2 CONC eye. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It came out as she said, the boy, when born, having the strange sign of which she had spoken, so that he was given a name that meant Sigurd Snake-in-Eye. So rejoiced was Ragnar at this that he ceased to think of Ingeborg and all h
2 CONC is old love for Kraka, or Aslög as she was now called, came back. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The remainder of the lives of Ragnar and Aslög and of their warlike sons is full of valiant deeds and magic arts, far too long to be told here, but [30] which gave them a high place in the legendary lore of the north, in which Rag
2 CONC nar Lodbrok is one of the chief heroes. At length Ragnar was taken prisoner by King Ethelred of England and thrown into a pit full of serpents, where he died. Afterwards Iwar and his brothers invaded England, conquered that countr
2 CONC y, and avenged their father by putting Ethelred to death by torture. Iwar took England for his kingdom and the realms of the north were divided among his brothers, and many more were the wars they had, until death ended the career
2 CONC  of these heroes of northern legend
1 NOTE King at Lethra, King of Denmark and Sweden. See Snorre's Saga and theIcelandic Landnamobok (Book of Settlement).   Lodbrok translates to HairyBritches.
2 CONT
2 CONT The celebrated and heroic warrior Ragnar, so called because he alwayswore in battle a pair of enchanted breeches, made of a bear skin with thehair outwards. King Reymer Lodbrok having invaded England in 794, wastaken prisoner by A
2 CONC ella, King of Northumberland, who put him to death ina most cruel manner, he being thrown into a pit hole full of adders,which stung him to death. When news of his death reached Denmark his fivevaliant sons were furious. When bein
2 CONC g told that their father had said,"How the young cubs would rage when they learned of the old bear's fate,"they vowed instant revenge. An expedition led by eight crowned headsinvaded England. In a battle at York the Anglo-Saxons w
2 CONC ere entirelyrouted and King Aella was made prisoner and subjected to the mostbarbarous treatment. The sons of Lodbrok ordered an eagle to be cut onthe fleshy part of his back, the ribs were severed from the spine and theheart and 
2 CONC lungs extracted. After this victory Northumbria appears nolonger as a Saxon kingdom. Ivor was made King of Northumbria and Danishmerchants and people flocked to England. Bjorn, another brother, was madeKing of Sweden. Gotefrid bec
2 CONC ame King of Jutland. Another son, Sigurd,called the Snake Eye, inherited the Danish crown.
1 NOTE --Other Fields
1 NOTE REFN: 5773 See Snorre's Saga and the Icelandic Landnamobo k (Book of Sett l ment). Many historians regard much of the genealogy at this point to be purely legendary, or even mythical.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lodbrok means "of the shaggy bones"
1 NOTE [Royal Families from timmychew File.FTW]
2 CONT [Pullen010502.FTW]
2 CONT See Snorre's Saga and the Icelandic Landnamobok (Book of Settlment).
2 CONT "Greatest of all Vikings" (Makers of England, Arthur Bryant, p. 95, Doubleday & Comp., 1962)
2 CONT "Royalty for Commoners", Roderick W. Stuart, 1992, 2nd edition.
2 CONT "Pedigrees of the County Families of Yorkshire", Foster, 1874, 2 vols. vol1: West Riding, vol2: North and East Ridings
2 CONT "Ancestral roots of certain American colonists who came to America before 1700", Weis, 1992, seventh edition.
2 CONT Was RAGNAR LOTHBROK historical?
2 CONT One of the things that makes this a difficult question to discuss is
2 CONT that the question "Was Ragnar Lothbrok historical?"  is itself
2 CONT somewhat ambiguous.  Thus, before the question can be discussed, the
2 CONT question has to first be more clearly defined.  To mention two
2 CONT opposite extremes, a skeptic could ask whether or not everything which
2 CONT is said about the character of Ragnar Lothbrok is historically
2 CONT accurate, observe that the answer is certainly "no", and then claim
2 CONT victory.  At the other extreme, a proponent of a historical Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok could ask if a Viking by the name of Ragnar ever existed,
2 CONT point out that a Viking having the correct name ("Reginheri") appears
2 CONT in the Frankish annals, and  claim that Ragnar Lothbrok was therefore
2 CONT historical.  Neither of these two extremes is acceptable in a serious
2 CONT argument on the subject, so I will discuss the subject from the
2 CONT following middle ground.  The criteria which I will use are that in
2 CONT order for Ragnar Lothbrok to be considered as historical, there should
2 CONT be a historically documented person of that name who actually
2 CONT performed a significant number of the deeds attributed to the
2 CONT legendary Ragnar Lothbrok.  I think these are reasonable criteria, and
2 CONT the  remainder of this discussion is based on these principles.  Now,
2 CONT to answer the question:  No, Ragnar Lothbrok does not appear to be a
2 CONT historical figure, based on the above criteria.  I will give some
2 CONT comments as to why I have this opinion, and then mention some reading
2 CONT material for those who want more.
2 CONT RAGNAR
2 CONT The contemporary historical records of the ninth century (when Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok supposedly lived) show only one Viking of the correct name, a
2 CONT Viking named "Reginheri" (a Latin form equivalent to the name Ragnar)
2 CONT in France WHO DIED Indiana THE YEAR 845, according to the contemporary
2 CONT Frankish annals.  The emphasized words in the previous sentence are
2 CONT often conveninetly overlooked by those who wish to use Reginheri as a
2 CONT historical prototype for Ragnar Lothbrok.  Since Reginheri died in
2 CONT France in the year  845, he cannot have participated in the later
2 CONT events which form the principal part of the legendary Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok's exploits.  In addition, there is no good evidence that
2 CONT Reginheri was the father of any of the individuals who later came to
2 CONT be regarded as sons of Ragnar Lothbrok.  Thus, Reginheri fails to
2 CONT satisfy the criterion mentioned above.  No other historical Norseman
2 CONT named Ragnar is known for the appropriate time period.
2 CONT LOTHBROK
2 CONT No contemporary record gives this name, and it is significant that
2 CONT when the name finally does make it appearance in the records 200 years
2 CONT later, it stands alone.  (Ari, writing in the twelfth century, was the
2 CONT first known writer to make Ragnar and Lothbrok the same person.)  The
2 CONT name first appears (as "Lothbroc") in "Gesta Normannorum Ducum", by
2 CONT William of Jumieges, writing about 1070, in which Lothbroc is called
2 CONT he father of Bjorn Ironside.  (A Viking named Bjorn is verified by the
2 CONT contemporary chronicles, but without the nickname.)  Adam of Bremen,
2 CONT writing soon afterward, called Ivar the son of "Lodparchus".  Besides
2 CONT the fact that this Lothbrok is not attested in any of the contemporary
2 CONT sources, there seems to be another problem, and that is that the name
2 CONT ("Lothbroka") appears to be a women's name.  See the article on
2 CONT Ragnars saga" by Rory McTurk in "Medieval Scandinavia:  an
2 CONT encyclopedia" (New York and London, 1993).  If this argument based on
2 CONT philology is correct, then this Lothbrok(a), if historical at all,
2 CONT would be a women, and clearly not identical with the legendary Ragnarr
2 CONT Lothbrok.  (I do not have the background in linguistics to comment
2 CONT further on this gender argument.)
2 CONT RAGNALL
2 CONT The "Fragmentary Annals of Ireland" (edited and translated by Joan N.
2 CONT Radner, Dublin, 1978, formerly called "Three Fragments") has an item
2 CONT of interest which has frequently been pointed out as possibly relating
2 CONT to the legend of Ragnar Lothbrok.  In it, a certain Ragnall (Rognvald)
2 CONT son of Alpdan (Halfdan), king of Norway, is mentioned, and his
2 CONT exploits prior to the fall of York to the Danes are given, in a
2 CONT context in which it is at least arguable that Ragnall and Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok were the same person.  There are two problem with this
2 CONT interpretation.  First, Ragnar and Ragnall are not the same name, even
2 CONT though they look similar.  Second, and more important, the Fragmentary
2 CONT Annals are themselves not a contemporary source, and there is good
2 CONT reason to be suspicious about them.  However, even if we were to allow
2 CONT that the events given there are historical (a concession which many
2 CONT historians would be unwilling to make), and then concede further that
2 CONT these events form the basis of the Ragnar legend, then we would still
2 CONT have that the person on whom the legend was based did not have the
2 CONT right name.
2 CONT Could RAGNALL and LOTHBROK have been the same person?
2 CONT We have already seen that the only historically attested Ragnar
2 CONT (Reginheri) cannot reasonably be regarded as a historical prototype
2 CONT for Ragnar Lothbrok.  Thus, it appears that the best attempt to argue
2 CONT for a historical Ragnar Lothbrok is to propose (as has been done on
2 CONT numerous occasions) that Ragnall and Lothbrok were both the same
2 CONT person, and then assume that the similar (but different) names Ragnall
2 CONT and Ragnar were accidently confused.  Thus, let us see what
2 CONT assumptions are needed in order to assume that Ragnall and Lothbrok
2 CONT were the same person, assuming that they existed at all.  In order for
2 CONT this to be the case, we must make the following assumptions:
2 CONT (1)  We must assume that Adam of Bremen (late eleventh century) was
2 CONT correct in giving "Lodparchus" (i.e., Lothbrok) as the name of the
2 CONT father of Ivar (late ninth century).
2 CONT (2)  We must assume that the "Coghad Gaedhel re Gallaibh" ("The War of
2 CONT the Gaedhil with the Gaill", ed. by Todd, London, 1867), a twelfth
2 CONT century Irish source, is correct in stating that Halfdan of Dublin
2 CONT (killed in Ireland in 877, according to the Annals of Ulster) was the
2 CONT son of a certain Ragnall, and that this Ragnall was the same as the
2 CONT Ragnall who appears in the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland.
2 CONT (3)  We must assume that the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle is correct in
2 CONT stating that a brother (unnamed, but called Ubbe in later sources) of
2 CONT Halfdan and Ivar was killed in England in 878, despite the
2 CONT contradictory testimony of Aethelweard which gives a very different
2 CONT reading for the same event (see 4).
2 CONT (4)  We must assume that the chronicle of Aethelweard is wrong in
2 CONT stating that Halfdan brother of Ivar was killed in England in 878, for
2 CONT otherwise that would prove that Halfdan of Dublin (d. 877 in Ireland)
2 CONT was a different person from Halfdan brother of Ivar.
2 CONT (5)  In addition to assuming that Halfdan of Dublin was the same
2 CONT person as Halfdan brother of Ivar, we must also assume that this Ivar
2 CONT was the same person as Adam of Bremen's Ivar, keeping in mind that
2 CONT Aethelweard's chronicle, if correct, would imply the existence of two
2 CONT Ivars in the British isles at this time.
2 CONT (6)  We must assume that the philological argument making Lothbrok(a)
2 CONT a feminine name is incorrect.
2 CONT (7)  If Ari, the earliest author to mention Ragnar Lothbrok, is to be
2 CONT considered a reliable source on this matter, then we must also assume
2 CONT that Halfdan of Dublin was the same person as the Halfdan brother of
2 CONT Sigifrid who appears in the Annals of Fulda for the year 873, despite
2 CONT the severe chronological problems which that would cause with Ari's
2 CONT genealogies.
2 CONT Of the above assumptions, numbers (1) through (6) are crucial if one
2 CONT wishes to argue that Ragnall and Lothbrok were the same, and (7) is
2 CONT needed also if it is to be assumed that the information given by Ari
2 CONT is accurate.  Given the noncontemporary nature of the first two items,
2 CONT along with the contradictions present some of the others, there is a
2 CONT very small chance that all six of the crucial assumptions are correct.
2 CONT However, if any one of the first six items is false, then the case for
2 CONT Ragnall being the same as Lothbrok collapses, and we must conclude
2 CONT that the "Ragnall Lothbrok" attempt for a historical Ragnar Lothbrok
2 CONT is unsatisfactory.  [Note:  See R. W. McTurk's article "Ragnarr
2 CONT Lothbrok in the Irish Annals?" (Proceedings of the Seventh Viking
2 CONT Congress, 1976, pp. 93-123), where a different, but much more rigid,
2 CONT list of the same type is given.]
2 CONT CONCLUSIONS
2 CONT Since all of the above attempts to find a historical Ragnar Lothbrok
2 CONT fail to satisfy the mentioned criteria, Lothbrok and Ragnall come from
2 CONT noncontemporary sources which are themselves open to suspicion, and
2 CONT the historical records show nobody else (as far as I know) who could
2 CONT be plausibly identified with Ragnar Lothbrok, it must be concluded
2 CONT that Ragnar Lothbrok is not historical according to the terms
2 CONT described above.  In fact, if there is any historical basis to Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok legend, it is quite likely that Ragnar Lothbrok is the result
2 CONT of combining two or more distinct individuals into a single character
2 CONT having the attributes of both, in much the same way as Ragnar
2 CONT Lothbrok's legendary "father" Sigurd Ring is in fact a composite of
2 CONT two different men who fought against each other for the Danish throne
2 CONT in the year 814, Sigifridus ("Sigurd") and Anulo (of which "Ring" is a
2 CONT translation of Latin "Annulus").  However, such composite characters
2 CONT cannot be considered as historical, and there is no evidence which
2 CONT comes close to being contemporary which shows that either Lothbrok or
2 CONT Ragnall existed.
2 CONT FURTHER READING
2 CONT The most ambitious attempt to portray Ragnar Lothbrok as a historical
2 CONT figure is "Scandinavian Kings in the British Isles 850-880" by Alfred
2 CONT P. Smyth (Oxford University Press, 1977).  For a very critical
2 CONT examination of Smyth's views, see "High-kings, Vikings and other
2 CONT kings", by Donnchadh O' Corrain, in Irish Historical Review, vol 21
2 CONT (1979), pp. 283-323 (very highly recommended).  Both of these sources
2 CONT cite numerous other relevant  sources for those who are interested in
2 CONT further details.
2 CONT [Note:  The usual apologies if my transliterations from the Old Norse
2 CONT alphabet into the alphabet available to me is a bit sloppy.]
2 CONT Stewart Baldwin
