1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dag_den_vise Dag den vise (isl. Dagr hinn spaki) eller Dagr Spaka, svensk sagokung. Enligt Heimskringla var han av Ynglingaätten och kung av Svitjod. Han var son till Dyggve och sades k
2 CONC unna förstå fågelsång. Därför hade han en tam sparv som flög vida omkring och kom tillbaka med nyheter.
2 CONT
2 CONT En dag flög den dock till Reidgotaland där den blev dödad medan den pickade säd i ett fält. Dag bestämde sig för att hämnas sin fågel och angrep Varra i Reidgotaland. När han skulle vända åter blev han dock dödad av en träl som sl
2 CONC ängde en hötjuga i hans rygg.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dag_the_Wise Dag the Wise or Dagr Spaka (2nd or 3rd century AD) was a mythological Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. He was the son of Dyggvi, the former king. According to legend, he could unders
2 CONC tand the speech of birds and had a sparrow that gathered news for him from many lands. When the bird was killed on one of these trips, Dag invaded Reidgotaland (considering the date and location, apparently Gothiscandza), in order
2 CONC  to avenge it. There he was ambushed by a thrall and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest two versions based on Ynglingatal, i.e. Historia Norwegiæ and Íslendingabók (see below) say that Dag was succeeded by his son Alrekr and Eírikr who in their turn were succeeded by Dag's grandson Agne (in Historia Norw
2 CONC egiæ incorrectly called Hogne[1]):
2 CONT
2 CONT Historia Norwegiæ:
2 CONT
2 CONT Cui [Dyggui] successit in regnum filius ejus Dagr, quem Dani in quodam vado, quod Sciotanvath vel Wapnavath dicitur, dum passeris injurias vindicare conaretur, publico bello occiderunt. Qui genuit Alrik; hunc frater suus Erikr fre
2 CONC no percussit ad mortem. Alricr autem genuit Hogna[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT His [Dyggve's] son Dag succeeded to his throne; he was killed by the Danes in a royal battle at a ford named Skjotansvad, while he was trying to avenge the violence done to a sparrow. This man engendered Alrek, who was beaten to d
2 CONC eath with a bridle by his brother, Eirik. Alrek was father to Agne, [...][3] 	
2 CONT Íslendingabók only lists the line of succession: x Dyggvi. xi Dagr. xii Alrekr. xiii Agni. xiiii Yngvi''[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT However, in the Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson gives Agne as Dag's son and successor, and the two brothers Alrekr and Eiríkr as his grandsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is what Snorri tells of Dag:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagr hét son Dyggva konungs, er konungdóm tók eptir hann; hann var maðr svá spakr, at hann skildi fugls rödd. Hann átti spörr einn, er honum sagði mörg tíðindi; flaug hann á ymsi lönd. Þat var eitt sinn, at spörrinn flaug á Reiðgo
2 CONC taland, á bœ þann, er á Vörva heitir; hann flaug í akr karls ok fékk þar matar. Karl kom þar ok tók upp stein ok laust spörrinn til bana. Dagr konungr varð illa við, er spörrinn kom eigi heim; gékk hann þá til sónarblóts til frétt
2 CONC ar, ok fékk þau svör, at spörr hans var drepinn á Vörva. Síðan bauð hann út her miklum ok fór til Gotlands; en er hann kom á Vörva, gékk hann upp með her sinn ok herjaði: fólkit flýði víðs vegar undan. Dagr konungr sneri herinum t
2 CONC il skipa, er kveldaði, ok hafði hann drepit mart fólk ok mart handtekit. En er þeir fóru yfir á nökkura, þar sem heitir Skjótansvað eða Vápnavað, þá rann fram ór skógi einn verkþræll á árbakkann ok skaut heytjúgu í lið þeirra, ok 
2 CONC kom í höfuð konungi skotit; féll hann þegar af hestinum ok fékk bana. Í þann tíma var sá höfðingi gramr kallaðr er herjaði, en hermennirnir gramir.[5] 		 King Dygve's son, called Dag, succeeded to him, and was so wise a man that h
2 CONC e understood the language of birds. He had a sparrow which told him much news, and flew to different countries. Once the sparrow flew to Reidgotaland, to a farm called Varva, where he flew into the peasant's corn-field and took hi
2 CONC s grain. The peasant came up, took a stone, and killed the sparrow. King Dag was ill-pleased that the sparrow did not come home; and as he, in a sacrifice of expiation, inquired after the sparrow, he got the answer that it was kil
2 CONC led at Varva. Thereupon he ordered a great army, and went to Gotland; and when he came to Varva he landed with his men and plundered, and the people fled away before him. King Dag returned in the evening to his ships, after having
2 CONC  killed many people and taken many prisoners. As they were going across a river at a place called Skjotan's [the Weapon's] Ford, a labouring thrall came running to the river-side, and threw a hay- fork into their troop. It struck 
2 CONC the king on the head, so that he fell instantly from his horse and died. In those times the chief who ravaged a country was called Gram, and the men-at-arms under him Gramer.[6][7] 	
2 CONT Then Snorri quoted Ynglingatal (9th century):
2 CONT
2 CONT     Frák at Dagr
2 CONT     dauða orði
2 CONT     frægðar fúss
2 CONT     of fara skyldi,
2 CONT     þá er valteins
2 CONT     til Vörva kom
2 CONT     spakfrömuðr
2 CONT     spörs at hefna.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ok þat orð
2 CONT     á austrvega
2 CONT     vísa ferð
2 CONT     frá vígi bar,
2 CONT     at þann gram
2 CONT     af geta skyldi
2 CONT     slöngviþref
2 CONT     Sleipnis verðar.[8][9]
2 CONT
2 CONT     What news is this that the king's men,
2 CONT     Flying eastward through the glen,
2 CONT     Report? That Dag the Brave, whose name
2 CONT     Is sounded far and wide by Fame --
2 CONT     That Dag, who knew so well to wield
2 CONT     The battle-axe in bloody field,
2 CONT     Where brave men meet, no more will head
2 CONT     The brave – that mighty Dag is dead!
2 CONT
2 CONT     Varva was wasted with the sword,
2 CONT     And vengeance taken for the bird --
2 CONT     The little bird that used to bring
2 CONT     News to the ear of the great king.
2 CONT     Varva was ravaged, and the strife
2 CONT     Was ended, when the monarch's life
2 CONT     Was ended too – the great Dag fell
2 CONT     By the hay-fork of a base thrall![10][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that Skjótansvað/Vápnavað appear both in Ynglinga saga and in Historia Norwegiæ's earlier summary of Ynglingatal but not in Snorri's later quotation from it, suggests that all of Ynglingatal was not presented by him.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Konge i Uppsala (Sverige)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dag the Wise or Dagr Spaka (2nd or 3rd century AD) was a mythological Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. He was the son of Dyggvi, the former king. According to legend, he could understand the speech of birds and had a sparrow
2 CONC  that gathered news for him from many lands. When the bird was killed on one of these trips, Dag invaded Reidgotaland (considering the date and location, apparently Gothiscandza), in order to avenge it. There he was ambushed by a 
2 CONC thrall and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest two versions based on Ynglingatal, i.e. Historia Norwegiæ and Íslendingabók (see below) say that Dag was succeeded by his son Alrekr and Eírikr who in their turn were succeeded by Dag's grandson Agne (in Historia Norw
2 CONC egiæ incorrectly called Hogne[1]):
2 CONT
2 CONT His [Dyggve's] son Dag succeeded to his throne; he was killed by the Danes in a royal battle at a ford named Skjotansvad, while he was trying to avenge the violence done to a sparrow. This man engendered Alrek, who was beaten to d
2 CONC eath with a bridle by his brother, Eirik. Alrek was father to Agne, [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, in the Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson gives Agne as Dag's son and successor, and the two brothers Alrekr and Eiríkr as his grandsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is what Snorri tells of Dag: King Dygve's son, called Dag, succeeded to him, and was so wise a man that he understood the language of birds. He had a sparrow which told him much news, and flew to different countries. Once the
2 CONC  sparrow flew to Reidgotaland, to a farm called Varva, where he flew into the peasant's corn-field and took his grain. The peasant came up, took a stone, and killed the sparrow. King Dag was ill-pleased that the sparrow did not co
2 CONC me home; and as he, in a sacrifice of expiation, inquired after the sparrow, he got the answer that it was killed at Varva. Thereupon he ordered a great army, and went to Gotland; and when he came to Varva he landed with his men a
2 CONC nd plundered, and the people fled away before him. King Dag returned in the evening to his ships, after having killed many people and taken many prisoners. As they were going across a river at a place called Skjotan's [the Weapon'
2 CONC s] Ford, a labouring thrall came running to the river-side, and threw a hay- fork into their troop. It struck the king on the head, so that he fell instantly from his horse and died. In those times the chief who ravaged a country 
2 CONC was called Gram, and the men-at-arms under him Gramer.
2 CONT
