2 CONT    What news is this that the king's men,
2 CONT    Flying eastward through the glen,
2 CONT    Report? That Dag the Brave, whose name
2 CONT    Is sounded far and wide by Fame --
2 CONT    That Dag, who knew so well to wield
2 CONT    The battle-axe in bloody field,
2 CONT    Where brave men meet, no more will head
2 CONT    The brave – that mighty Dag is dead!
2 CONT
2 CONT    Varva was wasted with the sword,
2 CONT    And vengeance taken for the bird --
2 CONT    The little bird that used to bring
2 CONT    News to the ear of the great king.
2 CONT    Varva was ravaged, and the strife
2 CONT    Was ended, when the monarch's life
2 CONT    Was ended too – the great Dag fell
2 CONT    By the hay-fork of a base thrall![10][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that Skjótansvað/Vápnavað appear both in Ynglinga saga and in Historia Norwegiæ's earlier summary of Ynglingatal but not in Snorri's later quotation from it, suggests that all of Ynglingatal was not presented by him. <now
2 CONC iki>--------------------</nowiki> Konge i Uppsala (Sverige) <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Dag the Wise or Dagr Spaka (2nd or 3rd century AD) was a mythological Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. He was the son of Dyggv
2 CONC i, the former king. According to legend, he could understand the speech of birds and had a sparrow that gathered news for him from many lands. When the bird was killed on one of these trips, Dag invaded Reidgotaland (considering t
2 CONC he date and location, apparently Gothiscandza), in order to avenge it. There he was ambushed by a thrall and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest two versions based on Ynglingatal, i.e. Historia Norwegiæ and Íslendingabók (see below) say that Dag was succeeded by his son Alrekr and Eírikr who in their turn were succeeded by Dag's grandson Agne (in Historia Norw
2 CONC egiæ incorrectly called Hogne[1]):
2 CONT
2 CONT His [Dyggve's] son Dag succeeded to his throne; he was killed by the Danes in a royal battle at a ford named Skjotansvad, while he was trying to avenge the violence done to a sparrow. This man engendered Alrek, who was beaten to d
2 CONC eath with a bridle by his brother, Eirik. Alrek was father to Agne, [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, in the Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson gives Agne as Dag's son and successor, and the two brothers Alrekr and Eiríkr as his grandsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is what Snorri tells of Dag: King Dygve's son, called Dag, succeeded to him, and was so wise a man that he understood the language of birds. He had a sparrow which told him much news, and flew to different countries. Once the
2 CONC  sparrow flew to Reidgotaland, to a farm called Varva, where he flew into the peasant's corn-field and took his grain. The peasant came up, took a stone, and killed the sparrow. King Dag was ill-pleased that the sparrow did not co
2 CONC me home; and as he, in a sacrifice of expiation, inquired after the sparrow, he got the answer that it was killed at Varva. Thereupon he ordered a great army, and went to Gotland; and when he came to Varva he landed with his men a
2 CONC nd plundered, and the people fled away before him. King Dag returned in the evening to his ships, after having killed many people and taken many prisoners. As they were going across a river at a place called Skjotan's [the Weapon'
2 CONC s] Ford, a labouring thrall came running to the river-side, and threw a hay- fork into their troop. It struck the king on the head, so that he fell instantly from his horse and died. In those times the chief who ravaged a country 
2 CONC was called Gram, and the men-at-arms under him Gramer.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dag_the_Wise <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.artursson.se/0004/4353.htm <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> 21. OF DAG THE WISE.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Dygve's son, called Dag, succeeded to him, and was so wise a man that he understood the language of birds. He had a sparrow which told him much news, and flew to different countries. Once the sparrow flew to Reidgotaland, to 
2 CONC a farm called Varva, where he flew into the peasant's corn-field and took his grain. The peasant came up, took a stone, and killed the sparrow. King Dag was ill-pleased that the sparrow did not come home; and as he, in a sacrifice
2 CONC  of expiation, inquired after the sparrow, he got the answer that it was killed at Varva. Thereupon he ordered a great army, and went to Gotland; and when he came to Varva he landed with his men and plundered, and the people fled 
2 CONC away before him. King Dag returned in the evening to his ships, after having killed many people and taken many prisoners. As they were going across a river at a place called Skjotan's [the Weapon's] Ford, a labouring thrall came r
2 CONC unning to the river-side, and threw a hay- fork into their troop. It struck the king on the head, so that he fell instantly from his horse and died. In those times the chief who ravaged a country was called Gram, and the men-at-ar
2 CONC ms under him Gramer. Thjodolf sings of it thus: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "What news is this that the king's men, Flying eastward through the glen, Report? That Dag the Brave, whose name Is sounded far and wide by Fame -- That Dag, who knew so well to wield The battle-axe in bloody field, Where brave me
2 CONC n meet, no more will head The brave -- that mighty Dag is dead!
2 CONT
2 CONT "Varva was wasted with the sword, And vengeance taken for the bird -- The little bird that used to bring News to the ear of the great king. Varva was ravaged, and the strife Was ended, when the monarch's life Was ended too -- the 
2 CONC great Dag fell By the hay-fork of a base thrall!"
