2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dag_the_Wise <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dag_the_Wise
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dag the Wise or Dagr Spaka (2nd or 3rd century AD) was a mythological Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. He was the son of Dyggvi, the former king. According to legend, he could understand the speech of birds and had a sparrow
2 CONC  that gathered news for him from many lands. When the bird was killed on one of these trips, Dag invaded Reidgotaland (considering the date and location, apparently Gothiscandza), in order to avenge it. There he was ambushed by a 
2 CONC thrall and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest two versions based on Ynglingatal, i.e. Historia Norwegiæ and Íslendingabók (see below) say that Dag was succeeded by his son Alrekr and Eírikr who in their turn were succeeded by Dag's grandson Agne (in Historia Norw
2 CONC egiæ incorrectly called Hogne[1]):
2 CONT
2 CONT Historia Norwegiæ:
2 CONT
2 CONT Cui [Dyggui] successit in regnum filius ejus Dagr, quem Dani in quodam vado, quod Sciotanvath vel Wapnavath dicitur, dum passeris injurias vindicare conaretur, publico bello occiderunt. Qui genuit Alrik; hunc frater suus Erikr fre
2 CONC no percussit ad mortem. Alricr autem genuit Hogna[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT  His [Dyggve's] son Dag succeeded to his throne; he was killed by the Danes in a royal battle at a ford named Skjotansvad, while he was trying to avenge the violence done to a sparrow. This man engendered Alrek, who was beaten to 
2 CONC death with a bridle by his brother, Eirik. Alrek was father to Agne, [...][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Íslendingabók only lists the line of succession: x Dyggvi. xi Dagr. xii Alrekr. xiii Agni. xiiii Yngvi''[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT However, in the Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson gives Agne as Dag's son and successor, and the two brothers Alrekr and Eiríkr as his grandsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is what Snorri tells of Dag:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dagr hét son Dyggva konungs, er konungdóm tók eptir hann; hann var maðr svá spakr, at hann skildi fugls rödd. Hann átti spörr einn, er honum sagði mörg tíðindi; flaug hann á ymsi lönd. Þat var eitt sinn, at spörrinn flaug á Reiðgo
2 CONC taland, á bœ þann, er á Vörva heitir; hann flaug í akr karls ok fékk þar matar. Karl kom þar ok tók upp stein ok laust spörrinn til bana. Dagr konungr varð illa við, er spörrinn kom eigi heim; gékk hann þá til sónarblóts til frétt
2 CONC ar, ok fékk þau svör, at spörr hans var drepinn á Vörva. Síðan bauð hann út her miklum ok fór til Gotlands; en er hann kom á Vörva, gékk hann upp með her sinn ok herjaði: fólkit flýði víðs vegar undan. Dagr konungr sneri herinum t
2 CONC il skipa, er kveldaði, ok hafði hann drepit mart fólk ok mart handtekit. En er þeir fóru yfir á nökkura, þar sem heitir Skjótansvað eða Vápnavað, þá rann fram ór skógi einn verkþræll á árbakkann ok skaut heytjúgu í lið þeirra, ok 
2 CONC kom í höfuð konungi skotit; féll hann þegar af hestinum ok fékk bana. Í þann tíma var sá höfðingi gramr kallaðr er herjaði, en hermennirnir gramir.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT   King Dygve's son, called Dag, succeeded to him, and was so wise a man that he understood the language of birds. He had a sparrow which told him much news, and flew to different countries. Once the sparrow flew to Reidgotaland, t
2 CONC o a farm called Varva, where he flew into the peasant's corn-field and took his grain. The peasant came up, took a stone, and killed the sparrow. King Dag was ill-pleased that the sparrow did not come home; and as he, in a sacrifi
2 CONC ce of expiation, inquired after the sparrow, he got the answer that it was killed at Varva. Thereupon he ordered a great army, and went to Gotland; and when he came to Varva he landed with his men and plundered, and the people fle
2 CONC d away before him. King Dag returned in the evening to his ships, after having killed many people and taken many prisoners. As they were going across a river at a place called Skjotan's [the Weapon's] Ford, a labouring thrall came
2 CONC  running to the river-side, and threw a hay- fork into their troop. It struck the king on the head, so that he fell instantly from his horse and died. In those times the chief who ravaged a country was called Gram, and the men-at-
2 CONC arms under him Gramer.[6][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Then Snorri quoted Ynglingatal (9th century):
2 CONT
2 CONT Frák at Dagr
2 CONT
2 CONT dauða orði
2 CONT
2 CONT frægðar fúss
2 CONT
2 CONT of fara skyldi,
2 CONT
2 CONT þá er valteins
2 CONT
2 CONT til Vörva kom
2 CONT
2 CONT spakfrömuðr
2 CONT
2 CONT spörs at hefna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ok þat orð
2 CONT
2 CONT á austrvega
2 CONT
2 CONT vísa ferð
2 CONT
2 CONT frá vígi bar,
2 CONT
2 CONT at þann gram
2 CONT
2 CONT af geta skyldi
2 CONT
2 CONT slöngviþref
2 CONT
2 CONT Sleipnis verðar.[5][8] What news is this that the king's men,
2 CONT
2 CONT Flying eastward through the glen,
2 CONT
2 CONT Report? That Dag the Brave, whose name
2 CONT
2 CONT Is sounded far and wide by Fame --
2 CONT
2 CONT That Dag, who knew so well to wield
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle-axe in bloody field,
2 CONT
2 CONT Where brave men meet, no more will head
2 CONT
2 CONT The brave – that mighty Dag is dead!
2 CONT
2 CONT Varva was wasted with the sword,
2 CONT
2 CONT And vengeance taken for the bird --
2 CONT
2 CONT The little bird that used to bring
2 CONT
2 CONT News to the ear of the great king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Varva was ravaged, and the strife
2 CONT
2 CONT Was ended, when the monarch's life
2 CONT
2 CONT Was ended too – the great Dag fell
2 CONT
2 CONT By the hay-fork of a base thrall![6][9]  
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that Skjótansvað/Vápnavað appear both in Ynglinga saga and in Historia Norwegiæ's earlier summary of Ynglingatal but not in Snorri's later quotation from it, suggests that all of Ynglingatal was not presented by him.
