2 CONT
2 CONT Halvdan Øysteinsson den gavmilde og matille (født ca. 750 død ca. 800) var ifølge sagaene konge over Vestfold og Romerike. Av ynglingeætten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Foreldre: Øystein Halvdansson og Hild Eriksdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT Gift med: Liv Dagsdotter, Helga Dagsdotter, datter av Dag Frode
2 CONT
2 CONT Barn: Gudrød Halvdansson Veidekonge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halvdan var kjent som en stor kriger og han vant store rikdommer på sine ferder. Han lønnet mennene sine bra, og var kjent for sin gavmildhet. Døde sottedød, ble hauglagt i Borre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre skriver;
2 CONT
2 CONT Så sier Tjodolv:
2 CONT
2 CONT Og til møte Lokes møy tredje konge fra livet kalte,
2 CONT
2 CONT da Halvdan, Holtanbonden,
2 CONT
2 CONT hadde nytt det norner bød.
2 CONT
2 CONT Og seierens menn siden gjemte budlungen der i Borre..
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ble kalt "den gamilde" og "matille".
2 CONT
2 CONT 52. OF HALFDAN THE MILD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan was the name of King Eystein's son who succeeded him. He was called Halfdan the Mild, but the Bad Entertainer; that is to say, he was reported to be generous, and to give his men as much gold as other kings gave of silver,
2 CONC  but he starved them in their diet. He was a great warrior, who had been long on viking cruises, and had collected great property. He was married to Liv, a daughter of King Dag of Westmare. Holtar, in Westfold,
2 CONT
2 CONT was his chief house; and he died there on the bed of sickness, and was buried at Borre under a mound. So says Thjodolf: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "By Hel's summons, a great king
2 CONT
2 CONT Was called away to Odin's Thing:
2 CONT
2 CONT King Halfdan, he who dwelt of late
2 CONT
2 CONT At Holtar, must obey grim Fate.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Borre, in the royal mound,
2 CONT
2 CONT They laid the hero in the ground."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan the Mild was the son of king Öystein Halfdansson, of the House of Yngling and he succeeded his father as king, according to Heimskringla.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The Ynglinga Saga":
2 CONT
2 CONT "52. OF HALFDAN THE MILD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan was the name of King Eystein's son who succeeded him. He was called Halfdan the Mild, but the Bad Entertainer; that is to say, he was reported to be generous, and to give his men as much gold as other kings gave of silver,
2 CONC  but he starved them in their diet. He was a great warrior, who had been long on viking cruises, and had collected great property. He was married to Liv, a daughter of King Dag of Westmare. Holtar, in Westfold, was his chief house
2 CONC ; and he died there on the bed of sickness, and was buried at Borre under a mound. So says Thjodolf: --
2 CONT
2 CONT 'By Hel's summons, a great king
2 CONT
2 CONT Was called away to Odin's Thing:
2 CONT
2 CONT King Halfdan, he who dwelt of late
2 CONT
2 CONT At Holtar, must obey grim Fate.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Borre, in the royal mound,
2 CONT
2 CONT They laid the hero in the ground.' "
2 CONT
