1 NOTE Halfdan 
1 NOTE ---------- 
1 NOTE + 877 gefallen 
1 NOTE Mächtiger Führer der Wikinger innerhalb des dänischen „Großen Heeres“, 
2 CONT das seit Herbst 865 für 15 Jahre weite Teile der Britischen Inseln 
2 CONT beherrschte. Halfdan und seine Brüder Ivarr und Ubbe sollen Söhne des 
2 CONT historisch nicht faßbaren Sagenhelden Ragnarr lothbrok gewesen sein. 
1 NOTE Nedan från
1 NOTE http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halfdan_Ragnarsson
1 NOTE Halfdan was one of the sons of Ragnar Lodbrok. He pillaged with his 
2 CONT brothers in England in 855, and he was the ruler of London 871-872 
2 CONT where he had coins made.
1 NOTE In 875, he conquered Northumberland where he became king. He was 
2 CONT widely unpopular due to his alleged cruelty. He fell during a new 
2 CONT Viking expedition in 877.
1 NOTE References
1 NOTE  This article contains content from the Owl Edition of Nordisk 
2 CONT familjebok, a Swedish encyclopedia published between 1904-1926 now in 
2 CONT Public Domain.
1 NOTE Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halfdan_Ragnarsson"
1 NOTE Nedan från
1 NOTE http://www.northvegr.org/lore/history_viking/051.php
1 NOTE        Halfdan thereafter (875) set about the conquest of Bernicia, 
2 CONT whose inhabitants had rebelled against their prince, Ecgberht, a 
2 CONT vassal of the Danes, and Ricsig, another ruler appointed by the 
2 CONT foreigners; he ravaged the country north of the Tyne and west to 
2 CONT Carlisle, burnt and looted monastery after monastery, was the cause of 
2 CONT the bishop of Lindisfarne abandoning his see, and even penetrated into 
2 CONT the lands of the Picts and the Strathclyde Welsh. He did not succeed 
2 CONT in crushing all resistance from the independent Northumbrian princes 
2 CONT at Bamborough, but when at length he was satisfied that little or no 
2 CONT danger was to be anticipated from the north, he returned to Deira, and 
2 CONT in 876 began the initial organization of what was clearly intended to 
2 CONT be a stable and law-abiding colony. His first action was to portion 
2 CONT out the province of Deira among his followers, and he made these new 
2 CONT Danish masters of the land responsible for the continued cultivation 
2 CONT of their holdings. In the next year he added to the territory thus 
2 CONT under direct Danish ownership a large tract of Mercia, namely that 
2 CONT part of the kingdom east of a boundary extending from the Peak 
2 CONT district in Derbyshire to Tamworth in Warwickshire and then south-east 
2 CONT along the Watling Street as far as the northern boundary of 
2 CONT Northamptonshire. Here, as in Deira, Danish ownership of the land, and 
2 CONT Danish law and social organization, were imposed on the English, but 
2 CONT there was also an important political reorganization of the district, 
2 CONT for this Mercian territory was divided into five portions, each under 
2 CONT a jarl who was instructed to maintain an army for the defence of his 
2 CONT area, and each having as its centre a specially fortified town that 
2 CONT was known as a borough (OE. burh = fortified place). These viking 
2 CONT headquarters were the towns of Lincoln, Stamford, Nottingham, Derby, 
2 CONT and Leicester, and together they form a famous historical group giving 
2 CONT the name of the 'Five Boroughs' to these lands in Danish Mercia and 
2 CONT Lindsey.' 
1 NOTE Chef de tribut Viking, coureur des mers, tué au combat à Strangford Lough en 877 par un Viking Norvégien
1 NOTE The large army of Danes that in 866 established itself in East Anglia 
2 CONT was, according to Scandinavian tradition, a force made up of the 
2 CONT followings of the three sons of Ragnar Lodbrok (p. 203 ), namely 
2 CONT Halfdan, Ivar, who had arrived from Ireland, (p. 279 ), and Ubbe; and 
2 CONT a further tradition, though it is scarcely to be believed, tells that 
2 CONT they had come to avenge the death of their father, who is said to have 
2 CONT been killed in an earlier raid on Northumbria. That these three chiefs 
2 CONT were indeed the sons of Ragnar is probable enough, but as to the 
2 CONT motive that inspired this great invasion it is more likely that they 
2 CONT came with the plain intention of winning land for themselves and their 
2 CONT men. For this is certainly what they set themselves to do. The first 
2 CONT action of the Danes after they had made themselves secure in East 
2 CONT Anglia was to obtain horses, and when they were properly equipped as 
2 CONT cavalry for a cross-country expedition, they made a circuit round the 
2 CONT fens and then moved northwards across Lindsey into the province of 
2 CONT Deira. As usual in this period of her political and military decline, 
2 CONT Northumbria was in a state of civil war, and in 867 the Danes captured 
2 CONT York without meeting any serious opposition. The two rival kings, Ælle 
2 CONT and Osbeorht, were now uncomfortably aware that there would be no 
2 CONT English kingdom of Northumbria for them to fight for if they did not 
2 CONT at once make common cause against the invaders; so they patched up a 
2 CONT peace and marched off together to recapture York. But it was too late; 
2 CONT the attempt failed and both the English kings were killed in the 
2 CONT fighting.
1 NOTE        The Danes made York (Jórvik it was now called in place of the 
2 CONT English name Eoforwic) their stronghold, and soon all Deira lay under 
2 CONT their dominion. Nor was it long before they were in a position to 
2 CONT extend their conquests. Bernicia, to the north, did not attract them, 
2 CONT and so was allowed to remain for a time under the rule of its English 
2 CONT princes; but to the south-west lay the richer lands of Mercia, and 
2 CONT thither a large force of the Danes advanced. Their route took them 
2 CONT down the valley of the Trent to Nottingham, and seizing that town, 
2 CONT they remained there for the winter. In the meantime the king of 
2 CONT Mercia, Burhred, appealed to Æthelred, the fourth son of Æthelwulf and 
2 CONT now king of the West Saxons, for help, and the Wessex king, with his 
2 CONT brother Alfred, marched with an army to join Burhred in an attempt to 
2 CONT win back Nottingham. The English laid siege to the town, but there was 
2 CONT no pitched battle and in the end the Mercians bought a peace, the 
2 CONT viking host thereupon retiring to York. For a year the whole Danish 
2 CONT force remained in Deira, but late in 869 a Danish army rode south, 
2 CONT burning the fenland monasteries and plundering recklessly on all 
2 CONT sides, until it arrived at Thetford in East Anglia, where it made 
2 CONT preparations to pass the winter. There Edmund, King of East Anglia, 
2 CONT met them in battle, but the English force was decisively beaten, 
2 CONT Edmund himself being captured by Ivar the Boneless and Ubbe and, it is 
2 CONT said, cruelly put to death at Hoxne because he would not become the 
2 CONT vassal of a pagan. The exact circumstances of Edmund's death are 
2 CONT unknown and the familiar story of his martyrdom cannot rank as more 
2 CONT than legend, but there is no reason to doubt that some cruel and 
2 CONT cold-blooded act of Danish barbarism attended the death of this 
2 CONT unfortunate petty king; for, insignificant though he was, few events 
2 CONT in the whole of the history of the viking wars so profoundly impressed 
2 CONT a horror-struck Christendom. The memory of Edmund, henceforward Saint 
2 CONT and Martyr, was soon venerated not only by the vanquished Anglians, 
2 CONT but throughout all England; the stream of pilgrims to his shrine, the 
2 CONT jealous protection of his relics, and the very naming of him, 
2 CONT perpetuated the shame of his murder and made of Edmund's martyrdom a 
2 CONT constant reproach to the Danes, even to Cnut, the great Danish king 
2 CONT who ruled in England in the eleventh century.
1 NOTE        After the fall of Edmund, East Anglia had perforce to submit to 
2 CONT the heathen invaders, and now the greater part of the eastern plain of 
2 CONT England was held fast in the grip of the Danes. The time had come when 
2 CONT central and southern England was to tremble lest it shared a similar 
2 CONT fate. Halfdan and a chief by the name of Bagsecg were the commanders 
2 CONT when the Danes next advanced, this time to attack Wessex. Their object 
2 CONT was to surprise Æthelred by a winter attack, and it was late in 
2 CONT December of 870 when they marched into the Thames valley and descended 
2 CONT upon Reading, where they made for themselves a fortified camp at the 
2 CONT confluence of the Kennet and the Thames. In January of 871 there was a 
2 CONT skirmish with a small English force at Englefield, six miles west of 
2 CONT Reading, in which the Danes were worsted, but four days later when 
2 CONT King Æthelred and his brother Alfred together assailed the Danish 
2 CONT stronghold at Reading the English were beaten off. Another four days 
2 CONT passed, and then a second battle took place between Æthelred and 
2 CONT Alfred and the whole force of the Danes who were drawn up on the 
2 CONT slopes of Ashdown, (1) some 25 miles to the west of Reading. The 
2 CONT result of the battle was a big victory for the English, and Bagsecg 
2 CONT and many of the viking chieftains were slain; but Æthelred did not 
2 CONT follow up his success, and only a fortnight later the English were 
2 CONT beaten in a third encounter that took place at Basing in Hampshire. In 
2 CONT March there was yet another pitched battle between the two Wessex 
2 CONT princes and the Danes, this time in Wiltshire at the village of Marton 
2 CONT on the outskirts of Savernake Forest; for the greater part of the day 
2 CONT the English held the upper hand, but they could not put the enemy to 
2 CONT flight, and, after heavy slaughter on both sides, they were forced to 
2 CONT withdraw leaving the Danes in possession of the field. 
1 NOTE        In the spring of this year, and with the Danes pressing hard 
2 CONT upon his realm, Æthelred died. As the two children that he left were 
2 CONT only infants, the crown did not pass in the direct line to his eldest 
2 CONT son; instead, he was succeeded on the throne by one of the greatest 
2 CONT men England has ever produced, his brother Alfred. It was a situation 
2 CONT of the utmost gravity that faced the new king. Deira, Alfred knew, was 
2 CONT lost to the Danes; East Anglia was theirs; Mercia had only escaped for 
2 CONT the time by the humiliating purchase of a peace; and now the Danes had 
2 CONT already been four months in Wessex. At such a time and in such a 
2 CONT crisis even a powerful and experienced ruler might have wavered; but 
2 CONT Alfred the Great was only a youth of 23 years when he came to the 
2 CONT throne and a prince possessing neither rude strength nor robust 
2 CONT health. He had, however, already proved his courage. He had fought the 
2 CONT Danes; he knew the manner of their warfare; and his own people trusted 
2 CONT him. So the young king never faltered, but led his army straightway 
2 CONT against the pagan host. The first battle took place at Wilton; it was 
2 CONT hard fought, but as before at Marton, the English at first drove back 
2 CONT the Danes, only to be themselves repulsed at the end of the day. 
2 CONT Despite this failure, Alfred did not once relax his close watch upon 
2 CONT the movements of the enemy nor the determined opposition whereby he 
2 CONT countered them; in this year, 871, he fought no less than nine pitched 
2 CONT battles against the Danes, and at last the invaders, who were losing 
2 CONT many of their men in this constant fighting, realized that the 
2 CONT conquest of Wessex was not likely to be effected in the present 
2 CONT campaign. The result was that Alfred was able to make a peace with 
2 CONT Halfdan and the viking army withdrew across the Thames. For the time, 
2 CONT at any rate, Wessex was saved.
1 NOTE        But this freedom for Wessex was bought at the expense of 
2 CONT Mercia, and in the next year the Danes determined upon the conquest of 
2 CONT Burhred's kingdom. This prince, since he had been occupied in a 
2 CONT struggle with the Welsh, had not come to the assistance of Wessex in 
2 CONT the hour of its peril, and Alfred in his turn, either because of his 
2 CONT bond with the Danes or because the better organization of his own 
2 CONT kingdom demanded his full attention, left Mercia to its fate. 
2 CONT Halfdan's first move was to march with his vikings to London, where 
2 CONT they spent the winter 871-2, and while they remained there Alfred 
2 CONT posted a small observation force on his side of the Thames. But it was 
2 CONT Burhred who was compelled to take some action, for he could not permit 
2 CONT the Danes to hold this important Thames mart unchallenged, and as he 
2 CONT dare not attack Halfdan, he too made a peace with the enemy, ransoming 
2 CONT the town by the payment of a heavy tribute. The Danes thereupon 
2 CONT temporarily withdrew from south-eastern Mercia; but after a return to 
2 CONT Deira they came south again into Lindsey and spent the winter 872-3 at 
2 CONT Torksey on the Trent near Lincoln. Then began a terrible ravaging of 
2 CONT Mercia, and by the following winter the Danes had established 
2 CONT themselves in the heart of the kingdom at Repton, where they destroyed 
2 CONT the mausoleum of the Mercian kings. At the end of 874 the overthrow of 
2 CONT the wretched kingdom was finally completed. Leicester, Nottingham, 
2 CONT Lichfield, and Tamworth had fallen, (1) and Burhred had abandoned his 
2 CONT country to its fate. He fled to Rome, where he died soon afterwards, 
2 CONT and Halfdan set up in his stead on the Mercian throne a man called 
2 CONT Ceolwulf, an English thane who was instructed to govern the country on 
2 CONT behalf of the Danes as long as it so pleased them to entrust it to 
2 CONT him. Halfdan's army then retired from Repton, and in doing so divided, 
2 CONT one portion under Halfdan himself returning to Deira to winter on the 
2 CONT Tyne, while the other, commanded by Guthrum, Oscytel, and Anwind, went 
2 CONT to Cambridge. 
1 NOTE        Thus were the foundations of the Danish colony in England, the 
2 CONT Danelaw, (1) laid. But while Halfdan and the vikings of Deira and the 
2 CONT Five Boroughs were intent on the proper colonization of the important 
2 CONT territories that were now their own, Guthrum and the Danes who had 
2 CONT settled at Cambridge coveted a larger dominion than their present 
2 CONT holding in East Anglia. And so once more they set out upon the 
2 CONT conquest of Wessex. 
1 NOTE        Except that in 875 a tiny fleet of six viking ships had been 
2 CONT defeated in a sea-battle, the kingdom of Alfred the Great had been 
2 CONT left in peace for a space of five years. But now the troubles of 
2 CONT Wessex were to begin again, and in 876 a force described as the 
2 CONT 'western army', and probably composed of vikings from Ireland, landed 
2 CONT near Poole Harbour in Dorset. This was a move in a preconceived attack 
2 CONT of a very serious kind, for the Irish host was promptly joined by 
2 CONT Guthrum's army that had marched overland from Cambridge and that had 
2 CONT successfully evaded the Wessex force collected to oppose its progress. 
2 CONT Together the two bands of Northmen plundered the neighbourhood, and 
2 CONT finally they seized the town of Wareham, which they made their 
2 CONT headquarters. Alfred, although his army was mobilized, was not 
2 CONT prepared to undertake the proper siege of the place, and he deemed it 
2 CONT wisest to make a peace, the vikings giving hostages and swearing that 
2 CONT they would leave Wessex at once in return for a money-payment. But 
2 CONT Guthrum, in spite of the solemn nature of his oath, broke his word; 
2 CONT the Danish cavalry slipped out of Wareham by night and made its way 
2 CONT hastily not out of Alfred's kingdom, but to Exeter. At the same time 
2 CONT the viking fleet sailed down the coast with the intention of turning 
2 CONT up the mouth of the Exe to join the rest of the force. 
1 NOTE        It was plainly the intention of the vikings to ravage Devon, 
2 CONT but fortune was now (877) on Alfred's side. While the Wessex king, who 
2 CONT had marched at once in pursuit to Exeter, succeeded in keeping the 
2 CONT land-force shut up in the town, the fleet that was to bring this 
2 CONT Danish army reinforcements met with a terrible disaster off Swanage 
2 CONT where in a great tempest over 120 of its ships foundered. Guthrum in 
2 CONT Exeter had now no other course than to surrender, and Alfred, having 
2 CONT accepted hostages, allowed him and his army to escape into Mercia. 
2 CONT Possibly the Danes had promised to return to East Anglia; but if so, 
2 CONT they broke their word once more. For they settled down in Gloucester 
2 CONT close to the Wessex frontier. 
1 NOTE        With a viking host so near, it was idle to pretend that Wessex 
2 CONT was saved a second time, and, in truth, the hour of its direst peril 
2 CONT was at hand. In midwinter of 877-8 Guthrum suddenly struck with his 
2 CONT full force against Chippenham where Alfred had a royal residence and 
2 CONT where perhaps he was living for the Christmas season, and early in the 
2 CONT following year Halfdan's brother Ubbe arrived from Wales on the north 
2 CONT coast of Devon with a fleet of 23 ships and an army of 840 men. The 
2 CONT effect of this double stroke, made at a season when invasion was 
2 CONT little feared, was overwhelming. Alfred, indeed, escaped from 
2 CONT Guthrumn, but all organized resistance broke down and a terrible 
2 CONT devastation of Wessex began. So serious was the plight of the land 
2 CONT that many of the West Saxons fled across the Channel, and at last it 
2 CONT seemed a certainty that Wessex must fall within the Danelaw. All 
2 CONT England, so men thought, must now pass irrevocably into the hands of 
2 CONT the invaders.
1 NOTE        But the king remained in Wessex, and the king did not yet 
2 CONT despair. Escaping from Chippenham with a small personal following, he 
2 CONT collected a little army during his retreat into the Somerset marshes, 
2 CONT and finally, at Eastertide of 878 he established himself in a 
2 CONT stockaded fort on the island of Athelney. (1) Throughout the months of 
2 CONT his hiding in the marshes he had not ceased to consider how he might 
2 CONT best make a counter-attack upon the Danes, and during the seven weeks 
2 CONT that he was encamped at Athelney he worried the enemy with an 
2 CONT incessant guerilla-war and at the same time completed the difficult 
2 CONT preliminary arrangements for the plan he had in mind. Then came the 
2 CONT good news that the men of Devon, under their alderman Odda, had 
2 CONT defeated Ubbe's vikings who were laying siege to 'Cynwit', a fort 
2 CONT either in north Devon or in Somerset, (2) and had not only almost 
2 CONT annihilated the Danes but had slain their leader Ubbe himself. (3) 
1 NOTE -----------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT ---------
1 NOTE 1. It is difficult to see any connexion between the name Athelney and 
2 CONT Alfred's sojourn on the island; the old form is Æthelinga-ig, usually 
2 CONT translated 'isle of the princes', though the first element may 
2 CONT conceivably be the corrupted form of a proper name. The site of 
2 CONT Alfred's fort is supposed to be the Borough Mump, a lofty knoll with a 
2 CONT sham ruined church on its summit, about halfway between Othery and 
2 CONT Ling, and just north of the confluence of the Tone and the Parrett; it 
2 CONT is connected by a causeway with the high ground between Othery and 
2 CONT Middlezoy and is well placed as a centre for the military control of 
2 CONT the marshes, being readily convertible by means of a palisade into an 
2 CONT almost impregnable fortress. But as the knoll bears no certain traces 
2 CONT of artificial fortifications, and as the only archaeological material 
2 CONT from the site is some Roman pottery that was found at the foot of the 
2 CONT Mump in Borough Bridge, there must remain some doubt as to its 
2 CONT identification with Alfred's fort. The 'Isle of Athelney', a low and 
2 CONT elongated platform of land, lies about a mile to the southwest and is 
2 CONT assumed to be the site of the monastery subsequently founded by Alfred 
2 CONT as a thank-offering for his victories.         
1 NOTE 2. The site has not been satisfactorily identified, but 'Kenwith 
2 CONT Castle', an earthwork in the parish of Abbotsham near Bideford, Devon, 
2 CONT and the scarped knoll about one mile north-west of Cannington near 
2 CONT Bridgwater, Somerset, have both been suggested; in fact, Cannington 
2 CONT Knoll is now honoured on the current (1) inch O.S. map with the name 
2 CONT of Cynwit and the date of the battle. Both identifications are 
2 CONT valueless (in spite of the bones found in the Warren field at 
2 CONT Cannington), and a third suggestion that Cynwit is Countesbury, near 
2 CONT Lynton, can also be neglected (see W. H. Stevenson, Asser's Life of 
2 CONT Alfred, Oxford, 1904, p. 262).         
1 NOTE 3. This was the battle of the 'Raven Banner', a celebrated standard 
2 CONT that was said to have been woven by the daughters of Ragnar Lodbrok 
2 CONT between dawn and dusk in a single day; it had a raven blazoned upon it 
2 CONT that seemed to flutter if the army was advancing to victory, but 
2 CONT drooped and was still if defeat was in store for the vikings: Saxon 
2 CONT Chronicle, Laud MS. E., s. a. 876, Annals of St. Neots, ed. with 
2 CONT Asser's Alfred, W. H. Stevenson , Oxford, 1904, p. 138. For a 
2 CONT description of a Norse magical banner of the same kind, see 
2 CONT Flateyjarbók, Olaf Trygg., (1) 86).       
