1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''MARIE [Isabelle?] van Gistel Vrouw van Zwevegem (-> 1431)'''
2 CONT d/o  ROGER van Gistel Heer van Straeten &  Elisabeth [Margareta] Vrouw van Dudzele 
2 CONT x LOUIS bâtard de Flandre "Friese", illegitimate s/o LOUIS II Count of Flanders & NN de Borre (-killed in battle Nicopolis 28 Sep 1396)
2 CONT *JEAN [I] de Flandre (->10 Sep 1439) Heer van Praet en van der Woestijne x JOHANNA van Reigaarsvliet
2 CONT xx Jean d’Halluin Seigneur de Roosebeke, Burggraaf van Harelbeke
2 CONT *ROGER de Halluin (-1463) x m CATHERINE Vilain
2 CONT *[MARGUERITE d’Halluin (-after 25 Apr 1435) x  PIETER van Reigarsvliet Heer van Boelare(-1466).] 
2 CONT *[MARGUERITE] d’Halluin (-12 Apr 1470, bur Bouvignies) x JACQUES d’Ollehain Seigneur d’Estambourg, de Bouvignies et de Gondecourt(-6 Aug 1483, bur Bouvignies). 
2 CONT * GUILLEMETTE d’Halluin x  JEAN Seigneur de Léaucourt
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FLANDERS,%20HAINAUT.htm#LouisFlandrePraetdied1396
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/nfranord.htm#JeanHalluindied1425
2 CONT ----
2 CONT LOUIS bâtard de Flandre "Friese", illegitimate son of LOUIS II Count of Flanders & his mistress --- de Borre (-killed in battle Nicopolis 28 Sep 1396).  His mother is referred to in the following source: Vredius quotes a manuscrip
2 CONC t which records “messire Loys de Frise fils bastard de...Loys de Male conte de Flandre, lequel il eut d’une fille de Monsieur de Borre”[864].  Heer van Praet en van der Woestijne: Espinoy records that Louis II Count of Flanders “e
2 CONC n avancement de son mariage avec Dame Marie de Guistelles, Dame de Zweueghem et de Rosebeke” granted “les terres et Baronies de Praet et de la Woestine” to his illegitimate son “Messire Louys de Flandres dit le Frizon” (no source 
2 CONC cited)[865].  Vredius quotes a manuscript which records that Louis “eut en partage [la Wostine] par acte du 25 de septembre 1373” [date not confirmed] and died at Nikopolis 28 Sep 1396[866]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m as her first husband, MARIE [Isabelle?] van Gistel Vrouw van Zwevegem, daughter of ROGER van Gistel Heer van Straeten & his wife Elisabeth [Margareta] Vrouw van Dudzele (-after 1431).  Père Anselme records her parentage (nami
2 CONC ng her “Marie”) and two marriages (reversing their order) (no sources cited)[867].  As noted above under her husband, Espinoy names her “Dame Marie de Guistelles, Dame de Zweueghem et de Rosebeke”.  Vredius quotes a manuscript whi
2 CONC ch records that “messire Loys de Frise fils bastard de...Loys de Male conte de Flandre...” married “madame...de Ghistelles...fille de messire --- Seigneur de Dudzeele” who later married “--- de Halewijn”[868].  Vredius quotes anot
2 CONC her manuscript which records that Louis married “dame Marie de Guistelles fille de messire Rogier chevalier seign. de Dudzeele, Straten et de Dame Isabeau heretiere de Dudzeele”[869].  Her family origin is confirmed by the arms di
2 CONC splayed on the epitaph of her granddaughter (by her second marriage) Isabelle.  Some doubt about Marie’s name is introduced by the charter dated 25 Apr 1435 which names “Pierre de Boulers escuier”, married to “Margaretha van Halew
2 CONC ijn, de oudste dochter van Jan van Halewijn en Isabella van Gistel”[870].  No “Jean d’Halluin” married to “Isabella van Gistel” has been found.  If Marguerite’s father was Jean d’Halluin Seigneur de Roosebeke (the second husband o
2 CONC f “Marie”, see below), maybe his wife was named “Isabelle” not “Marie” (that name being recorded only in secondary sources as noted above).  An indication that this suggestion about Marguerite’s parentage is correct is that “Marie
2 CONC  van Gistel”’s son by her first husband married “Johanna van Reigaarsvliet” (see below), presumably a relative of Marguerite’s husband although the precise family relationship has not been established (see the document FLANDERS NO
2 CONC BILITY), as multiple marriages between different members of two noble families are often noted.  She married secondly Jean d’Halluin Seigneur de Roosebeke, Burggraaf van Harelbeke.  Espinoy records that “messire Jean de Flandre...
2 CONC avec...sa mere Dame de Zweueghem et de Roosebeke” settled the succession of “messire Louys son pere en son vivant Seigneur de Praet et de la Woestine” and settled her dower, recorded in a document dated 1431[871].  This document d
2 CONC oes not name “Marie/Isabelle” but it does provide another indication that she was the mother of Marguerite d’Halluin : her second husband died before 1431, indicating the need to settle her dower. 
2 CONT '''
2 CONT Louis & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         JEAN [I] de Flandre (-after 10 Sep 1439).  Heer van Praet en van der Woestijne.  Espinoy records that “messire Jean de Flandre...avec...sa mere Dame de Zweueghem et de Roosebeke” settled the succession of “messire Louys
2 CONC  son pere en son vivant Seigneur de Praet et de la Woestine” and settled her dower (no date), recorded in a document dated 1431[872].  “Ian van Vlaenderen Heere van Praet ande vander Woestine ende Burghemeesters ende Schepenen van
2 CONC den selven Heerschepe van Praet” issued a charter dated 10 Sep 1439[873].  m JOHANNA van Reigaarsvliet, daughter of HENDRIK van Reigaarsvliet & his wife --- van Schendelbeek.  Père Anselme records her parentage and marriage[874]. 
2 CONC  Vredius quotes a manuscript which records that “Iean de Flandre Seign. de Praet et de la Woestine” married “Ieanne de Reyghersvliet fille de Henry, fils de Gautier”[875].  No primary source has been found which confirms her paren
2 CONC tage and marriage, or her date of death.  Jean & his wife had five children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/FLANDERS,%20HAINAUT.htm#LouisFlandrePraetdied1396
2 CONT ----
2 CONT GAUTHIER d’Halluin (-[after 15 Dec 1391?]).  Seigneur de Roosebeke, Burggraaf van Harelbeke.  The anonymous author records that Gauthier “se trouva, avec son père, assiégé dans Audenaerde en 1379”, and predeceased his father (no s
2 CONC ources cited)[857].  The latter comment appears to be incorrect assuming that the following references all apply to this Gauthier.  A list of knights in Flanders dated 1384-86 names “mer Joos van Haelwine...mer Wouter van Haelwine
2 CONC  sheren zone...mer Wouter van Haelwine van Rosebeke...mer Wouter van Haelwine van Calone...mer Olivier van Haelwine...mer Jan van Haelwine...mer Willem van Haelwine...mer Gheraerd van Haelwine”[858].  “...Wautier sire de Halewin..
2 CONC .” signed the peace of Tournai 18 Dec 1385[859].  A charter dated 15 Dec 1391 names “heeren...mer Woutren heere van Aelwine...ruddren” and “Watiers sires de Hallewin et de Watervliet...chevaliers”[860].  m MARIA [Margareta?] van ‘
2 CONC s Grans, daughter of WILLEM van ‘s Grans Seigneur d’Inchove & his wife Ida van Steenhuize-Zwevegem (-1395).  “Gautier de Halewyn dit de Roosebeke chevalier, écoutète d’Harelbeke”, son of “Daniel de Halewyn chevalier seigneur de Ro
2 CONC osebeke”, married “Marguerite fille héritière de Jean ‘s Grans seigneur d’Inchove et d’Ide fille de Gérard de Steenhuyze chevalier seigneur de Zweveghem” (no sources cited)[861].  Père Anselme records her death in 1395 (no sources
2 CONC  cited)[862].  The Halewijn/’s Grans marriage is confirmed by a 1384 charter which names “joncvrauwe Marie sGrands uxor mer Wouters van Halewijn” as a fiefholder from Bruges, Buylaert noting that after her death it was held succes
2 CONC sively “Jan van Halewijn van Roesbeke...Rogier van Halewijn”[863].  Gauthier & his wife had children (Père Anselme records another child whose existence has not been verified[864]): 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         JEAN d’Halluin (-killed in battle 1425, bur Haarlem).  A 1384 charter which names “joncvrauwe Marie sGrands uxor mer Wouters van Halewijn” as a fiefholder from Bruges, Buylaert noting that after her death it was held su
2 CONC ccessively “Jan van Halewijn van Roesbeke...Rogier van Halewijn”[865].  Seigneur de Roosebeke, Burggraaf van Harelbeke.  Seigneur d’Inchove (inherited from his mother).  Heer van Zwevegem: “Gheeraert van Steenhuus her van Zweveghe
2 CONC m” [Gerard [III], Jean’s maternal relation, see the document FLANDERS NOBILITY] sold his “heerschip en seingerie van Zweveghem”, held from “de heer van Gistel”, with another fief “gheheeten de cossaterie”, to “Janne van Haelwine h
2 CONC eer van Roosbecque en Inchout” by charter dated 16 Mar 1414 (O.S.?), sealed by “Gerard van Steenhuse”[866].  Père Anselme records his death and place of burial (no sources cited)[867].  m (after 1396) as her second husband, MARIE 
2 CONC [Isabelle?] van Gistel Vrouw van Zwevegem, widow of LOU'''IS bâtard de Flandre Heer van Praet, daughter of ROGER van Gistel Heer van Straeten & his wife Elisabeth [Margareta] Vrouw van Dudzele (-after 1431).  Père Anselme records 
2 CONC her parentage (naming her “Marie”) and two marriages (reversing their order) (no sources cited)[868].  Espinoy records that Louis II Count of Flanders “en avancement de son mariage avec Dame Marie de Guistelles, Dame de Zweueghem 
2 CONC et de Rosebeke” granted “les terres et Baronies de Praet et de la Woestine” to his illegitimate son “Messire Louys de Flandres dit le Frizon” (no source cited)[869].  Vredius quotes a manuscript recording that “messire Loys de Fri
2 CONC se fils bastard de...Loys de Male conte de Flandre...” married “madame...de Ghistelles...fille de messire --- Seigneur de Dudzeele” who later married “--- de Halewijn”[870].  Vredius quotes another manuscript which records that Lo
2 CONC uis married “dame Marie de Guistelles fille de messire Rogier chevalier seign. de Dudzeele, Straten et de Dame Isabeau heretiere de Dudzeele”[871].  Her family origin is confirmed by the arms displayed on the epitaph of her grandd
2 CONC aughter (by her second marriage) Isabelle.  Some doubt about Marie’s name is introduced by the charter dated 25 Apr 1435 which names “Pierre de Boulers escuier”, married to “Margaretha van Halewijn, de oudste dochter van Jan van H
2 CONC alewijn en Isabella van Gistel”[872].  No “Jean d’Halluin” married to “Isabella van Gistel” has been found.  If Marguerite’s father was Jean d’Halluin Seigneur de Roosebeke, maybe his wife was named “isabelle” not “Marie” (that na
2 CONC me being recorded only in secondary sources as noted above).  An indication that this suggestion about Marguerite’s parentage is correct is that “Marie van Gistel”’s son by her first husband married “Johanna van Reigaarsvliet”, pr
2 CONC esumably a relative of Marguerite’s husband although the precise family relationship has not been established (see the document FLANDERS NOBILITY), as multiple marriages between different members of two noble families are often no
2 CONC ted.  Espinoy records that “messire Jean de Flandre...avec...sa mere Dame de Zweueghem et de Roosebeke” settled the succession of “messire Louys son pere en son vivant Seigneur de Praet et de la Woestine” and settled her dower, re
2 CONC corded in a document dated 1431[873]. This document does not name “Marie/Isabelle” but it does provide another indication that she was the mother of Marguerite d’Halluin : her second husband died before 1431, indicating the need t
2 CONC o settle her dower.  Jean & his wife had [four] children: 
2 CONT '''
2 CONT i)          ROGER de Halluin (-1463).  A 1384 charter which names “joncvrauwe Marie sGrands uxor mer Wouters van Halewijn” as a fiefholder from Bruges, Buylaert noting that after her death it was held successively “Jan van Halewij
2 CONC n van Roesbeke...Rogier van Halewijn”[874].  Seigneur de Roosebeke, Burggraaf van Harelbeke. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -        see below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         [MARGUERITE d’Halluin (-after 25 Apr 1435).  A charter dated 25 Apr 1435 names “Pierre de Boulers escuier”, married to “Margaretha van Halewijn, de oudste dochter van Jan van Halewijn en Isabella van Gistel”[875].  No 
2 CONC “Jean d’Halluin” married to “Isabella van Gistel” has been found.  Maybe Marguerite’s father was Jean d’Halluin Seigneur de Roosebeke whose wife was named in various secondary as “Marie van Gistel”.  Maybe “Marie” was an error for
2 CONC  “Isabelle”, as discussed above.  m PIETER van Reigarsvliet Heer van Boelare, son of LODEWIJK van Reigaarsvliet Heer van Boelare & his wife Isabelle de Fosseux (-1466).] 
2 CONT
2 CONT iii)        [MARGUERITE] d’Halluin (-12 Apr 1470, bur Bouvignies).  The anonymous author records her name, parentage, marriage, and the dates of death and places of burial of the couple (no sources cited)[876].  If Marguerite, wif
2 CONC e of Pieter van Reigaarsvliet, was the daughter of Jean d’Halluin Seigneur de Roosebeke as suggested above, maybe the wife of Jacques d’Ollehain was not named Marguerite.  Vrouw van Desselghem.  m JACQUES d’Ollehain Seigneur d’Est
2 CONC ambourg, de Bouvignies et de Gondecourt, son of --- (-6 Aug 1483, bur Bouvignies). 
2 CONT
2 CONT iv)        GUILLEMETTE d’Halluin .  The anonymous author records her parentage and marriage (no sources cited)[877].  m JEAN Seigneur de Léaucourt, son of ---. 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/nfranord.htm#JeanHalluindied1425
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria van Gistel (wife of Jan II) played the role of the "Heraldic and Social Catalyst" who connected the Halluins with the highest circles of the Burgundian nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Maria van Gistel (van Ghistelles); Lady of Zweveghem, Lady of Straeten (heiress)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. THE LEGAL IDENTITY'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Land: Zweveghem (suo jure), Straeten, and interests in the Châtellenies of Bruges and Courtrai.
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles: Lady of Zweveghem (jure uxoris),  Lady of Straeten (heiress).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jurisdiction: The Franc of Bruges and the Land of Courtrai.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alliances: House of Ghistelles (Straeten branch); House of Flanders (via her first marriage); House of Halluin (via her second marriage).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. THE STRATEGIC FOOTPRINT'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Dates Flourished: c. 1380–1431 (Died after 1431).
2 CONT
2 CONT Role: Heiress of Zweveghem and Link to the Burgundian Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strategic Position: Maria was a key figure in the social promotion of the Halluins. As the widow of Louis "Le Frizon" (a bastard of the Count of Flanders), she brought immense social prestige to her second marriage with Jan II. He
2 CONC r status as the heiress of Zweveghem ensured that the Halluins acquired a permanent and prestigious headquarters in West Flanders, which served as the foundation for their later elevation to Knights of the Order of the Golden Flee
2 CONC ce.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. SUMMARY RESEARCH NOTE: THE ANCHOR POINT'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria serves as a Chronological Anchor for the generational transition around 1400. The fact that she married Jan II only after 1396 (following the death of her first husband at the Battle of Nicopolis) proves conclusively that sh
2 CONC e cannot be the mother of the Aalst "Power Bloc" (Gislain/Ghislaine), who were already active in 1385. We apply the Class 3 Chronological Fold correction here: Maria is the matriarch of the 15th-century Zweveghem branch, while her
2 CONC  mother-in-law (Marie van Gent) was the matriarch of the 14th-century Aalst branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. BIOGRAPHICAL SUMMARY'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria van Ghistelles was the daughter of Roger, Lord of Straeten, and Isabeau van Dudzele. Her life reflects the turbulent but opportunistic transition into the Burgundian era. Her first marriage linked her to the comital family o
2 CONC f Flanders, granting her an exceptional position at court. After the death of her first husband in 1396, she linked her fate and her considerable estates—including the important lordship of Zweveghem—to Jan II van Halewijn. Togeth
2 CONC er, they formed a "power couple" that combined the financial resources of the Halluins with the political capital of the Ghistelles. She survived her husband, Jan II (killed in action in 1425) by many years, remaining an influenti
2 CONC al figure in the administration of her family's estates until after 1431.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. DOCUMENTATION AND EVIDENCE'''
2 CONT * Marriage Record: Documented marriage to Jan II van Halewijn post-1396.
2 CONT * Property Transfer: Charters regarding the lordship of Zweveghem, which she managed alongside Jan II and which was formally consolidated in 1414/15.
2 CONT * Death Anchor: Citations in the accounts of the Châtellenie of Courtrai until after 1431.
2 CONT * Secondary Authority: Racines et Histoire (Page 3), explicitly stating her lineage and both marriages.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. HERALDRY'''
2 CONT Arms: Gules, a chevron ermine (Van Ghistelles).
2 CONT
2 CONT Status: Her coat of arms was quartered with the Halluin lions by her descendants. This quarter was an essential part of the "proofs of nobility" for the later Knights of the Golden Fleece from her lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT VII. METHODOLOGICAL COMPLIANCE
2 CONT
2 CONT This profile has been constructed in strict accordance with the Curator’s Logic Project guidelines. We have used the 1396 Chronological Anchor to isolate Maria from earlier Ghistelles alliances. By applying the Geographical Firewa
2 CONC ll to Zweveghem, we have anchored her role as the rightful bringer of this property into the Halluin family. This prevents a Class 6 Vacuum Filler error where all Ghistelles women are merged into a single identity.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">This summary is generated with AI assistance to serve as a research lead for the House of Halluin. This profile is built upon a High-Fidelity Evidence Model. All connections to the Inchov
2 CONC e and Aalst jurisdictions are based on documented property transfers and contemporary office-holding records. Traditional "Ancien Régime" pedigrees that suggest earlier or later dates for the Inchove acquisition have been Exorcise
2 CONC d to correct chronological anomalies. If primary evidence (Charters/Cartularies) contradicts the current placement, the "Unknown" placeholder will be reinstated per Curator’s Logic Step 6.</span>
2 CONT ----
2 CONT A: Dates Flourished:	c. 1380–1431 (Died after 1431).
2 CONT B: Status::	Heiress of Zweveghem and Link to the Burgundian Court.
2 CONT C: Strategic Role::	Maria was a key figure in the social promotion of the Halluins. As the widow of Louis "Le Frizon" (a bastard of the Count of Flanders), she brought immense social prestige to her second marriage with Jan II.Her
2 CONC  status as the heiress of Zweveghem ensured that the Halluins acquired a permanent and prestigious headquarters in West Flanders, which served as the foundation for their later elevation to Knights of the Order of the Golden Fleec
2 CONC e.
