2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hroðgar, Hrothgar, Hróarr, Hroar, Roar, Roas or Ro was a legendary Danish king, living in the early 6th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Danish king Hroðgar appears in the Anglo-Saxon epics Beowulf and Widsith, and also in Norse sagas, Norse poems, and medieval Danish chronicles. In both Anglo-Saxon and Scandinavian tradition, Hroðgar is a Scylding, the son of He
2 CONC alfdene, the brother of Halga, and the uncle of Hroðulf. Moreover, in both traditions, the mentioned characters were the contemporaries of the Swedish king Eadgils; and both traditions also mention a feud with men named Froda and 
2 CONC Ingeld. The consensus view is that Anglo-Saxon and Scandinavian traditions describe the same people.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hroðgar, Hrothgar, Hróarr, Hroar, Roar, Roas or Ro was a legendary Danish king, living in the early 6th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Danish king Hroðgar appears in the Anglo-Saxon epics Beowulf and Widsith, and also in Norse sagas, Norse poems, and medieval Danish chronicles. In both Anglo-Saxon and Scandinavian tradition, Hroðgar is a Scylding, the son of He
2 CONC alfdene, the brother of Halga, and the uncle of Hroðulf. Moreover, in both traditions, the mentioned characters were the contemporaries of the Swedish king Eadgils; and both traditions also mention a feud with men named Froda and 
2 CONC Ingeld. The consensus view is that Anglo-Saxon and Scandinavian traditions describe the same people
2 CONT
2 CONT In the epic poem Beowulf, Hroðgar is mentioned as the builder of the great hall Heorot, and ruler of Denmark when the Geatish hero Beowulf arrives to defeat the monster Grendel.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Hroðgar is first introduced in Beowulf, it is explained that he was the second of four children of King Healfdene: he had an older brother, Heorogar, who was king before him; a younger brother Halga; and a sister, who was mar
2 CONC ried to the king of Sweden. The sister is not named in the manuscript and most scholars agree this is a scribal error[6], but suggested names are Signy and Yrsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT The poem further tells that Hroðgar was "given victory in war" and so his kinsmen eagerly followed him. He is both honest and generous: "He broke no oaths, dealt out rings, treasures at his table". When Beowulf leads his men to De
2 CONC nmark, he speaks of Hroðgar to both a coast-guard and to Hroðgar's herald: he calls Hroðgar a "famed king", "famed warrior", and "protector of the Scyldings" (the ruling clan), and describes him as "old and good." The poet emphasi
2 CONC zes that the Danes "did not find fault" with Hroðgar, "for that was a good King". When Beowulf defeats Grendel, Hroðgar rewards Beowulf and his men with great treasures, showing his gratitude and open-handedness. The poet says tha
2 CONC t Hroðgar is so generous that "no man could fault him, who wished to speak the truth."
2 CONT
2 CONT Hroðgar was married to a woman named Wealhþeow, who was a Helming, probably defining her as a relative of Helm, the ruler of the Wulfings. When Hroðgar welcomes Beowulf, he recalls his friendship with Beowulf's family. He met Beow
2 CONC ulf's father Ecgþeow "when I first ruled the Danes" after the death of Heorogar; he laments Heorogar's fall ("He was better than I!") and recalls how he settled Ecgþeow's blood feud with the Wulfings. Hroðgar thanks God for Beowul
2 CONC f's arrival and victory over Grendel, and swears to love Beowulf like a son.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Scandinavian sources, consisting of Norse sagas, Icelandic poems and Danish chronicles, Hroðgar also appears as a Danish king of the Scylding dynasty. He remains the son of Healfdene and the elder brother of Halga. Moreover
2 CONC , he is still the uncle of Hroðulf. The Scandinavian sources also agree with Beowulf by making Hroðgar contemporary with the Swedish king Eadgils. These agreements with Beowulf are remarkable considering the fact that these source
2 CONC s were composed from oral tradition 700 to 800 years after the events described, and 300 to 400 years later than Beowulf and Widsith.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are also notable differences. The Heaðobards Ingeld and Froda also appear in Scandinavian tradition, but their tribe, the Heaðobards, had long been forgotten, and instead the tribal feud was rendered as a family feud. Their 
2 CONC relationship as father and son had also been reversed in some sources, and so either Ingeld or Froda is given as the brother of Healfdene. Ingeld or Froda murdered Healfdene, but was himself killed in revenge by Hroðgar and Halga.
2 CONC  Moreover, in Scandinavian tradition, Hroðgar is a minor character in comparison to his nephew Hroðulf. Such differences indicate that Beowulf and Scandinavian sources represent separate traditions.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- Hroar Halfdansson - was born about 0526 in Roskilde, Denmark. He is the son of Halfdan Frodasson. Hroar married Princess Ogne of Northumberland about 0546 in Denmark. Princess Ogne was born about 0530. She is 
2 CONC the daughter of King Norbril of Northumberland.
