1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agne Agne, Agni, Hogne or Agni Skjálfarbondi was a mythological king of Sweden, of the House of Yngling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that he was the son of Dag the Wise, and he was mighty and famous. He was also skilled in many ways.
2 CONT
2 CONT One summer, he went to Finland with his army where he pillaged. The Finns gathered a vast host under a chief named Frosti.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT A great battle ensued which Agne won and many Finns were killed together with Frosti. Agne then subdued all of Finland with his army, and captured not only great booty but also Frosti's daughter Skjalf and her kinsman Logi.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Agne returned to Sweden and they arrived at Stocksund (Stockholm) where they put up their tent on the side of the river where it is flat. Agne had a torc which had belonged to Agne's great-great-great-grandfather Visbur (who, inte
2 CONC restingly, was the son of Skjalf's niece Drífa). Although, they were related, Agne married Skjalf who became pregnant with two sons, Erik and Alrik.
2 CONT
2 CONT Skjalf asked Agne to honour her dead father Frosti with a great feast, which he granted. He invited a great many guests, who gladly arrived to the now even more famous Swedish king. They had a drinking competition in which Agne be
2 CONC came very drunk. Skjalf saw her opportunity and asked Agne to take care of Visbur's torc which was around his neck. Agne bound it fast around his neck before he went to sleep.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king's tent was next to the woods and was under the branches of a tall tree for shade. When Agne was fast asleep, Skjalf took a rope which she attached to the torc. Then she had her men remove the tent, and she threw the rope 
2 CONC over a bough. Then she told her men to pull the rope and they hanged Agne avenging Skjalf's father. Skjalf and her men ran to the ships and escaped to Finland, leaving her sons behind.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agne was buried at the place and it is presently called Agnafit ( Agne-fate  in English ), which is east of the Tauren (the Old Norse name for Södertörn) and west of Stocksund.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Þat tel ek undr,
2 CONT     ef Agna her
2 CONT     Skalfar ráð
2 CONT     at sköpum þóttu,
2 CONT     þar gœðing
2 CONT     með gullmeni
2 CONT     Loga dís
2 CONT     at lopti hóf
2 CONT     svalan hest
2 CONT     Signýjar vers.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT     How do ye like the high-souled maid,
2 CONT     Who, with the grim Fate-goddess' aid,
2 CONT     Avenged her sire? – made Swithiod's king
2 CONT     Through air in golden halter swing?
2 CONT     How do ye like her, Agne's men?
2 CONT     Think ye that any chief again
2 CONT     Will court the fate your chief befell,
2 CONT     To ride on wooden horse to hell?.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ynglingatal then gives Alrekr and Eiríkr as Agne's successors.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT Qui [Dagr] genuit Alrik; hunc frater suus Erikr freno percussit ad mortem. Alricr autem genuit Hogna; istum uxor sua juxta locum Agnafit, qui nunc Stokholmr dicitur, propriis manibus interfecit suspendendo ad arborem cum catena au
2 CONC rea. Cujus filius Ingialdr [...][7] 	
2 CONT This man [Dag] engendered Alrek, who was beaten to death with a bridle by his brother, Eirik. Alrek was father to Agne, whose wife dispatched him with her own hands by hanging him on a tree with a golden chain near a place called 
2 CONC Agnafit. His son, Ingjald, [...][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Agne is incorrectly called Hogne[7]. Unlike Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiæ does not give Dagr as Agne's predecessor, but Alrekr. Instead Alrekr is Agne's predecessor and Agne is succeeded by Yngvi (incorrectly called Ingialdr[7]).
2 CONC  The even earlier source Íslendingabók cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and it gives the same line of succession as Historia Norwegiæ: xii Alrekr. xiii Agni. xiiii Yngvi[9].
2 CONT
2 CONT The location indicated by Snorri Sturluson as the place of Agne's death has a barrow called Agnehögen (Agne's barrow) in Lillhersby. The barrow was excavated by Oxenstierna and dated to c. 400.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Konge i Uppsala (Sverige)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
