1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''ROLLO''' ["Ganger" Hrolf], son of [RAGNVALD "the Wise" Jarl of Möre in Norway & his wife Ragnhild] (-Rouen [928/33], bur ---, transferred [1064] to Rouen Cathedral[1]).  The parentage of Rollo/Rolf is uncertain 
2 CONC and the chronology of his life confused. He is known to history as ROBERT I Comte [de Normandie], although no early source has been identified which refers to him by this name or title. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''x NN'''
2 CONT *1.         ['''KADLINE''' .   m BEOLAN King [in Scotland].]
2 CONT *2.         ['''NIEDERGA''' .  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''xx ([886] or after, repudiated, remarried > 912) POPPA''', d/o BERENGAR Comte de Bayeux
2 CONT *3.         '''GUILLAUME''' (Rouen [900/05]-murdered Pequigny 17 Dec 942).  He succeeded his father in [928/33] as GUILLAUME I "Longuespee" Comte [de Normandie]. x SPROTA  xx  LUITGARDIS de Vermandois
2 CONT *4.         '''GERLOC''' (->969) x ([935]) GUILLAUME I "Tête d'Etoupe" Comte de Poitou s/o EBLES "Mancer" Comte de Poitou, Duke of Aquitaine & his first wife Aremburga ([900]-3 Apr 963)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''xxx (912) GISELA''', d/o CHARLES III "le Simple" King of the West Franks & Frederuna - ([908/16]-< her husband). 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORMANDY.htm#Adelaisdiedafter1037
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Among biographical remarks about Rollo written by the cleric Dudo of Saint-Quentin in the late 10th century, he claimed that Rollo was from Dacia (Latin name for Denmark at that time), and had moved from there to the island of Sca
2 CONC nza. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Gange-Hrólfr 'Rollo' Ragnvaldsson=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rollo'''/"'Rolf"' Ganger (The Walker) Ragnvaldsson, '''1st Duke of Normandy'''<br/>
2 CONT (''Ganger'', was a nickname given to him because he was such a large man that no horse could bear him and he was mostly forced to walk.) <br/>
2 CONT ''Rolf'' is a contraction of ''Hrodwulf'' (Rudolf) which is a conjunction of the stem words ''hrod'' (renown) + ''wulf'' (wolf), and is written in Old Norse as ''Hrólfr'.
2 CONT
2 CONT An attempt was made in 2016 to extract DNA from the graves of his grandson and great-grandson, but failed - it was only shown that the bones in the grave were far older than the grandson, so it couldn't be the right bones.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.deloriahurst.com/deloriahurst%20page/3310.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ==content to clean up==
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Normandy was the title given to the rulers of the Duchy of Normandy in northwestern France, which has its origins as the County of Rouen, a fief created in 911 by King Charles II "the Simple" of France for Rollo, a Norwegi
2 CONC an nobleman and Viking leader of Northmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gangr-Hrólfr Ragnvaldsson''', or '''Rollo''' de Normandie was a Norse nobleman (according to Snorre, his father was a Jarl or more modernly, an Earl.... though a Scandinavian "Jarl" is a higher rank than an "Earl" of the Britis
2 CONC h Isles and are not interchangeable) and the founder and first ruler of the Viking  principality in what soon became known as Normandy. The name "Rollo" is a Frankish-Latin name probably taken from the Old Norse name Hrólfr, moder
2 CONC n Scandinavian name Rolf (cf. the latinization of Hrólfr into the similar Roluo in the Gesta Danorum).  Hrolfr is buried in Notre Dame Cathedral in Rouen France - his sarcophagus is located along the south side of the cathedral in
2 CONC  a line with the sarcophagus of Richard the Lion Heart (1155-1199). Only Richard's heart was placed in his sarcophagus.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Parents: Ragnvald Eysteinsson Mørejarl & Hild Nefja (uncertain, see below)
2 CONT * Spouses:
2 CONT * 1. Poppa de Bayeux
2 CONT * Children:
2 CONT ** Vilhjalm Langaspjót (Guillaume Longue Épée)
2 CONT ** Geirlaug (Gerloc) who later took the name Adela
2 CONT *2. Gisela de France (betrothal, no children)
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Landnáma (The book of Settlers in Iceland, written in the 12th century), Rollo had a daughter named Kaðlín (Kathlin or Cathlin) her mother is not named (Notes by Anna Petursdottir): 
2 CONT * Kaðlin (Kathlin)
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaðlín is mentioned along with her father Rollo in chapter 33 in Landnámabók (The Book of Settlers) and her father, Rollo, and his brothers, also their father, Ragnvald are mentioned in chapter 82 : https://www.snerpa.is/net/snorr
2 CONC i/landnama.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORMANDY.htm#Adelaisdiedafter1037 Project MedLands, NORMANDY]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ['''ROLLO [Hrolf "Ganger/the Walker'"] (-[928]).  
2 CONT Orkneyinga Saga names “Hrolf who conquered Normandy” as son of “Earl Rognwald” and his wife “Ragnhild the daughter of Hrolf Nose”, adding that he was so big that no horse could carry him, giving rise to his name “Göngu-Hrolf”.   S
2 CONC norre names "Rolf and Thorer" as the two sons of "Earl Ragnvald" and his wife Hild, recording that Rolf was banished from Norway by King Harald and travelled to the Hebrides, settling first in Orkney before moving southwards throu
2 CONC gh Scotland, and eventually conquering Normandy.   The Historia Norwegie records that, after Orkney was conquered by "principi Rogwaldi" and his followers, "de quorum collegio…Rodulfus" captured Rouen in Normandy, commenting that 
2 CONC he was known as "Gongurolfr" because he was obliged to walk as he was too large to travel on horseback. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Dudo of Saint-Quentin, Rollo arrived in northern France in 876, although there is some debate about being a more likely date.   William of Jumièges records that Rollo was chosen by lot to be leader of the Viking colon
2 CONC ists.  Viking raids intensified in northern France.  Although they were defeated after raiding Chartres , Charles III "le Simple" King of the West Franks granted the Normans land around Rouen in which to settle[.  The uncertain na
2 CONC ture of the demise was the source of future problems between the French crown, which claimed that it was an enfeofment for which the ruler owed allegiance, and the later Dukes of Normandy who claimed it was an unconditional allod 
2 CONC for which no allegiance was owed.   A charter dated 14 Mar 918 which granted land to the monastery of Saint-Germain-des-Prés "except that part…which we have granted to the Normans of the Seine, namely to Rollo and his companions".
2 CONC   '''He was later known as ROBERT I Comte [de Normandie].''' ROLLO [ Ganger Hrolf], son of [RAGNVALD "the Wise" Jarl of Möre in Norway & his wife Ragnhild ---] (-Rouen [928/33],  bur ---, transferred [1064] to Rouen Cathedral.  Th
2 CONC e parentage of Rollo/Rolf is uncertain and the chronology of his life confused.  Richer names "Rollone filio Catilli" as leader of the Vikings who raided along the Loire and against whom "Robertus Celticæ Galliæ dux" campaigned.  
2 CONC No further reference has been found to "Catillus/Ketel".   Flodoard provides no information on Rollo´s ancestry.  The early 12th century William of Malmesbury states that "Rollo…[was] born of noble lineage among the Norwegians, th
2 CONC ough obsolete from its extreme antiquity" and adds that he was "banished by the king´s command from his own country"[8].   The later Orkneyinga Saga is more specific, naming “Hrolf who conquered Normandy” as son of “Earl Rognwald”
2 CONC  and his wife “Ragnhild the daughter of Hrolf Nose”, adding that he was so big that no horse could carry him, giving rise to his name “Göngu-Hrolf”.  Snorre names "Rolf and Thorer" as the two sons of "Earl Ragnvald" and his wife H
2 CONC ild, recording that Rolf was banished from Norway by King Harald and travelled to the Hebrides, settling first in Orkney before moving southwards through Scotland, and eventually conquering Normandy.   The Historia Norwegie record
2 CONC s that, after Orkney was conquered by "principi Rogwaldi" and his followers, "de quorum collegio…Rodulfus" captured Rouen in Normandy, commenting that he was known as "Gongurolfr" because he was obliged to walk as he was too large
2 CONC  to travel on horseback[.  This source makes no reference to any blood relationship between Rollo and "principi Rogwaldi".  Guillaume de Jumièges revised, abbreviated and updated an earlier history compiled by Dudo of Saint-Quenti
2 CONC n, stating that Rollo'a father "possédant presque en totalité le royaume de Dacie, conquit en outre les territoires limitrophes de la Dacie et de l´Alanie" and left "deux fils…l´aîné Rollon et le plus jeune Gurim".  He records tha
2 CONC t the king of Dacia defeated the two brothers and killed Gorm, and that Rollo fled the country, first landing in England, where he made peace with "le roi…Alstem".  If this refers to Æthelstan King of Wessex, the account must be c
2 CONC onfused given King Æthelstan´s succession in 924.   Freeman suggests that Guillaume de Jumièges must be referring to "Guthrum-Æthelstan of East-Anglia", although this does not resolve the chronological problems assuming that the A
2 CONC nglo-Saxon Chronicle is correct in recording Guthrum´s death in 890[15].   After recording Rollo´s expeditions in Frisia, Guillaume de Jumièges states that Rollo landed at Jumièges after sailing up the Seine in 876[16], another su
2 CONC spect date which Houts suggests should be corrected to .  Guillaume de Jumièges records that Rollo defeated "Renaud duc de toute la France", captured "le château de Meulan", defeated and killed Duke Renaud in another campaign, bes
2 CONC ieged Paris, captured Bayeux, and attacked Paris again while his other troops devastated Evreux where they killed "son évêque…Sibor".  Guillaume de Jumièges records that Rollo attacked Chartres but withdrew after being defeated by
2 CONC  "Richard duc de Bourgogne" and "Anselme l évêque".  William of Malmesbury records that "Rollo…experienced a check at Chartres" but escaped the "plentiful slaughter" of the Vikings by the townspeople, before capturing Rouen "in 87
2 CONC 6"[.   Guillaume of Jumièges records that Charles III "le Simple" King of the West Franks granted Rollo "tout le territoire maritime qui s´étend depuis la rivière d´Epte jusqu´aux confines de la Bretagne" together with "sa fille…G
2 CONC isèle", that "les princes de cette province…Béranger et Alain" swore allegiance to Rollo, and that '''Rollo was baptised in 912 by "l´archévêque Francon", adopting the name ROBERT after "le duc Robert" who acted as his sponsor.'''
2 CONC    William of Malmesbury records that "it was determined by treaty, that [Rollo] should be baptised, and hold the country of the king as his lord"  The charter which confirms the original grant (assuming that there was such a docu
2 CONC ment) has not survived.  However, the grant of land is inferred from a charter dated 14 Mar 918, under which land was donated to the monastery of Saint-Germain-des-Prés specifying that the donation excluded "that part…which we hav
2 CONC e granted to the Normans of the Seine, namely to Rollo and his companions".  The uncertain nature of the demise was the source of future problems between the French crown, which claimed that Normandy was an enfeofment for which th
2 CONC e ruling duke owed allegiance, and the later dukes of Normandy, who claimed that it was an unconditional allod for which no allegiance was owed.  The version of events recorded by Flodoard provides a different slant and names two 
2 CONC Viking leaders.  Firstly, Flodoard records that in 923 "Ragenoldus princeps Nortmannorum" who occupied "in fluvio Ligeri" devastated "Franciam trans Isaram", that "Nortmanni" made peace in 924 "cum Francis", that King Raoul grante
2 CONC d them "Cinomannis et Baiocæ" [Maine and Bayeux], but that "Raginoldus cum suis Nortmannis" devasted the land between the Loire and the Seine.  This passage makes no mention of the supposed earlier grant of land along the shore.  
2 CONC Secondly, the same source records that "Raginoldus cum suis Nortmannis" devasted Burgundy in 925, that Héribert [II] Comte de Vermandois besieged Norman castles "super Sequanam", that "Nortmanni" devastated "pagum Belvacensem atqu
2 CONC e Ambianensem" [Beauvais and Amboise], while Comte Héribert and Arnoul Count of Flanders forced "Rollo princeps" from his strongholds.  Thirdly, Flodoard states that "Hugo filius Rotberti et Heribertus comes" campaigned against "N
2 CONC ortmannos" in 927, that "filius Rollonis" did homage to ex-king Charles at "castellum…Auga", and that "Rollo" held "filius Heriberti Odo" as a hostage in 928, which suggests some sort of alliance between Rollo and Comte Héribert. 
2 CONC   William of Malmesbury records that Rollo died at Rouen.  The date of his death is uncertain: Flodoard names Rollo as living in 928 (see above) but the same source names "Willelmus princeps Nortmannorum" in 933.   Orderic Vitalis
2 CONC  implies that the transfer of Robert's body to Rouen Cathedral took place after the "the ninth year" in office of Archbishop Maurilius, who had succeeded Mauger de Normandie, which would date the event to [1064].  '''He is known t
2 CONC o history as ROBERT I Comte [de Normandie], although no early source has been identified which refers to him by this name or title.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[m] [firstly] ---.  The identity of Rollo´s first wife or concubine is not known.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''married  [secondly] or after, repudiated, remarried after 912) POPPA, daughter of BERENGAR Comte de Bayeux & his wife ---.  Guillaume of Jumièges records that Rollo took "Popa, fille de Bérenger, homme illustre" when he capture
2 CONC d Bayeux and "s´unit avec elle, à la manière des Danois".'''  According to Orderic Vitalis, Rollo "stormed and captured Bayeux, slew its count Berengar and took to wife his daughter Poppa".  In another passage, the same source rec
2 CONC ords that Rollo besieged Paris, captured Bayeux, killed "Berengarium comitem" and married his daughter Popa, in 886, although this date appears early in light of the likely birth date range of the couple's son Guillaume.  The Chro
2 CONC nico Rotomagensis records that "mortua a Gisla, accepit Rollo propriam uxorem filiam comitis Silvanectensis Widonis".  Robert of Torigny combines the information, recording that "Rollo dux Northmannorum" married "Popam prius repud
2 CONC iatam uxorem…filiam…Berengarii comitis Baiocensis neptem vero Widonis comitis Silvanectensis".  The Historia Norwegie records that, after capturing Rouen, "Rodulfus" married the daughter of its deceased count by whom he was father
2 CONC  of "Willelmum…Longosped".   Guillaume de Jumièges records that "le comte Bernard" welcomed "son neveu Richard" (grandson of Rollo) at Senlis after his escape from captivity, although in another passage he describes how Rollo capt
2 CONC ured Bayeux and took "une très-noble jeune fille Popa, fille de Bérenger" in the town, marrying her "à la manière des Danois", in a later passage adding that Rollo married Poppa, whom he had previously repudiated, a second time af
2 CONC ter the death of his wife.  It would be possible to reconcile the different versions if Comte Bernard's mother was married twice, her first husband being Bérenger Comte de Bayeux. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''married thirdly (912) GISELA, daughter of CHARLES III "le Simple" King of the West Franks & his first wife Frederuna --- ([908/16]-before her husband).'''  The Genealogica Arnulfi Comitis names (in order) "Hyrmintrudim, Frederu
2 CONC nam, Adelheidim, Gislam, Rotrudim et Hildegardim" as the children of "Karolus rex…ex Frederuna regina". Guillaume of Jumièges records that Charles III "le Simple" King of the West Franks granted Rollo "tout le territoire maritime 
2 CONC qui s´étend depuis la rivière d´Epte jusqu´aux confines de la Bretagne" together with "sa fille…Gisèle", and their marriage which took place after Rollo´s baptism[.  Her marriage is recorded in the Norman annals for 912, which sta
2 CONC te that she died without issue, presumably soon after the marriage when Gisla must still have been an infant.   The chronicle of Dudo of Saint-Quentin describes her as of "tall stature, most elegant…", which is of course inconsist
2 CONC ent with her supposed birth date range.  The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum records the marriage of "filiam suam [=rex Karolus] nomine Gillam" to "Rollo".  Settipani considers that the marriage did not occur, and that the Norman
2 CONC  sources confused it with the marriage of Gisela, daughter of Lothaire II King of Lotharingia, to the Viking leader Gotfrid. 
2 CONT
