2 CONT ==[http://books.google.com/books?hl=no&id=KGIeAAAAMAAJ&q=fauske#search_anchor Rollo's Origin]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is named as Rollo and said to have come from Dacia by Dudo of St. Quentin (c. 965-after 1043), the historian of the Norman dukes and the earliest source. Dudo does not name Rollo's parents. The ''Orkneyinga saga'', a later sour
2 CONC ce (c. 1230), identifies him with Hrolf Gange, who is said to have been a son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Ragnvald-Eysteinsson/6000000001045491041 Ragnvald Eysteinsson], jarl of Møre.See, for example, Stewart Baldwin, "[https:/
2 CONC /groups.google.com/forum/?hl=en#!searchin/soc.genealogy.medieval/rollo$20hrolf/soc.genealogy.medieval/AqjvzYyjOQ0/WXzGkZXcu28J Rollo of Normany]" in soc.genealogy.medieval, Mar. 16, 1998. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There is much support for the claim of Rollo's homeland being Sykkylven in Sunnmøre (Møre), Norway. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dacia, the country Dudo refers to as Rollo's homeland, was what people outside Scandinavia called the Nordic countries as a unity: Norway, Denmark, Sweden and Iceland together. Dudo describes Dacia as a country of high mountains, 
2 CONC surrounding Rollo's estate - just like Gange-Hrolf's Fauske in Sykkylven. (see photos)
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time the language, Old Norse (called dönsk tunga by Snorri and others) was still the same and had not started to divide into separate dialects or languages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fasge, the place Adam of Bremen describes as Rollo's home, can easily be placed in Sykkylven where Gangu-Hrolfr had his estate at the farm called Fauske, Aure or Aurum. The Danish historian Steenstrup identified (works from 1876-8
2 CONC 2) Fasge with the town Faxe in Denmark, but linguistic argument shows that this consonant change is highly unlikely, and that the Norwegian place-name Fauske is more probable. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The outstanding linguist Håkon Melberg argued in his dissertation that linguistic studies could shed light on the origin of the Scandinavian people and their history. In particular he opposes Steenstrup's analysis and points at se
2 CONC veral discrepancies, making Denmark improbable as Gange-Hrolf's origin. 
2 CONT
