2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 27th ggf of Gordon Fisher
2 CONT
2 CONT Or:  HROLFR the GANGER (walker), GANGE-ROLV, ROLF, ROLLO OF NORMANDY;
2 CONT andlater in life, ROBERT; also HRO'LFR
2 CONT
2 CONT "The central fact of Norman history ... is ... the grant of Normandy andhis
2 CONT northern followers in the year 911.  ...  For the actual occurences ofthat
2 CONT year, we have only the account of a romancing historian of a hundred years
2 CONT later, reenforced here and there by the exceedingly scanty records of the
2 CONT time.  The main fact is clear, namely that the Frankish king, Charles the
2 CONT Simple, granted Rollo as a fief a considerable part, the eastern part, of
2 CONT later Normandy.  Apparently Rollo did homage for his fied in feudalfashion
2 CONT by placing his hands between the hands of the king, something, we aretold,
2 CONT which "neither his father, nor his grandfather, nor his great-grandfather
2 CONT before him had ever done for any man."  Legend goes on to relate, however,
2 CONT that Rollo refused to kneel and kiss the king's foot, crying out in hisown
2 CONT speech, "No, by God!" and that the companion to whom he delegated the
2 CONT unwelcome obligation performed it so clumsily that he overturned the king,
2 CONT to the great merriment of the assembled Northmen.  ...  As to Rollo's
2 CONT personality, we have only the evidence of later Norman historians of
2 CONT doubtful authority and the Norse saga of HArold Fairhair.  If, as seems
2 CONT likely, their accounts relate to the same person, he was known in thenorth
2 CONT as Hrolf the Ganger, because he was so huge that no horse could carry him
2 CONT and he must needs gang afoot.  A pirate at home, he was driven into exileby
2 CONT the anger of King Harold, whereupon he followed his trade in the Western
2 CONT Isles and in Gaul, and rose to be a great Jarl among his people.  The saga
2 CONT makes him a Norwegian, but Danish scholars have sought to prove him aDane,
2 CONT and more recently the cudgels have been taken up for his Swedish origin.To
2 CONT me the NOrwegian theory seems on the whole the most probable, being basedon
2 CONT a trustworthy saga and corroborated by other incidental evidence.  ... The
2 CONT important fact is that Norway, Denmark, and even more distant Sweden, all
2 CONT contributed to the colonists who settled in Normandy under Rollo and his
2 CONT successors, and the achievements of the Normans thus become the common
2 CONT heritage of the Scandinavian race.  (P) The colonization of Normandy was,of
2 CONT course, only a small part of the work of this heroic age of Scandinavian
2 CONT expansion.  The great emigration from the North in the ninth and tenth
2 CONT centuries has been explained in part by the growth of centralizedgovernment
2 CONT and the consequent departure of the independent, the turbulent, and the
2 CONT untamed for new fields of adventure; but its chief cause was doubtlessthat
2 CONT which lies back of colonizing movements in all ages, the growth of
2 CONT population and the need of more room.  Five centuries earlier this
2 CONT land-hunger had pushed the Germanic tribes across the Rhine and Danube and
2 CONT produced the great wandering of the peoples which destroyed the Roman
2 CONT empire; and the Viking raids were simply a later aspect of this same
2 CONT *Vo"lkerwanderung*, retarded by the outlying position of the Scandinavian
2 CONT lands and by the greater difficulty of migration by sea. For, unlike the
2 CONT Goths who swept across the map of Europe in vast curves of marching men,or
2 CONT the Franks who moved forward by slow stages of gradual settlement in their
2 CONT occupation of Roman Gaul, the Scandinavian invaders were men of the seaand
2 CONT migrated in ships."
2 CONT    --- Charles Homer Haskins, *The Normans in European History*, Boston &
2 CONT NY, 1915, p 26-30 passim.  From p 48 & 50:  "At this point the fundamental
2 CONT question forces itself upon us, how far was Normandy affected by
2 CONT Scandinavian influences?  What in race and language, in law and custom,was
2 CONT the contribution of the north to Normandy?  And the answer must be that in
2 CONT most respects the tangible contribution was slight.  Whatever may havebeen
2 CONT the state of affairs in the age of colonization and settlement, by the
2 CONT century which followed the Normans had become to a surprising degree
2 CONT absorbed by their environment.  .....  What, then, was the Scandinavian
2 CONT contribution to the making of Normandy if it was neither law nor speechnor
2 CONT race?  First and foremost, it was Normandy itself, created as a distinct
2 CONT entity by the Norman occupation and the grant to Rollo and his followers,
2 CONT without whom it would have remained an undifferentiated part of northern
2 CONT France. Next, a new element in the population, numerically small in
2 CONT proportion to the ass, but a leaven to the whole --- quick to absorb
2 CONT Frankish law and Christian culture but retaining its northern qualities of
2 CONT enterprise, of daring, and of leadership.  It is no accident that thenames
2 CONT of the leaders in early Norman movements are largely Norse.  And finally a
2 CONT race of princes, high-handed and masteful but with a talent for political
2 CONT organization, state-builders at home and abroad, who made Normandy the
2 CONT strongest and most centralized principality in France and joined to it a
2 CONT kingdom beyond the seas which became the strongest state in westernEurope."
2 CONT
2 CONT "GANGER ROLF, "the Viking" (or ROLLO), banished from Norway to theHebrides
2 CONT ca. 876, 890 participated in Viking attack on Bayeux, where Count Berenger
2 CONT of Bayeux was killed, and dau. Poppa captured and taken, 886, by Rollo(now
2 CONT called Count of Rouen) as his "Danish" wife.  Under Treaty of St. Claire,
2 CONT 911, rec'd the Duchy of Normandy from CHARLES III, "the Simple"; d. ca.927
2 CONT (Isenburg says 931), bur. Notre Dame, Rouen. ... Note: Isenburg inserts a
2 CONT Robert between Rollo and William I, and makes Robert the conqueror of
2 CONT Bayeux, husb. of Poppa, and 1st Duke.  Chronology favors the descent given
2 CONT by Moriarty and Onslow.  It seems probable that Robert was another namefor
2 CONT Rollo.  If there really was a Robert as 1st Duke, then [ROBERT I] would be
2 CONT ROBERT II, which is not the case.  For additional data on William II of
2 CONT Normandy and I of England the reader may consult David C. Douglas,*William
2 CONT the Conqueror* (1964).  Besides a dau. Gerloc (or Adela) who m. 935WILLIAM
2 CONT I ... Count of Poitou, Ganger Rolf had [WILLIAM I, "Longsword"]."
2 CONT    --- Weis & Sheppard, *Ancestral Roots ... *, 7th Edition, 1992, p 110
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rollo (Rollon, Ranger Rolf [sic, instead of Ganger], 1st Duke ofNormandy,
2 CONT Count of Rouen; conquered Normandy; b. c870, Maer, Norway, d. 927-932; md
2 CONT (2) 891 Poppa de Bayeux, Duchess of Norway; b. c872, Bayeux, France; dau
2 CONT Berenger de Bayeux, Count of Bayeux; d. bef. 930; and N.N. of Rennes."
2 CONT   --- Roderick W Stuart, *Royalty for Commoners*, 2nd edn, 1992, p 123-124
2 CONT
2 CONT      The definitive establishment of the Normans, to whom the country owes
2 CONT its name, took place in 911, when by the treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte,
2 CONT concluded between King Charles the Simple of France and Rolf or Rollo,chief
2 CONT of the Normans, the territory comprising the town of Rouen and a few'pagi'
2 CONT situated on the sea-coast was ceded to the latter; but the terms of the
2 CONT treaty are ill-defined, and it is consequently almost impossible to findout
2 CONT the exact extent of this territory or to know whether Brittany was at this
2 CONT time made a feudal dependency of Normandy.  But the chronicler Dudo of
2 CONT Saint-Quentin's statement that Rollo married Gisela, daughter of Charlesthe
2 CONT Simple, must be considered to be legendary work of Dudo of Saint-Quentin
2 CONT [who?] is practically our only authority.
2 CONT      Rollo died in 927 and was succeeded by his son William ...
2 CONT    --- (Source ???)
2 CONT
2 CONT "Charles [the Simple], the son-in-law of eEward, constrained thereto by
2 CONT Rollo, through a succession of calamities, conceded to him that part ofGaul
2 CONT which at present is called Normandy.  It would be tedious to relate forhow
2 CONT many years, and with what audacity, the Normans disquieted every placefrom
2 CONT the British ocean, as I have said, to the Tuscan sea.  First Hasten, and
2 CONT then Rollo; who, born of noble lineage among the Norwegians, thoughobsolete
2 CONT from its extreme antiquity, was banished, by the king's command, from his
2 CONT own country, and brought over with multitudes, who were in danger, either
2 CONT from debt or consciousness of guilt, and whom he had allured by great
2 CONT expectations of advantage.  Betaking himself therefore to piracy, afterhis
2 CONT cruelty had raged on every side at pleasure, he experienced a check at
2 CONT Chartres.  For the townspeople, relying neither on arms norfortifications,
2 CONT piously impoored the assistance of the blessed Virgin Mary.  The shift too
2 CONT of the virgin, which Charles the Bald displayed to the winds on the
2 CONT samparts, thronged by the garrison, after the fashion of a banner.  The
2 CONT enemy on seeing it began to laught, and to direct their arrows at it.This,
2 CONT however, was not done with impunity; for presently their eyes became dim,
2 CONT and they could neither retreat nor advance.  The townsmen, with joy
2 CONT perceiving this, indulged themselves in a plentiful slaughter of them, as
2 CONT far as fortune permitted.  Rollo, however, whom God reserved for the true
2 CONT faith, escaped, and soon after gained Rouen and the neighboring cities by
2 CONT force of arms, in the year of our Lord 876, and one year before the deathof
2 CONT Charles the Bald, whose grandson Lewis, as is before mentioned, vanquished
2 CONT the Normans, but did not expel them; but Charles, the brother of thatLewis,
2 CONT grandson of Charles the Bald, by his son Lewis, as I have said aboce,
2 CONT repeatedly experiencing, from unsuccessful conflicts, that fortune gavehim
2 CONT nothing which she took from others, resolved, after consulting hisnobility,
2 CONT that it was advisable to make a show of royal munificence, when he was
2 CONT unable to repel injury; and, in a friendly manner, sent for Rollo.  He was
2 CONT at this time far advanced in years; and, consequently, easily inclined to
2 CONT pacific measures.  It was therefore determined by treaty, that he shouldbe
2 CONT baptized, and hold that country of the king as his lord.  The inbred and
2 CONT untameable ferocity of the man may well be imagined, for, on receivingthis
2 CONT gift, as the by standers suggested to him, that he ought to kiss the footof
2 CONT his benefactor, disdaining to kneel down, he seized the king's foot and
2 CONT dragged it to his mouth as he stood erect.  The king falling on his back,
2 CONT the Normans began to laugh, and the Franks to be indignant; but Rollo
2 CONT apologized for his shameful conduct, by saying that it was the custom ofhis
2 CONT country.  Thus the affair being settled, Rollo returned to Rouen, andthere
2 CONT died."
2 CONT    --- William of Malmesbury, *Chronicle of the Kings of England*, c 1135,
2 CONT tr John Allen Giles, London (Henry G Bohn) 1847, p 125-126
2 CONT
2 CONT "It is not known when Rollo arrived in the Viking kingdom [in Normandy].
2 CONT Dudo says that he took Rouen in 877, but most historians are agreed that
2 CONT Rollo probably did not appear in Francia until the early tenth century.The
2 CONT possibility exists however, that Dudo is preserving a belief that Vikings
2 CONT had been established in the Rouen area from about this time.  Rollo is
2 CONT thought to have been Norwegian rather than Danish, and later Icelandic
2 CONT sources identify him with Hrolf the Ganger (walker), son of Ragnvald earlof
2 CONT Moer, who had a career as a Viking before settling in Francia.  Hemarried a
2 CONT Christian woman and his son William, according to the Lament of William
2 CONT Longsword, was born overseas.  (P) Nothing more in known about the 'Treaty
2 CONT of St Clair-sur-Epte' concluded in a personal interview between Charlesthe
2 CONT Simple and Rollo than Dudo tells us, and he has been accused of inventing
2 CONT the meeting.  That a cession of territory in the Seine, extending as far
2 CONT west as the mouth of the Seine on the coast and near the source of theEure
2 CONT inland is affirmed by a charter of Charles the Simple dated 14 March 918.
2 CONT ..... Flodoard adds the information that Rollo received baptism and the
2 CONT Frankish name Robert with the cession of this territory.  (P) Rollo seemsto
2 CONT have been made a count in 911, with the traditional duties assigned to a
2 CONT Carolingian count, namely, protection and the administration of justice.He
2 CONT was certainly subordinate to the Frankish king.  With the proliferation of
2 CONT titles accorded the leader of the Normandy Vikings in later sources, some
2 CONT historians hace suggested that Rollo was made a duke, but Werner hasargued
2 CONT that there was no Norman *marchio* before 950-6, and no duke before
2 CONT 987-1006, that is, after Hugh Capet had gained the throne of France. .....
2 CONT (P) Rollo appears to have received his territory on similar terms as the
2 CONT Bretons had received the Cotentin, except that the bishoprics were also
2 CONT ceded. ..... In exchange, Rollo was to defend the Seine from otherVikings,
2 CONT accept baptism and become the *fidelis* of the Frankish king.  That there
2 CONT were other groups of Vikings in the region, particularly in the westernpart
2 CONT of Normandy, is clear.  The west stayed pagan longer; it was a century
2 CONT before a bishop was appointed to the Cotnetin.  .....  (P) The arrangement
2 CONT made in 911 proved successful ..... The area of Normandy by 933corresponded
2 CONT to the area of the archdiocese of Rouen, with the seven *civitates* of
2 CONT Rouen, Bayeux, Avranches, Evreux, See's, Lisieux and Coutances.  The
2 CONT fortunes of the bishops of Rouen and of the (principes* of Normandy werein
2 CONT fact closely associated from the very beginning."
2 CONT    --- Rosamond McKitterick, *The Frankish Kingdom under the Carolingians,
2 CONT 751-987*, London & NY (Longman) 1983, p 237-238
2 CONT
2 CONT "A.D. 917. .....  Rollo, first duke of Normandy, died, and was succeededby
2 CONT his son William."
2 CONT    --- Florence of Worcester (died c 1117), *A History of the Kings of
2 CONT England* (OR: *The Chronicle of Florence of Worcester*), trans Joseph
2 CONT Stephenson, 1853 (reprinted by Llanerch Enterprises, Felinfach, Lampeter,
2 CONT Dyfed, Wales SA48 8PJ, 1980s (?)), p 76
1 NOTE References: History and Genealogy of the Pearsall Family in england and America (3 Vols)1928 by clarence e Pearsall; soule, Sowle and soulis History (1926) by g.T. Ridlon, Sr.; Ancestral Roots of sixty Colonists by frederick Lewis
2 CONC  Weis (4th ed-1969)
2 CONT
2 CONT *************
2 CONT
2 CONT Scandanavian Ancestry (Rollo)
2 CONT
2 CONT Rollo, is claimed to be a direct descendant of ours through our Pearsall ancestry. Rolo was a Norwegian Jarl (chieftain or noble). He was  a sea pirate, who earned the disfavor of King Harold of Norway who caused his eviction from
2 CONC  Norway. Rollo with a large fleet, landed at Roeun, France and became the conqueror of Normandy (the northern area of France above Paris. As the conqueror, he gave land and titles to his fellow Scandanavians.
2 CONT William the Conqueror, was a direct descendant of Rollo and of Scandanavian blood. William invaded and conquered Great Britain with the assistance of many noblemen from Normandy. William's decisive victory was at the Battle of Has
2 CONC tings. Subsequent to the Battle of Hastings, a list was caused to be compiled by William of the names of those men who  had crossed the sea with him from Normandy to England. This list was suspended in the magnificent abbey he con
2 CONC structed on the site of the battle. 
2 CONT The list became known as the "Roll of Battell Abbey". This ancient document states in part, : "Many who came out of Normandy were nobles in their native country;especially such as were stiled from their places(of residence) as Le 
2 CONC Sire de Soule, Le Sieur de Haye, or Le Sieur de Mortain, whereby we understand them Lords (equivalent to the English title) and owners of such manors, towns and castles(chateaus)from whence they took their denominations or surname
2 CONC s."
2 CONT Based upon the above the author of the Soule, Sowle, and Soulis History concludes no family in the living present has a greater claim to a Norman origin than the Soule family.
2 CONT The Conqueror bestowed upon those who filled important  command in his army manorial estates all over England, as promised he had promised to induce them to join. It is assumed that the Le Siere de Soule did not survive the Battle
2 CONC  of Hastings, since no estate was was immediately conferred upon him as proven by the absence of his name from Doomsday survey. Soon thereafter, However, the  Soule-Solis family came to England soon thereafter and were given vast 
2 CONC holdings in England and Scotland.
2 CONT Therefore, Sandanavian Norman heritage is claimed by notable genealogists of both the Soule and Pearsall families. Rollo, the Scandanavian Conqueror of Norway may be considered  our symbolic ancester. (See Soule, Sowle and Soules 
2 CONC History by rev G.T. Ridlon, Sr. (1926); History and Genealogy of the Pearsall Family in England and America by Clarence e. Pearsall, 3 Vols (1928)).
2 CONT
2 CONT Supposedly he was from Maer, Norway, but this is unproven, as well as the parental links. He was known in the north as Hrolf the Ganger, because he was so huge that no horse could carry him and he must follow gang afoot. A pirate 
2 CONC at home, he was driven into exile by the anger of King Harold, whereupon he followed his trade in the Western Isles and in Gaul, and rose to be a great Jarl among his people.
2 CONT
2 CONT It should be noted that Rollo never claimed the title of Duke. He was Count in Rouen, nothing more. The title was occasionally used interchangeably with Count by his descendants through Robert II (Rollo was Robert I) the Magnficen
2 CONC t. Robert's son, William II (the Conqueror), insisted on the title of Duke, though his right to it is not at all clear. As no one ever got the better of William for long, what he demanded was accepted all around. - - John Yohalem
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing is known for sure regarding Rollo's parentage and place of birth. The native Norman sources [e.g., Dudo] give Rollo a brother named "Gurim" (probably "Gorm", but almost certainly not the same man as the well known Gorm of 
2 CONC Denmark), but do not give a name to Rollo's father. Late Icelandic sources state that he was the son of Rognvald of More, but that claim is controversial (and, in my opinion, false), although often repeated as if it were an establ
2 CONC ished fact. - - Stewart Baldwin
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally a part of Charlemagne's empire, Normandy was fairly wealthy, with lots of monasteries and small towns. Lying on the northern coast De France, it became a favorite and easy target for Vikings in the 9th C. It lost most o
2 CONC f its monasteries and was not much of a prize when a Viking came to the King Charles III "the simple" of the West Franks in 911 with a proposal. The Viking was Rolf (claimed by Norway, Denmark and Sweden, but probably from Norway)
2 CONC  with many men at his command. He offered to defend the coast against other Vikings in return for a title. And, naturally, he and his people would convert to Christianity. So, Rolf the Viking became Count De Normandy (later the Du
2 CONC ke De Normandy), and the King of the West Franks breathed a deep sigh of relief.
2 CONT
2 CONT The title "Duke De Normandy", by the way, seems never to have been used before the end of the 10th century, and to have been official only with the accession of William II (the Conqueror). The title granted by Charles the Simple w
2 CONC as Count, usually taken to mean of Rouen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The territory given to Rollo comprised the town of Rouen and a few 'pagi' situated on the seacoast was ceded to the latter; but the terms of the treaty are ill?defined, and it is consequently almost impossible to find out the exac
2 CONC t extent of this territory or to know whether Brittany was at this time made a feudal dependency De Normandy.
