1 NOTE OR "ROLF""GONGE ROLF (MARCHING ROLF)""THE WALKER"; MAERMOR OF NORWAY; 1ST DUKE
2 CONT OF NORMANDY; COUNT OF ROUEN
1 NOTE "ROLLO"
1 NOTE 32nd great grandfather through Adele and 34th through son William
1 NOTE 35th great grandfather
1 NOTE Gange-Rolv, Scandinavian rover who founded the duchy of Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Making himself independent of King Harald I of Norway, Rollo sailed off to raid Scotland, England, Flanders, and France on pirating expeditions and, about 911, established himself in an area along the Seine River. Charles III the 
2 CONC Simple of France held off his siege of Paris, battled him near Chartres, and negotiated the treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, giving him the part of Neustria that came to be called Normandy; Rollo in return agreed to end his brigand
2 CONC age. He gave his son, William I Longsword, governance of the dukedom (927) before his death. Rollo was baptized in 912 but is said to have died a pagan.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagre's Saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "24. ROLF GANGER DRIVEN INTO BANISHMENT.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Ragnvald was King Harald's dearest friend, and the king had the greatest regard for him. He was married to Hild, a daughter of Rolf Nefia, and their sons were Rolf and Thorer. Earl Ragnvald had also three sons by concubines, 
2 CONC -- the one called Hallad, the second Einar, the third Hrollaug; and all three were grown men when their brothers born in marriage were still children Rolf became a great viking, and was of so stout a growth that no horse could car
2 CONC ry him, and wheresoever he went he must go on foot; and therefore he was called Rolf Ganger. He plundered much in the East sea. One summer, as he was coming from the eastward on a viking's expedition to the coast of Viken, he land
2 CONC ed there and made a cattle foray. As King Harald happened, just at that time, to be in Viken, he heard of it, and was in a great rage; for he had forbid, by the greatest punishment, the plundering within the bounds of the country.
2 CONC  The king assembled a Thing, and had Rolf declared an outlaw over all Norway. When Rolf's mother, Hild heard of it she hastened to the king, and entreated peace for Rolf; but the king was so enraged that here entreaty was of no av
2 CONC ail.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT Rolf Ganger went afterwards over sea to the West to the Hebrides, or Sudreys; and at last farther west to Valland, where he plundered and subdued for himself a great earldom, which he peopled with Northmen, from which that land is
2 CONC  called Normandy. Rolf Ganger's son was William, father to Richard, and grandfather to another Richard, who was the father of Robert Longspear, and grandfather of William the Bastard, from whom all the following English kings are 
2 CONC descended. ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT Rolv was married first in 886 with Popa, and second in in 911 with Gisela of France. Rolv lived in Rouen, where he died in 931.
2 CONT
2 CONT Banished by King Harald I of Norway, Rollo sailed off to raid Scotland, England, Flanders, and France on pirating expeditions and, about 911, established himself in an area along the Seine River. Charles III the Simple of France h
2 CONC eld off his siege of Paris, battled him near Chartres, and negotiated the treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, giving him the part of Neustria that came to be called Normandy; Rollo in return agreed to end his brigandage. He gave his s
2 CONC on, William I Longsword, governance of the dukedom (927) before his death. Rollo was baptized in 912 but is said to have died a pagan.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st Duke of Normandy, 911.
