1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ragnvald Heidum Hære (Olavsson) (Rögnvaldur heiðumhæra) ca ?-850, PAM
2 CONT
2 CONT (In English: Rognvald the "Mountain-high", or rather the "highly honoured") Ragnvald (or Rognvald) "the Mountain-High" (alternately "highly honoured") Olafsson was a petty king of Vestfold in what is today Norway. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was expelled to Orkney by his half-brother Hálfdan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family
2 CONT *Father: Óláfr Geirstad (Alf Gudrødsson), King of Vestfold, a cousin of Harald Fairhair 
2 CONT *Wife: Thóra Sigurdsdóttir (daughter of Sigurdr orms-í-auga?), or unknown. No documentation known.
2 CONT *Daughter: Aseda (Asdis, Ascrida) Rögnvaldsdóttir, wife of Eystein Glumra Ivarsson. No documentation known.
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest contribution to posterity was that he asked the skald Þjóðólfr of Hvinir to compose a poem about his ancestry. This poem is known as Ynglingatal and is not only one of the oldest, but also one of the most famous and d
2 CONC ebated of the Old Norse poems.
2 CONT
2 CONT Þjóðólfr ended the poem with these lines: ''
2 CONT     Under the heaven's blue dome, a name
2 CONT
2 CONT     I never knew more true to fame
2 CONT
2 CONT     Than Rognvald bore; whose skilful hand
2 CONT
2 CONT     Could tame the scorners of the land, --
2 CONT
2 CONT     Rognvald, who knew so well to guide
2 CONT
2 CONT     The wild sea-horses through the tide:
2 CONT
2 CONT     The "Mountain-high" was the proud name
2 CONT
2 CONT     By which the king was known to fame.  ''
2 CONT --------------------
