1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===SOURCES
2 CONT [http://www.germanicmythology.com/FORNALDARSAGAS/ThattrRagnarsSonar.html '''''Þáttr af Ragnars Sonum''] (The Tale of Ragnar's Sons) says Sigurd's wife was Blaeja, daughter of Ælla.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Sigurðr ormr í auga átti Blæju, dóttur Ellu konungs."
2 CONT
2 CONT >''"Sigurd Snake-in-Eye married Blæja, the daughter of King Ella."
2 CONT
2 CONT The [https://books.google.com.my/books?id=rLBUAAAAcAAJ&dq=Flateyjarb%C3%B3k+volume+1&pg=PA98&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=helunu&f=false '''''Jómsvíking saga''] (part of ''Ólaf Tryggvasons' saga'', which is part of [https://en.wikipedi
2 CONC a.org/wiki/Flateyjarb%C3%B3k '''''Flateyjarbók'']) says Sigurd's wife was Heluna, daughter of Ælla.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Sigurdr atti Helunu dottur Ella konungs."
2 CONT
2 CONT >''"Sigurd married Heluna, the daughter of King Ella."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===NOTE 
2 CONT With reference to [http://www.geni.com/discussions/149468?msg=1040035&page=1 '''this discussion], it seems the most accurate reconstruction of events would be that Sigurd married a woman (possibly a daughter of king Ælla of Northu
2 CONC mbria) who's name has been lost to history. On marriage, following a Norse tradition, she gained a nickname of "Blæja" or "dark eye" (possibly in reference to her blue, black or grey eye colour) which we find preserved in ''Þáttr 
2 CONC af Ragnars Sonum'' (The Tale of Ragnar's Sons). 
2 CONT Other oral traditions lost this nickname also and so by the time of the recording of the ''Jómsvíking saga'' (part of ''Ólaf Tryggvasons' saga'', which is part of ''Flateyjarbók'') she was assigned the name "Heluna".  Ironically, 
2 CONC Heluna is derived from the Greek Helena which we know from onomastic studies was not present in England until hundreds of years after the time frame this woman lived in.  
2 CONT
