1 CONT Attesté en 1154.
0 @N6000000003220313449@ NOTE Han ble lendmann for kong Magnus Barfot før år 1103. Var med på kongens vesterhavstokt til Ulster i Irland hvor kong Magnus ble drept 24/8 1103. Vidkunn var den siste som rømte fra slaget, og han ble derfor den fremste lendmann fo
1 CONC r kong Magnus sønner; Øystein, Olav og Sigurd (Jorsalfar). Var med Sigurd på dennes Jorsalferd, før 1112 - som hovedanfører. Kong Sigurd sendte sin sønn Magnus (senere kong Magnus Blinde) til fostring hos Vidkunn på Bjarkøy. Vidku
1 CONC nns hustru er ukjent.
0 @N6000000003319698777@ NOTE
1 CONT Also Fornjotur
0 @N6000000003949959908@ NOTE The Northern Finnish "Kainuu" people were called Kven by the Swedes, Norwegians and Icelanders. The Kingdom of Kvenland ruled the North for hundreds of years. Kvenland people lived on the North half of the Gulf of Bothnia, on the 
1 CONC Western, Northern and Eastern shores.
1 CONT These mysterious people ruled the North for millennia. They were a "Kalevala" people like the Karelians. The Finnish National Epic "Kalevala," which was composed of Karelian songs and stories, describes these heroic people of the 
1 CONC North. Although they are related people, they often clashed in their struggle for control over certain areas.
1 CONT The Kainuu people were after the riches of the fur and related trade. They settled at the main rivers such as Kainuunjoki and Tornionjoki in the north, and established trading centers at the mouths of these rivers. These rivers we
1 CONC re at the Eastern end of the Atlantic trading area. Kemijoki area was also in their control, which pointed toward the Arctic Ocean. The tributaries of these rivers offered good access to the land and its riches. In the distant pas
1 CONC t, these were the exclusive domain of the Saami (Lapps) but due to their nomadic habits, they were easily displaced and placed under the taxation of the Kainuu people and their Kings. The Saami were gradually displaced from their 
1 CONC traditional areas including Lake Laatokka (Ladoga), until today they live mainly in Northern Norway, Sweden, Finland and the Kola Peninsula in Russia.
1 CONT Sable skins were highly prized by civilizations all over the continent, in fact all the way Arabia and beyond. Four hundred years ago the animal was still found in Kola, but now only in Siberia. The Arabian Ibn Ruste wrote in 912 
1 CONC that the "Rus" lived by hunting and trapping Sable and Squirrel. He meant the Northern people who were known in the Eastern world as "Rus." 
0 @N6000000005597849676@ NOTE AKA Aegir
0 @N6000000005758036354@ NOTE Kvenland (a.k.a. Cwenland or Quenland) is an ancient name for an area in Fennoscandia, most probably the same as Iron Age Finland. Kvenland is only known from a Norwegian account from the 9th century and from two Icelandic sagas w
1 CONC ritten down probably in the 13th century. As a name for a country, Kvenland seems to have been out of ordinary usage already at the beginning of the second millennium.    Swedish, Russian and Sami sources do not have any informati
1 CONC on about Kvenland. The reason why Finland was called "Kvenland" in the old Norwegian and Icelandic sources is probably just because they called the Sami people "Finns" and needed to distinguish Finns from them. The origin of the n
1 CONC ame "kven" is not clear.    Some historians have speculated Kvenland to be a country of its own in the Northern Finland called Kainuu or Kainuunmaa. This assumption has however not been confirmed with any archaeological findings.
0 @N6000000001956329432@ NOTE
1 CONT King of Kvenlan
