0 @N6000000003796269425@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 4. VINLANDE or VINLANDI- a Swedish king who married Driva, daughter of Snow the Old from Finland, where he spent one winter. Vinlande left Finland in the spring, promising to return within three years. When he had not returned aft
1 CONC er ten years, Driva sent the son she had by Vinlande to his father in Sweden and sought: the help of a witch to either kill Vinlande or force him ro rerurn ro Finland. During a nightmare, Vinlande was trampled to death by "Mara" (
1 CONC the nightmare or female ghost). Their son was:
1 CONT
1 CONT 5. VISBUR
0 @N6000000002187696092@ NOTE Vanlade
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vanlade, Vanlande was a Swedish king at Uppsala of the House of Yngling in Norse mythology. He was the son of Sveigder. Snorri Sturluson wrote in the Ynglinga saga, that Vanlade was a great warrior.
1 CONT
1 CONT Once he stayed for the winter in Finland with Snær the Old, and was married to his daughter Drífa. When he left in spring, he left Drífa behind. Although he had promised her to come back in three years time, she had to wait ten ye
1 CONC ars. Then she sent her and Vanlade's son Visbur to Sweden, and sent a message to the völva Huld. She asked the völva to either bring Vanlade back to Finland or kill him.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the völva worked to bring Vanlade back, he was staying at Uppsala and he felt a great need to travel to Finland. His advisors and friends told him not to go and claimed that it was the witchraft of the Finns that made him want 
1 CONC to go. Having resisted the wish to leave, he became drowsy and went to bed. A little later he screamed that he was hagridden and his men came to help him. However, when they grasped his head the Mara stepped on his legs and when t
1 CONC hey held his legs she trod on his head. This caused him to die. The Swedes burnt his body by the River Skyt (one of the tributaries of the River Fyris) and raised a menhir over him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri quoted some lines from Ynglingatal composed in the 9th century:
1 CONT
1 CONT     En á vit
1 CONT     Vilja bróður
1 CONT     vitta véttr
1 CONT     Vanlanda kom,
1 CONT     þá er trollkund
1 CONT     of troða skyldi
1 CONT     liðs grímhildr
1 CONT     ljóna bága;
1 CONT     ok sá brann á beði Skútu
1 CONT     menglötuðr,
1 CONT     er mara kvalði.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     And Vanlande, in a fatal hour,
1 CONT     Was dragg'd by Grimhild's daughter's power,
1 CONT     The witch-wife's, to the dwelling-place
1 CONT     Where men meet Odin face to face.
1 CONT     Trampled to death, to Skytaa's shore
1 CONT     The corpse his faithful followers bore;
1 CONT     And there they burnt, with heavy hearts,
1 CONT     The good chief killed by witchcraft's arts.(Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sveigder Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Visbur
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Geography
1 CONT
1 CONT Geographical note: the river skutá is today called Björklingeån. According to the encyclopedias Nationalencyklopedin and Nordisk familjebok it passed its name onto the village and the parish of Skuttunge. The area does not only co
1 CONC ntain raised stones, but also 45 grave fields (most from the Iron Age), including a dolmen.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
0 @N6000000003051314961@ NOTE Vanlandi succeeded his father, Sveigthir, at Uppsala, but was killed by a nightmare that an evel witch conjured up (according to legend). [Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev]
0 @N6000000003796269405@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 5. VISBUR - Swedish King who was killed by his sons from his first wife. After Visbur sent away his first wife and two sons in order to take another wife, the first wife sent the two sons back to Visbur to ask him to return their 
1 CONC mother's dowry - three large farms and a gold ornament. Visbur refused to return anything to them. During the night they gathered a group of men and went to Visbur's house where they burned the house with Visbur in it. His son was
1 CONC :
1 CONT
1 CONT 6. DOMALDE 
0 @N6000000002187696074@ NOTE Visbur
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT In Scandinavian mythology, Visbur was a king of the House of Ynglings and the son of Vanlade.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates in Ynglinga saga that Visbur was the son of Vanlade and Drífa, a daughter of Snær the Old. He married a daughter of Aud the Rich, and they had two sons, Gisl and Öndur.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Visbur found a new wife whereupon his old wife took the two boys and returned to her father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Visbur had a third son, Domalde, by the new wife. When Gisl and Öndur were twelve and thirteen years old they went to their father to retrieve their mothers dowry. Visbur, however, refused to consent, and then the two sons said th
1 CONC at the golden necklace would be the bane of the best man of his house. They then returned and planned to murder their father.
1 CONT
1 CONT The völva Huld was hired to help them, but she prophesied that killing kin would lead to having kinslaying as a permanent trait in the House of Yngling (Scylfing). They did not care about this warning, went to their father and bur
1 CONC nt him to death inside his hall.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri included a piece from Ynglingatal in his account in the Heimskringla:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Ok Visburs
1 CONT     vilja byrgi
1 CONT     sævar niðr
1 CONT     svelga knátti,
1 CONT     þá er meinþjóf
1 CONT     markar öttu
1 CONT     setrs verjendr
1 CONT     á sinn föður;
1 CONT     ok allvald
1 CONT     í arinkjóli
1 CONT     glóða garmr
1 CONT     glymjandi beit.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     Have the fire-dogs' fierce tongues yelling
1 CONT     Lapt Visbur's blood on his own hearth?
1 CONT     Have the flames consumed the dwelling
1 CONT     Of the here's soul on earth?
1 CONT     Madly ye acted, who set free
1 CONT     The forest foe, red fire, night thief,
1 CONT     Fell brother of the raging sea,
1 CONT     Against your father and your chief.(Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Vanlade Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Domalde
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
0 @N6000000003051319821@ NOTE Visbur, the son of Vanlandi, was murdered by his own sons who burned him one night in his hall (according to legend). [Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev]
0 @N6000000001421033500@ NOTE [hezboone.FTW]
1 CONT !Per "My Boone Family":  Sveide the Viking, Norse King, 760 A.D.  At this time the title "king" did not denote ruler of a country as we know it today.  It was more nearly comparable to that of clan chief within a designated territ
1 CONC ory. Sveide ruled a portion of what is now Norway and was succeeded by his son.
