0 @N6000000002187690467@ NOTE Harald I of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Fairhair
1 CONT King of Norway
1 CONT Reign 872 – 930
1 CONT Coronation none
1 CONT Born c. 850
1 CONT Eastern Norway
1 CONT Died 933
1 CONT Rogaland, Norway
1 CONT Buried Karmsund
1 CONT Predecessor none
1 CONT Successor Eirik Bloodaxe
1 CONT Wife/wives Ragnhild Eriksdotter
1 CONT Åsa Håkonsdotter
1 CONT Svanhild Øysteinsdotter
1 CONT various concubines
1 CONT Issue 21 known sons
1 CONT 3 known daughters
1 CONT Sons include:
1 CONT Eirik Bloodaxe
1 CONT Bjørn Farmann
1 CONT Olaf Geirstadalf
1 CONT Haakon the Good
1 CONT Dynasty Fairhair dynasty
1 CONT Father Halfdan the Black
1 CONT Mother Ragnhild Sigurdsdotter
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Fairhair or Harald Finehair (Old Norse: Haraldr hárfagri, Norwegian: Harald Hårfagre), (c. 850 – c. 933) was the first king (872 – 930) of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Little is known of the historical Harald. The only contemporary sources mentioning him are the two skaldic poems Haraldskvæði and Glymdrápa, by Þorbjörn Hornklofi. The first poem describes life at Harald's court, mentions that he 
1 CONC took a Danish wife, and that he won a victory at Hafrsfjord. The second relates a series of battles Harald has won. He is not mentioned in any contemporary foreign sources. His life was described in several of the Kings' sagas, bu
1 CONC t the first of these were not written until the end of the 12th century, over 250 years after his death. Their accounts of Harald and his life differ on several points, and much of the content is clearly mythological. He is credit
1 CONC ed with having unified Norway into one kingdom. Modern historians assume that his rule was limited to the coastal areas of southern Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The saga descriptions
1 CONT
1 CONT The sagas tell us that Harald succeeded, on the death of his father Halfdan the Black Gudrødsson in A.D. 860, to the sovereignty of several small, and somewhat scattered kingdoms in Vestfold, which had come into his father's hands
1 CONC  through conquest and inheritance.
1 CONT
1 CONT The unification of Norway is, according to a tale, somewhat of a love story. The tale begins with a marriage proposal that resulted in rejection and scorn from Gyda, the daughter of Eirik king of Hordaland. She said she refused to
1 CONC  marry Harald "before he was king over all of Norway". Harald was therefore induced to take a vow not to cut nor comb his hair until he was sole king of Norway, and that ten years later, he was justified in trimming it; whereupon 
1 CONC he exchanged the epithet "Shockhead" or "Tanglehair" for the one by which he is usually known. Most scholars today regard this story as a literary tale inspired by the Romance stories popular at the courts, by the time Heimskringl
1 CONC a was written.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 866, Harald made the first of a series of conquests over the many petty kingdoms which would compose Norway, including Värmland in Sweden, and modern day south-eastern Norway, which had sworn allegiance to the Swedish king Erik
1 CONC  Eymundsson. In 872, after a great victory at Hafrsfjord near Stavanger, Harald found himself king over the whole country. His realm was, however, threatened by dangers from without, as large numbers of his opponents had taken ref
1 CONC uge, not only in Iceland, then recently discovered; but also in the Orkney Islands, Shetland Islands, Hebrides Islands, Faroe Islands and the northern European mainland. However, his opponents leaving wasn't entirely voluntary. Ma
1 CONC ny Norwegian chieftains who were wealthy and respected posed a threat to Harald; therefore, they were subjected to much harassment from Harald, prompting them to vacate the land. At last, Harald was forced to make an expedition to
1 CONC  the West, to clear the islands and the Scottish mainland of some Vikings who tried to hide there.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was long thought that Harald thus caused the Norse settlement of Iceland and beyond. According to this view, Iceland was settled by "malcontents" from Norway, who resented Harald's claim of rights of taxation over lands, which 
1 CONC the possessors appear to have previously held in absolute ownership. This view has been largely abandoned by modern historians, as archaeological finds have shown that the settlement of Iceland started before Harald's reign in Nor
1 CONC way.
1 CONT Norwegian petty kingdoms ca. 820 AD at the death of Gudrød the Hunter. The most important kingdoms were Vestfold (red), Hålogaland (purple), Alvheim (yellow) and Agder (green).
1 CONT
1 CONT Petty kingdoms ca. 860 AD at the death of Halfdan the Black. In red is the kingdom inherited by Harald Fairhair.
1 CONT
1 CONT Petty kingdoms ca. 872 AD (the unified kingdom shown in red) before the defining Battle of Hafrsfjord.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Harald I's division of the kingdom ca. 930 AD. The yellow areas are petty kingdoms assigned to Harald's sons and kinsmen, the red area remained Harald's territory as High King, the purple is the domain of the earls of Lade, o
1 CONC range is the domain the earls of Møre.
1 CONT The division of the kingdom after the Battle of Svolder (1000) between Sweden (yellow), Denmark (red) and the jarl of Lade (purple).
1 CONT
1 CONT Unified Norway during the reign of Saint Olav ca. 1020 AD. In pale red the Finnmarken ("Marches of the Sami") most of which paid tribute to the kings of Norway.
1 CONT Harald Haarfager later in his life.
1 CONT Harald Haarfager later in his life.
1 CONT
1 CONT The latter part of Harald's reign was disturbed by the strife of his many sons. He gave them all the royal title and assigned lands to them, which they were to govern as his representatives; but this arrangement did not put an end
1 CONC  to the discord, which continued into the next reign. When he grew old, Harald handed over the supreme power to his favourite son Eirik Bloodaxe, whom he intended to be his successor. Eirik I ruled side-by-side with his father whe
1 CONC n Harald was 80 years old. Harald died three years later due to age in approximately 933. The number of sons he left varies in the different saga accounts, from 11 to 20. Twelve of his sons are named as kings, two of them over the
1 CONC  whole country.
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald's children with Åsa, daughter of Håkon Grjotgardsson, Earl of Lade:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Guttorm Haraldsson, king of Ranrike
1 CONT     * Halvdan Kvite (Haraldsson), king of Trondheim
1 CONT     * Halvdan Svarte (Haraldsson), king of Trondheim.
1 CONT     * Sigrød Haraldsson, king of Trondheim
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Gyda:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ålov Årbot (Haraldsdotter)
1 CONT     * Rørek Haraldsson
1 CONT     * Sigtrygg Haraldsson
1 CONT     * Frode Haraldsson
1 CONT     * (Torgils Haraldsson)¹
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Ragnhild Eiriksdotter of Jutland:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Eirik Bloodaxe, king of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Svanhild, daughter of Eystein Earl:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bjørn Farmann, king of Vestfold.
1 CONT     * Olaf Haraldsson Geirstadalf, king of Vingulmark, later also Vestfold.
1 CONT     * Ragnar Rykkel
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Åshild, daughter of Ring Dagsson:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ring Haraldsson
1 CONT     * Dag Haraldsson
1 CONT     * Gudrød Skirja
1 CONT     * Ingegjerd Haraldsdotter
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Snøfrid, daughter of Svåse the Finn:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Halvdan Hålegg
1 CONT     * Gudrød Ljome
1 CONT     * Ragnvald Rettilbeine
1 CONT     * Sigurd Rise
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Tora Mosterstong, his maid:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Haakon the Good, king of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Other children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ingebjørg Haraldsdotter
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald was the grandfather of Harald II.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. This Torgils Haraldsson is identical to "Thorgest" in the Irish history. Snorre Sturlason, in his Heimskringla, claims that Torgils was Harald's son. This is now thought to be unlikely.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
