0 @N6000000000944948551@ NOTE Konge. Død 927. 
1 CONT Bjørns hovedsete var Sem i Vestfold. Tilnavnet Farmann eller kjøpmann fikk han fordi han drev det stort i handel - han fikk varer nordfra, og kjøpte og solgte både i Danmark og lenger sør. Så sier Snorre, og det stemmer godt med d
1 CONC et som var rimelig for en Vestfold-konge å ta seg til. Det kan da være at det nettopp var han som flyttet kaupangen fra Skiringssal til Tønsberg, nærmere sin egen kongsgård i Sem. Kaupang forsvinner iallfall fra historien omkring 
1 CONC denne tiden.
1 CONT Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagres saga:
1 CONT ?36. Bjørn, kong Haralds sønn, rådde da i Vestfold, han satt for det meste i Tønsberg og var lite på hærferd. Til Tønsberg kom det mange kjøpmannsskip både der fra Viken og lenger nordfra i landet, og sør fra Danmark og fra Saksla
1 CONC nd. Kong Bjørn hadde også kjøpskip ute i fart på andre land og skaffet seg på den måten kostbare saker og andre varer som han syntes han trengte. Brødrene hans kalte ham farmann eller kjøpmann. Bjørn var en klok mann, rolig og sin
1 CONC dig, og så ut til å bli en god høvding, han fikk seg et godt og høvelig gifte, og han fikk en sønn som het Gudrød.?
1 CONT En dag kom hans halvbror Eirik reisende fra Østersjøen, han hadde mye folk og trengte proviant og telt. Han krevde skatten på sin fars vegne - underkongene delte landsinntektene halvt med Harald. Men Bjørn ville ikke gjøre regnska
1 CONC p for noen annen enn Harald selv. Det endte med at Eirik gikk til angrep. Om kvelden, da Bjørn og hans menn satt ved drikken, omringet de huset. Bjørn falt der.
1 CONT Bjørn ligger i Farmannshaugen ved Tønsberg.
0 @N6000000002187690448@ NOTE Your continued donations keep Wikipedia running!    
1 CONT Bjørn Farmann
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT To meet Wikipedia's quality standards, this article or section may require cleanup.
1 CONT Please discuss this issue on the talk page, or replace this tag with a more specific message. Editing help is available.
1 CONT This article has been tagged since October 2005.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Harald Harfager's Saga:
1 CONT
1 CONT King Harald's son, Bjorn, who went by the name Bjorn Farmann ruled over Vestfold at that time, and generally lived at Tunsberg, and went but little on war expeditions. Tunsberg at that time was much frequented by merchant vessels,
1 CONC  both from Viken and the north country, and also from the south, from Denmark, and Saxland. King Bjorn had also merchant ships on voyages to other lands, by which he procured for himself costly articles, and such things as he thou
1 CONC ght needful; and therefore his brothers called him Farman (the Seaman), and Kaupman (the Chapman). Bjorn was a man of sense and understanding, and promised to become a good ruler. He made a good and suitable marriage, and had a so
1 CONC n by his wife, who was named Gudrod. Eirik Blood-axe came from his Baltic cruise with ships of war, and a great force, and required his brother Bjorn to deliver to him King Harald's share of the scat and incomes of Vestfold. But i
1 CONC t had always been the custom before, that Bjorn himself either delivered the money into the king's hands, or sent men of his own with it; and therefore he would continue with the old custom, and would not deliver the money. Eirik 
1 CONC again wanted provisions, tents, and liquor. The brothers quarrelled about this; but Eirik got nothing and left the town. Bjorn went also out of the town towards evening up to Saeheim. In the night Eirik came back after Bjorn, and 
1 CONC came to Saeheim just as Bjorn and his men were seated at table drinking. Eirik surrounded the house in which they were; but Bjorn with his men went out and fought. Bjorn, and many men with him, fell. Eirik, on the other hand, got 
1 CONC a great booty, and proceeded northwards. But this work was taken very ill by the people of Viken, and Eirik was much disliked for it; and the report went that King Olaf would avenge his brother Bjorn, whenever opportunity offered.
1 CONC  King Bjorn lies in the mound of Farmanshaug at Saeheim.
0 @N6000000003232683340@ NOTE Described As A Wise And Quiet Man
1 CONT
1 CONT Alias:<ALIA> Bjrn/Ursus the /Merchant/, Byorn Farmann
0 @N6000000003232721887@ NOTE Described As A Wise And Quiet Man
1 CONT
1 CONT Alias:<ALIA> Bjrn/Ursus the /Merchant/, Byorn Farmann
0 @N6000000003949860498@ NOTE Under-king in Vesfold, owned merchant ships. [Anceatral Roots]
1 CONT He ruled over Westfold, and usually resided in Tnsberg where many merchant ships came and went. He had merchantmen who sailed to other lands and brought things back for him. The medieval historian, Snorri Sturluson describes him a
1 CONC s a shrewd man with a calm disposition who engaged in little warfare. He is said to have had a good marriage.
1 CONT Bjrn's half-brother, Eric ("Bloodaxe"), while on the road and in need of provisions, came to Bjrn, demanding to be given the taxes due their father, saying he would deliver the taxes to their father for Bjrn who usually took care 
1 CONC of that himself. Bjrn refused to give him the revenue. The two fought, and Bjrn was killed. The name of his wife is unknown.
0 @N6000000003051144019@ NOTE [Weis 206] owned merchant ships
0 @N6000000000945044962@ NOTE Bjørns hovedsete var Sem i Vestfold. Tilnavnet Farmann eller kjøpmann fikk han fordi 
