0 @N6000000000945044982@ NOTE Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagres saga. 
1 CONT ?38. ... Da kong Harald var nesten 70 år gammel, fikk han en sønn med en kvinne som
1 CONT het Tora og ble kalt Mosterstang, hennes ætt var fra Moster, og hun hadde gode frender, hun
1 CONT var skyld til Horda-Kåre. Hun var en staut kvinne og svært vakker; hun ble regnet som
1 CONT kongens tjenestejente. Det var mange den gang som gjorde tjeneste hos kongen, enda de var
1 CONT av god ætt, både menn og kvinner. Når det gjaldt storfolks barn, var det skikk å være svært
1 CONT nøye med hvem som skulle øse vann over dem og gi dem navn. Da det nå led mot den tid
1 CONT Tora ventet hun skulle føde barnet, ville hun reise til kong Harald, han var da nord på Seim, og
1 CONT hun var på Moster. Hun seilte så nordover på Sigurd jarls skip. De lå ved land om natten, og
1 CONT der fødte Tora barnet oppe på hella ved langangen; det var en gutt. Sigurd jarl øste vann over
1 CONT gutten og kalte ham opp etter sin egen far Håkon Ladejarl; gutten ble snart vakker og stor av
1 CONT vekst og lignet svært på sin far. Harald lot gutten følge moren, og de to var på kongsgårdene
1 CONT mens gutten var liten.
0 @N6000000002187914770@ NOTE Haakon I of Norway
1 CONT
1 CONT Haakon I (ca. 920–961) (Old Norse: Hákon Aðalsteinsfóstri, Norwegian: Håkon Adalsteinsfostre), surnamed the Good, was the third king of Norway and the youngest son of Harald Fairhair.
1 CONT
1 CONT Haakon was fostered by King Athelstan of England, as part of a peace agreement made by his father. The English king brought him up in the Christian religion, and on the news of his father’s death provided him with ships and men fo
1 CONC r an expedition against his half-brother Eirik Bloodaxe, who had been proclaimed king. On his arrival in Norway, Haakon gained the support of the landowners by promising to give up the rights of taxation claimed by his father over
1 CONC  inherited real property.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eirik fled and thereafter concentrated his efforts in the British Isles, eventually meeting a violent end there. His sons allied themselves with the Danes, but were invariably defeated by Haakon, who was successful in everything h
1 CONC e undertook except in his attempt to introduce Christianity, which aroused an opposition he did not feel strong enough to face. He was mortally wounded at the Battle of Fitjar in 961, after a final victory over Eirik’s sons. So en
1 CONC tirely did even his immediate circle ignore his religion that Eyvindr Skáldaspillir, his court poet composed a poem, Hákonarmál, on his death representing his welcome by the heathen gods into Valhalla.
1 CONT
1 CONT The succession issue was settled as Harald II, third son of Eirik, ascended the throne. However the Norwegians were severely tormented by years of war and welcomed the Danish invading force led by Harold Bluetooth.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Eirik Bloodaxe King of Norway
1 CONT 934–961 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Harald Greyhide
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
0 @N6000000003232674698@ NOTE Raised In England
1 CONT Described As A Wise And Good-Humored Man
1 CONT His Sword Was Named Kvernbit (Grinding-Stone Cutter) Said To Be The Best Weapon In Norway Said To Have Been Born When His Father Was About 70 Years Old
1 CONT Youngest Of Konungr Harald Fairhairs' Sons
1 CONT
1 CONT Alias:<ALIA> Hákon /Adalsteinsfostre/, Hákon the Good
1 CONT According to Tom Bjørnstad, Norwegian genealogist
1 CONT (http://home.ringnett.no/bjornstad):
1 CONT "Hákon Adalsteinsfostre (a.k.a. Hákon the Good), who got his nickname because he was brought up as a foster son by Cyning Æþelstan of England. Konungr Hákon was the first Christian king of Norway, but he failed in christening the 
1 CONC country."
