0 @N6000000005597610902@ NOTE !Name is;  Rognvald I, "The Wise" Jarl Of /MORE/
0 @N6000000005758036262@ NOTE Ragnvald "the Wise" Eysteinsson (830?890) (Old Norse: Rgnvaldr M?rajarl Norwegian: Ragnvald Mrejarl), was jarl (earl) in the northwest coast of Norway, called More, approximately of the Norwegian county today known as Mre og Romsd
1 CONC al. He died at the Orkney Islands.    He was son of King Eystein "Glumra (the Noisy)" Ivarsson of Oppland. His maternal grandfather was King Ragnvald the Mountain-High of Vestfold. One of his paternal great-grandfathers was King H
1 CONC alfdan the Old of Oppland. Mythical material (saga) postulate that his line was a male-line descent from Ancient kings of Finland.    His second wife was Ragnhild Hrolfsdottir (Raghildr (Hildr) Hrolfsdttir), daughter of Hrolf Nefi
1 CONC a (Hrolfr Nefja). Ragnvald was the father of Hrolf Ganger, who according to Norse-Icelandic tradition was the founder of Normandy, (from his marriage), and Turf-Einar, ancestor of the jarls of Orkney (from a concubine).    If Hrol
1 CONC f Ganger was indeed the founder of Normandy, that would mean that Ragnvald`s male-line descen
0 @N6000000003232730277@ NOTE [Sargent.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [JamesLinage.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT [feonadorf.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT A Norwegian Viking. He supported King Harold Fairhair in an attempt to unify Norge.[Direct Linage1.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [JamesLinage.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT [feonadorf.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT [charlemegne.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ragnvald I the wise of More, aka Rognvald Mere-Earl. acceded about 872
1 CONT
1 CONT Ragnvald I, "the Wise", Jarl of North and South More, and of Ramsdal in Norway, see 867, d. 890; m. Hiltrude (or Raginhilde), dau. Hrolf Nefia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT [charlemegne.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Glumra Eystein the Noisy, Jarl of the Upplanders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eystein Glumra, Jarl of the Upplands,  Ca. 830, said to have fathered two known children, Swanhild, wife of Harald, King of Norge; and Ragnvald I, "the Wise".
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT bk&q chart 27 on p 439 "Danish and Norse Kingdoms--Earls of  Orkney" and pp 385, 440, 441-2, 498
1 CONT
1 CONT p 498 "Willelm (le Bastard) was descended from RAGNALD, the ancestor of the earls of Orkney."
1 CONT
1 CONT p 385 (under Olaf the White) ". . . The historical record is vague about Olaf's fate. One record suggests that he became involved with RAGNALD in the battle to clear the Orkneys of Norse pirates and was killed at the battle of Haf
1 CONC rsfiord in 872. . . . He (Olaf) was hailed as the greatest ruler of the western seas."
1 CONT
1 CONT p 440 [NB1] The jarl or earl of More on the west coast of Norge, who fought alongside the future king Harald Finehair in establishing a united Norge. Harald married Ragnald's sister Swanhilda. During this civil war many Norwegians
1 CONC  were driven out of Norge and settled in the Shetlands and Orkney, called the Nordreys by the Norse (as distinct from the Sudreys, or Hebrides and Man), which they used as a base to attack Norway and Norse ships. The traditional v
1 CONC iew is that around 874 Harald sent Ragnald to the Hebrides to bring these islands under his authority. During the expedition Ragnald's son, Ivar, was killed. As a consequence, once Ragnald had conquered the islands, Harald granted
1 CONC  them back to him in recompense for his son. The more recent view is that Ragnald may have set out earlier on the expedition to conquer the Nordrey pirates himself and worked in league with Olaf the White, who was the Norse king o
1 CONC f Dublin. According to this view Olaf granted Ragnald the Orkneys around the year 871 or earlier. However, Ragnald had no desire to remain in the isles so in 875 (or earlier) Ragnald gave the islands to his brother Sigurd, and ret
1 CONC urned to Norge where he continued to fight on behalf of the king. This created enemies and around the year 894 he was burned alive in his hall by two of Harald's sons by another wife. Ragnald had three children by his wife Ragnhil
1 CONC d. In addition to Ivar, who died, there was Rolf, who became the first Duke of Normandy, and Þori, who succeeded him as Jarl of More. He also had several illegitimate sons, two of whom (Hallad and Einar I) became later earls of Or
1 CONC kney.
0 @N6000000006422192622@ NOTE !Name is;  Rognvald I, "The Wise" Jarl Of /MORE/
