1 CONT
1 CONT  Rollo the Viking (855-931) brought his  Normans ( northmen, vikings)
1 CONT from                        Norway to the northwest of France. This is
1 CONT the Rollo, or Rollon in the French
1 CONT spelling, who in 911 at the church in St.  Clair sur Epte signed the
1 CONT treaty with King
1 CONT  Louis the Simple of France that created him hereditary Duke of the new
1 CONT Duchy of Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Some say that Rollo was the same as Ganger-Hrolf, or Rollo the
1 CONT Ganger, who was called that because he was so gangly, to be  specific,
1 CONT his legs were so long that when he rode a horse it  looked like he was
1 CONT walking; Ganger means Walker. However,   according to Peter
1 CONT Kurrild-Klitgaard, Ph.D., the early sources all         distinguish the
1 CONT two men.
0 @N6000000002319320892@ NOTE See message under Richard I, Duke of Normandy. Rollo was the 1st Duke of
1 CONT Normandy and an ancestor of William I of England, aka, William II of
1 CONT Normandy. See #301.
0 @N6000000002187576746@ NOTE Rollo of Normandy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo (c.860 - c.932) was the Frankish-Latin name taken by (probably) Hrolf Ganger (Hrolf the Walker, this was because he was so big no horse could carry him, Old Norse: Hrólfr Rögnvaldsson and Göngu-Hrólfr, Norwegian: Gange-Rolf)
1 CONC , Swedish: Gångerolf). He has also been called "Rollo the Gangler" in some works, or occasionally "Robert".
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo was a Viking leader, probably (based on Icelandic sources) from Norway, the son of Ragnvald, Earl of Moer; sagas mention a Hrolf, son of Ragnvald jarl of Moer. However, the latinization Rollo has in no known instance been ap
1 CONC plied to a Hrolf, and in the texts which speak of him, numerous latinized Hrolfs are included. Dudo of St. Quentin (by most accounts a more reliable source, and at least more recent and living nearer the regions concerned), in his
1 CONC  Gesta Normannorum Ducum, tells of a powerful Danish nobleman at loggerheads with the king of Denmark, who then died and left his two sons, Gurim and Rollo, leaving Rollo to be expelled and Gurim killed.(1)
1 CONT
1 CONT With his followers (known as Normans, or northmen), Rollo invaded the area of northern France now known as Normandy. Wace, writing some 300 years after the event, gives a Scandinavian origin, as does the Orkneyinga Saga, with Dani
1 CONC sh or Norwegian being most likely.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unlike most Vikings whose intentions were to plunder Frankish lands, Rollo's true intentions were to look for lands to settle. Upon arrival in France, and after many battles with the Vikings, the French King Charles the Simple und
1 CONC erstood that he could no longer hold back their advances, and decided as a tempory measure to give Rollo land around Rouen under the condition that he would convert to Christianity and defend the Seine River from other raiding Vik
1 CONC ings.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte (911) with King Charles, Rollo pledged feudal allegiance to the king, changed his name to the Frankish version, and converted to Christianity, probably with the baptismal name Robert. In retur
1 CONC n, and in admission of defeat, King Charles granted Rollo the lower Seine area (today's upper Normandy) and the titular rulership of Normandy, centred around the city of Rouen. There exists some argument among historians as to whe
1 CONC ther Rollo was a "duke" (dux) or whether his position was equivalent to that of a "count" under Charlemagne. According to legend, when required, in conformity with general usage, to kiss the foot of King Charles, he refused to sto
1 CONC op to what he considered so great a degradation; yet as the homage could not be dispensed with, he ordered one of his warriors to perform it for him. The latter, as proud as his chief, instead of stooping to the royal foot, raised
1 CONC  it so high, that the King fell to the ground.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is important to note that Rollo stayed true to his word of defending the shores of the Seine river in accordance to the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, but in time he and his followers had very different ideas. Rollo began to d
1 CONC ivide the land between the Epte and Risle rivers among his chieftains and settled there with a de facto capital in Rouen. With these settlements, Rollo began to further raid other Frankish lands, now from the security of a settled
1 CONC  homeland, rather than a mobile fleet. Eventually, however, Rollo's men intermarried with the local women, and became more settled as Frenchmen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo expanded his territory as far west as the Vire River and sometime around 927 he passed the Duchy of Normandy to his son, William Longsword. Rollo may have lived for a few years after that, but certainly died before 933. Acco
1 CONC rding to the historian Adhemar, 'As Rollo's death drew near, he went mad and had a hundred Christian prisoners beheaded in front of him in honour of the gods whom he had worshiped, and in the end distributed a hundred pounds of go
1 CONC ld around the churches in honour of the true god in whose name he had accepted baptism.' Even though Rollo had converted to Christianity, some of his pagan roots surfaced at the end.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is a direct ancestor of William the Conqueror. Through William, he is a direct ancestor of the present-day British royal family, including Elizabeth II of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT The "clameur de haro" on the Channel Islands is, supposedly, an appeal to Rollo.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Ålesund, Viking Age, Dukes of Normandy
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT — Duke of Normandy Succeeded by:
1 CONT William I 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT 1: It should be noted that for Franks, Scandinavians were called Danes irrespective of their precise origin in Scandinavia. This happened for instance in the case of Hygelac, a king of the Geats, who is called Danish, something th
1 CONC at has caused many a Danish scholar to state that he is the first historical "king of Denmark". That Dane was confused with Dacia does not make the sources less confusing, and likewise the Geat Hygelac was not only called Danish b
1 CONC ut also Gothic and Getic depending on the manuscript.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References and external links
1 CONT D.C. Douglas, "Rollo of Normandy", English Historical Review, Vol. 57 (1942), pp. 414-436 
1 CONT Robert Helmerichs, [Rollo as Historical Figure] 
1 CONT Rosamond McKitterick, The Frankish Kingdom under the Carolingians, 751-987, (Longman) 1983 
1 CONT Dudonis gesta Normannorum - Dudo of St. Quentin Gesta Normannorum latin version at Bibliotheca Augustana 
1 CONT Dudo of St. Quentin's Gesta Normannorum - An English Translation 
1 CONT Gwyn Jones. Second edition: A History of the Vikings. Oxford University Press. (1984). 
1 CONT William W. Fitzhugh and Elizabeth Ward. Vikings: The North Atlantic Saga. Smithsonian Institute Press. (2000) 
1 CONT Eric Christiansen. The Norsemen in the Viking Age. Blackwell Publishers Ltd. (2002) 
1 CONT Agnus Konstam. Historical Atlas of the Viking World. Checkmark Books. (2002) 
1 CONT Holgar Arbman. Ancient People and Places: The Vikings. Thames and Husdson. (1961) 
1 CONT Eric Oxenstierna. The Norsemen, New York Graphics Society Publishers, Ltd. (1965) 
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Duke of Normandy 911-932. Rollo was sometimes referred to as Rolf. According to some sources, Rollo was born as early as 846. A Norwegian chieftain, Rollo became the *first Norman* when he and his followers settled in the lower Se
1 CONC ine area in about 911. At that time, he received the Duchy of Normandy from Charles III of France. Rollo was known as the Walker to his fellow Vikings because he was so stout he could not find a horse to carry him, although accord
1 CONC ing to an alternate theory it was because his legs were so long that they touched the ground while Rollo was astride. Source: RoyaList. The Viking chieftain Rollo, a.k.a. Gangr Hrolfr or Rolf the Ganger and probably the son of Rog
1 CONC nvald, Earl of Møre in Norway, controlled the mouth of river Seine in France. Charles the Simple, having exacted a vow of loyalty, bestowed on Rollo the title "Count of Rouen" and gave him the surrounding lands in 911 A.D. This br
1 CONC ought the raids of the Vikings to an end and lead to the establishment of the Duchy of Normandy. From this the Vikings turned to a "pay, and we shall keep the peace" policy it seems. Adding up all figures cited in the Frankish sou
1 CONC rces, we find that they paid the Vikings 310 kilograms of gold, and 19.5 tons of silver. (From *The Viking Age* by Christine & Tom Bjørnstad). Source: Leo van de Pas 
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.worldroots.com/cgi-bin/gasteldb?@I04692@
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0 @N6000000002322269636@ NOTE http://www.mids.org/sinclair/rollo.html
1 CONT
1 CONT http://members.aol.com/rolloclan/index1.html
1 CONT Rollo (or Rolf, or Hrolf, or Rou) (860?-930?), Norse conqueror of what
1 CONT became the French province of Normandy (911).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT
1 CONT  Rollo the Viking (855-931) brought his  Normans ( northmen, vikings)
1 CONT from                        Norway to the northwest of France. This is
1 CONT the Rollo, or Rollon in the French
1 CONT spelling, who in 911 at the church in St.  Clair sur Epte signed the
1 CONT treaty with King
1 CONT  Louis the Simple of France that created him hereditary Duke of the new
1 CONT Duchy of Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Some say that Rollo was the same as Ganger-Hrolf, or Rollo the
1 CONT Ganger, who was called that because he was so gangly, to be  specific,
1 CONT his legs were so long that when he rode a horse it  looked like he was
1 CONT walking; Ganger means Walker. However,   according to Peter
1 CONT Kurrild-Klitgaard, Ph.D., the early sources all         distinguish the
1 CONT two men.
0 @N6000000001744891382@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [Brøderbund WFT Vol. 2, Ed. 1, Tree #1241, Date of Import: May 8, 1997]
1 CONT
1 CONT !DUKE OF NORMANDY
1 CONT Rolf or Rollo was the first Norseman to settle or conquer that part of Northern France called Normandy. From Rolf are descended the Jarls (Earls) of Normandy (including William the Conqueror) and the Kings of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Rolf was a great Viking; he was grown so big that no steed could bear him and therefore he walked everywhere; he was called Rolf the Ganger. He harried much in the eastern countries. One summer when he had come to the Vik from a 
1 CONC Viking raid in the east he made a shore raid. King Harald was then in the Vik, and when he learned of this he became very wroth, for he had strongly forbidden robbery in the land. At the thing, therefor, King Harald declared Rolf 
1 CONC to be an outlaw in Norway. But when Rolf's mother heard that, she went to the king and asked frith for Rolf. But the king was so wroth that her asking availed her naught. Then quoth Hild:
1 CONT
1 CONT The name of Nevja is torn;
1 CONT Now driven in flight from the land
1 CONT Is the warrior's bold kinsman.
1 CONT Why be so hard, my lord?
1 CONT
1 CONT Evil it is by such a wolf,
1 CONT Noble prince, to be bitten;
1 CONT He will not spare the flock
1 CONT If he is driven to the woods.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rolf the Ganger afterwards crossed the sea to the Hebrides and from there went south-west to France; he harried there and possessed himself of a great jarldom [usually assumed to be in about 876], and it was afterwards called Norm
1 CONC andy. From Rolf are descended the jarls in Normandy. ...from whom are descended all the later kings of England. Queen Ragnhild the Mighty lived three years after she came to Norway...." [Rolf is believed to have died about 931, af
1 CONC ter abdicating to his son William Longsword in 928, and is buried in Notre Dame Cathedral in Rouen, and at least part of his body was moved to Fecamp about 1060. He is called Rollo or Rollon by the French].
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Snorre Sturlason (1179-1241), "Heimskringla or the Lives of the Norse Kings" (NY: Dover, 1932, 1990). Also, genealogical source is Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom database, ver 4.17.
1 CONT
1 CONT Norsk Diografisk Leksikon, Norw. 10, v.4, p.351-353; Plantagenet Ancestry, England 116, p.6; Dukes of Normandy, France 5, p.17-24, 31-45; Gen. Tab. Souv., France 22, Tab. 48; Keiser und Koenig Hist., Gen. Hist. 25, pt 1, p. 100-10
1 CONC 1; Espolin (GS #12462, pt 1, p. 97); !The Noble Lineage of the Delaware West Family by Anne Fox 1958; Name varient: Hrolf, he was baptized by the Bishop of France and was given the name of Robert. From http://www8.informatik.uni-e
1 CONC rlangen.de/html/chl-enter.html.[Brøderbund WFT Vol. 2, Ed. 1, Tree #1241, Date of Import: May 8, 1997]
1 CONT
1 CONT !DUKE OF NORMANDY
1 CONT Rolf or Rollo was the first Norseman to settle or conquer that part of Northern France called Normandy. From Rolf are descended the Jarls (Earls) of Normandy (including William the Conqueror) and the Kings of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Rolf was a great Viking; he was grown so big that no steed could bear him and therefore he walked everywhere; he was called Rolf the Ganger. He harried much in the eastern countries. One summer when he had come to the Vik from a 
1 CONC Viking raid in the east he made a shore raid. King Harald was then in the Vik, and when he learned of this he became very wroth, for he had strongly forbidden robbery in the land. At the thing, therefor, King Harald declared Rolf 
1 CONC to be an outlaw in Norway. But when Rolf's mother heard that, she went to the king and asked frith for Rolf. But the king was so wroth that her asking availed her naught. Then quoth Hild:
1 CONT
1 CONT The name of Nevja is torn;
1 CONT Now driven in flight from the land
1 CONT Is the warrior's bold kinsman.
1 CONT Why be so hard, my lord?
1 CONT
1 CONT Evil it is by such a wolf,
1 CONT Noble prince, to be bitten;
1 CONT He will not spare the flock
1 CONT If he is driven to the woods.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rolf the Ganger afterwards crossed the sea to the Hebrides and from there went south-west to France; he harried there and possessed himself of a great jarldom [usually assumed to be in about 876], and it was afterwards called Norm
1 CONC andy. From Rolf are descended the jarls in Normandy. ...from whom are descended all the later kings of England. Queen Ragnhild the Mighty lived three years after she came to Norway...." [Rolf is believed to have died about 931, af
1 CONC ter abdicating to his son William Longsword in 928, and is buried in Notre Dame Cathedral in Rouen, and at least part of his body was moved to Fecamp about 1060. He is called Rollo or Rollon by the French].
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Snorre Sturlason (1179-1241), "Heimskringla or the Lives of the Norse Kings" (NY: Dover, 1932, 1990). Also, genealogical source is Church of JC of the LDS "Ancestral File" CD-Rom database, ver 4.17.
1 CONT
1 CONT Norsk Diografisk Leksikon, Norw. 10, v.4, p.351-353; Plantagenet Ancestry, England 116, p.6; Dukes of Normandy, France 5, p.17-24, 31-45; Gen. Tab. Souv., France 22, Tab. 48; Keiser und Koenig Hist., Gen. Hist. 25, pt 1, p. 100-10
1 CONC 1; Espolin (GS #12462, pt 1, p. 97); !The Noble Lineage of the Delaware West Family by Anne Fox 1958; Name varient: Hrolf, he was baptized by the Bishop of France and was given the name of Robert. From http://www8.informatik.uni-e
1 CONC rlangen.de/html/chl-enter.html.
0 @N6000000001783547165@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo, occasionally known as Rollo the Viking, (c. 860 - c. 932) was the founder and first ruler of the Viking principality in what soon became known as Normandy. He is also in some sources known as Robert of Normandy, using his b
1 CONC aptismal name.  The name Rollo is a Frankish-Latin name probably taken from Scandinavian name Hrólf.
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rollo,_Count_(or_Duke)_of_Normandy
1 CONT
1 CONT The founder of the line, Rollo or Rolf the Ganger, was a Viking raider chief, who was born in 850, at Maer, Nord-Trondelag, Norway, the son of Ragnvald 'the Wise' Eysteinson and his second wife, Hiltrude (Ragnhild) Hrolfsdottir.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rolf was granted the region of Normandy by Charles the Simple, King of France, in 911, at the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, in exchange for feudal alliegiance and conversion to Christianity at which he took the baptismal name of
1 CONC  Robert. He was baptized in a fountain fed by a spring named in honor of Saint Clair.
1 CONT <http://www.englishmonarchs.co.uk/dukes_of_normandy.htm>
1 CONT
1 CONT Origin
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo was a Viking leader of contested origin. Dudo of St. Quentin, in his De moribus et actis primorum Normannorum ducum (Latin), tells of a powerful Danish nobleman at loggerheads with the king of Denmark, who then died and left
1 CONC  his two sons, Gurim and Rollo, leaving Rollo to be expelled and Gurim killed. William of Jumièges also mentions Rollo's prehistory in his Gesta Normannorum Ducum however he states that he was from the Danish town of Fakse. Wace, 
1 CONC writing some 300 years after the event in his Roman de Rou, also mentions the two brothers (as Rou and Garin), as does the Orkneyinga Saga.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Norwegian and Icelandic historians identified this Rollo with a son of Rognvald Eysteinsson, Earl of Møre, in Western Norway, based on medieval Norwegian and Icelandic sagas that mention a Ganger Hrolf (Hrolf, the Walker)
1 CONC . The oldest source of this version is the Latin Historia Norvegiae, written in Norway at the end of the 12th century. This Hrolf fell foul of the Norwegian king Harald Fairhair, and became a Jarl in Normandy. The nickname of that
1 CONC  character came from being so big that no horse (or at least not the Norwegian ponies of that era) could carry him.
1 CONT
1 CONT The question of Rollo's Danish or Norwegian origins was a matter of heated dispute between Norwegian and Danish historians of the 19th and early 20th century, particularly in the run-up to Normandy's 1000-year-anniversary in 1911.
1 CONC  Today, historians still disagree on this question, but most would now agree that a certain conclusion can never be reached.
1 CONT
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rollo,_Count_(or_Duke)_of_Normandy
1 CONT
1 CONT Invasion of France
1 CONT
1 CONT Statue of Rollo in RouenIn 885, Rollo was one of the lesser leaders of the Viking fleet which besieged Paris under Sigfred. Legend has it that an emissary was sent by the king to find the chieftain and negotiate terms. When he ask
1 CONC ed for this information, the Vikings replied that they were all chieftains in their own right. In 886, when Sigfred retreated in return for tribute, Rollo stayed behind and was eventually bought off and sent to harry Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later, he returned to the Seine with his followers (known as Danes, or Norsemen). He invaded the area of northern France now known as Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 911 Rollo's forces were defeated at the Battle of Chartres by the troops of King Charles the Simple. In the aftermath of the battle, rather than pay Rollo to leave, as was customary, Charles the Simple understood that he could 
1 CONC no longer hold back their onslaught, and decided to give Rollo the coastal lands they occupied under the condition that he defend against other raiding Vikings. In the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte (911) with King Charles, Rollo 
1 CONC pledged feudal allegiance to the king, changed his name to the Frankish version, and converted to Christianity, probably with the baptismal name Robert. In return, King Charles granted Rollo the lower Seine area (today's upper Nor
1 CONC mandy) and the titular rulership of Normandy, centred around the city of Rouen. There exists some argument among historians as to whether Rollo was a "duke" (dux) or whether his position was equivalent to that of a "count" under C
1 CONC harlemagne. According to legend, when required to kiss the foot of King Charles, as a condition of the treaty, he refused to perform so great a humiliation, and when Charles extended his foot to Rollo, Rollo ordered one of his war
1 CONC riors to do so in his place. His warrior then lifted Charles' foot up to his mouth causing him to fall to the ground.
1 CONT
1 CONT Settlement
1 CONT
1 CONT Initially, Rollo stayed true to his word of defending the shores of the Seine river in accordance to the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, but in time he and his followers had very different ideas. Rollo began to divide the land bet
1 CONC ween the Epte and Risle rivers among his chieftains and settled there with a de facto capital in Rouen. With these settlements, Rollo began to further raid other Frankish lands, now from the security of a settled homeland, rather 
1 CONC than a mobile fleet. Eventually, however, Rollo's men intermarried with the local women, and became more settled as Frenchmen. At the time of his death, Rollo's expansion of his territory had extended as far west as the Vire River
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Rolf took as his second wife Popee, the daughter of the Berengar of Rennes, the previous Lord of Normandy, the marriage produced six children:-
1 CONT William I 'Longsword' ( 901-0942)
1 CONT Robert (c. 903-)
1 CONT Crespina (c. 905-)married Grimaldus Prince of Monaco, from this issue came the Grimaldi line of Monaco.
1 CONT Gerlatta (c.907-) married William II of Aquitaine.
1 CONT Kathlin (c.909-) wife of Bjolan, a Scottish King
1 CONT Adela (Gerloc) (c.911-962)
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo then repudiated Poppa and married in 912 Gisela (d.919) daughter of Charles III of France and had:
1 CONT Griselle wife of Thorbard av Møre (Which was changed to Herbert de la Mare, when he became the first Lord of St. Opportune-la-Mare. Opportune-the-Mare).
1 CONT
1 CONT He then re-married his first wife again after Gisela's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://www.englishmonarchs.co.uk/dukes_of_normandy.htm>,  <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rollo>
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the historian Adhemar, 'As Rollo's death drew near, he went mad and had a hundred Christian prisoners beheaded in front of him in honour of the gods whom he had worshipped, and in the end distributed a hundred pounds 
1 CONC of gold around the churches in honour of the true God in whose name he had accepted baptism.' Even though Rollo had converted to Christianity, some of his pagan roots surfaced at the end.
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rollo,_Count_(or_Duke)_of_Normandy
1 CONT
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Rollo is a direct ancestor of William the Conqueror. Through William, he is a direct ancestor and predecessor of the present-day British royal family.
1 CONT
1 CONT The "Clameur de Haro" in the Channel Islands is, supposedly, an appeal to Rollo.
1 CONT
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rollo,_Count_(or_Duke)_of_Normandy
